\id JHN - Restored Context Bible (RCB)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h John
\toc1 The Good News According to John
\toc2 John
\toc3 Jhn
\mt1 John

\imt Introduction
\ip This book never names its writer. At the end it says only that "the disciple whom Jesus loved" is the one who testifies to these things and who wrote them, and that a group of others — "we" — vouch that his testimony is true (21:24). That disciple is in the room at the last supper, leaning against Jesus; he is at the cross when none of the other men are; he is first to the empty tomb and first to recognize the figure on the shore. He sees before anyone else sees. The name John comes from the title the early copies carry, not from anything the book says about itself.
\ip He writes Greek for people who do not live inside Jewish practice. Every Hebrew or Aramaic word he keeps, he stops and translates — Rabbi, Messiah, Cephas, Siloam, Gabbatha, Rabbouni. He explains that the stone jars standing at a wedding are there "for the purification of the Jews," that Jews and Samaritans have no dealings, that winding a body in linen with spices is how the Jews bury. He tells his readers that Passover and Tents are feasts "of the Jews." Nobody inside that world needs to be told any of it.
\ip One word he uses that no other book in the New Testament uses says what his readers were up against: \tl aposynagōgos\tl*, put out of the synagogue. It appears three times (9:22; 12:42; 16:2), and every time it is the price of saying out loud that Jesus is the Christ. The synagogue was not only a house of prayer; it was the community itself — its court, its trade, its marriages, its burials. To be put out of it was to lose everything at once. A blind man healed on a Sabbath is put out for refusing to take back what he knows. Rulers who do believe keep silent rather than risk it. And Jesus warns that worse is coming: an hour when the men who kill you will think they are bringing God an offering. This book is written for people who are being made to choose, and who know exactly what the choosing costs.
\ip So it is built to make that choice possible. It opens before the world was made and tells you who Jesus is before anything happens, so that you watch the rest knowing what nobody in the story knows. Then it sets out seven signs, each followed by a discourse that says what the sign meant — and each discourse splits the crowd that hears it. Halfway through, the public teaching stops; Jesus speaks only to his own, at length, on the night he is handed over. He dies at the hour the Passover lambs are being killed. And the book says outright why it exists: "these have been written so that you may trust that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God, and so that trusting you may have life in his name" (20:31).

\c 1
\s1 In the Beginning Was the Word
\p
\v 1 In the beginning\f + \fr 1:1 \ft The opening words of Genesis, word for word as the Greek Old Testament has them. But Genesis says God "made"; this says the Word "was". Whatever the beginning was the beginning of, the Word was already there when it started.\f* was the Word\f + \fr 1:1 \ft Greek \tl logos\tl*. Several streams meet in this one word. God creates by speaking — "God said, Let there be light" — and Israel's poets treat that spoken word as an agent sent out to do work: "By the word of the LORD the heavens were made" (Psalm 33:6); "He sent out his word and healed them" (Psalm 107:20); "it will not return to me empty" (Isaiah 55:11). Israel's wisdom writers say the same of Wisdom, who was beside God before the world and worked with him at the making of it (Proverbs 8:22-31). And in the Aramaic paraphrases read aloud in the synagogues, wherever God acts in the world it is "the Memra" — the Word — of the LORD who acts. Every one of those stops short of someone you could meet on a road. Verse 14 does not.\f*, and the Word was with God\f + \fr 1:1 \ft The preposition is \tl pros\tl*, "toward" — not merely beside but face to face. The Word is turned toward God.\f*, and the Word was God\f + \fr 1:1 \ft The Greek word order is "God was the Word," with \tl theos\tl* carrying no article and standing first. That construction states what the Word is, not which one he is. It does not collapse him into the God he was just said to be "with"; it says that whatever God is, the Word is.\f*.
\v 2 This one was in the beginning with God.
\v 3 All things came to be through him, and apart from him not one thing came to be. What has come to be\f + \fr 1:3-4 \ft The sentence break here follows the punctuation of the critical Greek text, which carries "What has come to be" forward into verse 4. Most English versions break the other way: "nothing was made that has been made. In him was life."\f*
\v 4 in him was life, and the life was the light of men.
\v 5 And the light shines in the darkness, and the darkness did not lay hold of it\f + \fr 1:5 \ft Greek \tl katelaben\tl*, which means both to seize or overpower and to grasp or understand. The darkness neither put the light out nor took it in. English cannot hold both, so "lay hold of" is used to keep the door open; most versions pick one — "overcome," or "understood."\f*.
\p
\v 6 There came a man, sent from God; his name was John.
\v 7 This one came as a witness, to testify about the light, so that all might trust through him.
\v 8 He was not the light, but he came to testify about the light \add [i.e. the lamp is not the sun; do not mistake the one for the other]\add*.
\v 9 The true\f + \fr 1:9 \ft Greek \tl alēthinos\tl* — not "true" as opposed to a lie, but the real thing as opposed to the copies and previews that pointed to it. This writer uses the word the same way of the true bread (6:32), the true worshipers (4:23), and the true vine (15:1).\f* light, which gives light to every man, was coming into the world\f + \fr 1:9 \ft "Coming into the world" can attach either to the light or to every man. It is taken here of the light. The other reading is "the true light that gives light to every man coming into the world."\f*.
\v 10 He was in the world, and the world came to be through him, and the world did not know him.
\v 11 He came to what was his own, and his own people did not receive him.
\v 12 But as many as did receive him, to them he gave the right to become children of God — to those who trust in his name,
\v 13 who were begotten not out of bloods\f + \fr 1:13 \ft The Greek plural is odd and deliberate — probably both parents' lines. Three things are being ruled out in a row: ancestry, the body's own urge, and a father's decision. Those are the three ways covenant membership had always been handed down. None of them makes a child of God.\f*, nor out of the will of flesh, nor out of the will of a man, but out of God.
\s1 The Word Became Flesh
\p
\v 14 And the Word became flesh\f + \fr 1:14 \ft Not "became a man" and not "took a body." \tl Sarx\tl*, flesh, is the most physical and most breakable word available. "All flesh is grass" (Isaiah 40:6). The one who was not made entered the condition of what wears out and dies.\f* and pitched his tent among us\f + \fr 1:14 \ft Greek \tl eskēnōsen\tl*, from \tl skēnē\tl*, tent — the Greek Old Testament's word for the tabernacle Israel carried through the wilderness. "Let them make me a sanctuary, that I may dwell among them" (Exodus 25:8); and when it was finished, "the glory of the LORD filled the tabernacle" (Exodus 40:34). That is the whole sentence in miniature: a tent pitched among the people, and the glory inside it.\f*, and we gazed upon his glory — glory as of an only son from a father — full of favor and truth\f + \fr 1:14 \ft Behind the Greek pair stands the Hebrew \tl chesed we'emet\tl*, covenant-love and faithfulness — the two words God speaks over himself when he passes in front of Moses on the mountain: "abounding in steadfast love and faithfulness" (Exodus 34:6). What Moses heard God say about himself, this writer says is standing in front of him, full of it.\f*.
\v 15 John testifies about him and has cried out, saying, "This was the one I spoke of: 'The one coming after me has come to be ahead of me, because he was before me.'"
\v 16 For out of his fullness we have all received, and favor in place of favor\f + \fr 1:16 \ft Greek \tl charin anti charitos\tl*. \tl Anti\tl* means "instead of, in exchange for" — one gift replacing another, as verse 17 goes on to describe. Most English versions have "grace upon grace," a piling up, which is not what the preposition says.\f*.
\v 17 For the Torah was given through Moses; favor and truth came to be through Jesus Christ \add [i.e. both came from God, and both came through a man; the second does not cancel the first, it carries it the whole way]\add*.
\v 18 No one has ever seen God. The only one, himself God\f + \fr 1:18 \ft The critical text reads \tl monogenēs theos\tl*, "the only one, God." The familiar reading, from the majority of later manuscripts, is "the only begotten Son."\f*, who is in the bosom of the Father \add [i.e. close in against him, the way a guest at a meal leans back against the host]\add* — that one has made him known\f + \fr 1:18 \ft Greek \tl exēgēsato\tl*, from which "exegesis" comes: to lead out, set forth, tell in full. Every earlier showing of God was partial. This one is the telling itself.\f*.
\s1 What John Told the Delegation from Jerusalem
\p
\v 19 And this is the testimony of John, when the Jews\f + \fr 1:19 \ft "The Jews" renders \tl hoi Ioudaioi\tl* everywhere it occurs in this book, but English forces one word onto a term that shifts. Sometimes it means the people; sometimes the inhabitants of Judea as against Galilee; and very often, as here, the ruling authorities in Jerusalem, who send priests and Levites and expect answers. At 11:19 and 11:31 it plainly means ordinary neighbors come to a funeral; at 7:13 and 9:22 it plainly means the men with the power to expel. The reader has to judge by the scene.\f* sent priests and Levites to him from Jerusalem to ask him, "Who are you?"
\v 20 And he confessed and did not deny; he confessed, "I am not the Christ."
\v 21 And they asked him, "What then? Are you Elijah?\f + \fr 1:21 \ft Malachi's last words promised Elijah back before the great and terrible day of the LORD (Malachi 4:5). The delegation is working down a list of the figures expected before the end, and Elijah is on it.\f*" And he says, "I am not." "Are you the Prophet?\f + \fr 1:21 \ft The prophet like Moses of Deuteronomy 18:15-18, expected as a distinct figure from both Elijah and the Christ.\f*" And he answered, "No."
\v 22 So they said to him, "Who are you? — so that we may give an answer to those who sent us. What do you say about yourself?"
\v 23 He said, "I am a voice of one shouting in the wilderness, 'Make straight the way of the Lord,' as Isaiah the prophet said\f + \fr 1:23 \ft Isaiah 40:3. In Isaiah the road being cleared is the LORD's own road back to his people at the end of the exile. John claims to be the roadwork and nothing else — not a person of rank but a voice.\f*."
\p
\v 24 And they had been sent from the Pharisees.
\v 25 And they asked him and said to him, "Why then do you immerse\f + \fr 1:25 \ft Greek \tl baptizō\tl*, to dip or plunge under. Washing by immersion was ordinary in Jewish practice — for uncleanness, and for a Gentile coming into Israel. What was not ordinary was doing it to Israelites, which treats the covenant people as though they were standing outside and had to come back in. Hence the question: by whose authority?\f*, if you are not the Christ, nor Elijah, nor the Prophet?"
\v 26 John answered them, saying, "I immerse in water. In your midst stands one you do not know,
\v 27 the one coming after me, of whom I am not worthy to untie the strap of his sandal \add [i.e. the one job so low that a Jewish slave could not be made to do it]\add*."
\v 28 These things happened in Bethany beyond the Jordan\f + \fr 1:28 \ft The critical text reads "Bethany beyond the Jordan"; the majority text and older English versions have "Bethabara." This is the east bank, the side Israel camped on before Joshua led them across into the land. "Jesus" is the Greek form of "Joshua."\f*, where John was immersing.
\s1 Look — the Lamb of God
\p
\v 29 The next day he sees Jesus coming toward him and says, "Look — the Lamb of God\f + \fr 1:29 \ft No earlier text says this in so many words, but three things are audible at once. The Passover lamb, whose blood on the doorposts held death back and whose flesh was eaten on the night of the exodus (Exodus 12) — and this book will have Jesus die at the hour those lambs are killed. The lamb of Isaiah 53:7, led to the slaughter without opening his mouth, who "bore the sin of many." And the lamb of Genesis 22, when Isaac asks where it is and Abraham answers, "God will provide for himself the lamb." This one is God's own — the lamb God provides.\f*, who lifts away the sin of the world.
\v 30 This is the one I spoke of: 'After me comes a man who has come to be ahead of me, because he was before me.'
\v 31 And I did not know him; but for this I came immersing in water — so that he might be made visible to Israel."
\v 32 And John testified, saying, "I have gazed upon the Spirit coming down like a dove out of heaven, and it remained on him\f + \fr 1:32 \ft "Remained" is \tl emeinen\tl*, from \tl menō\tl*, the verb that runs through this whole book — the disciples staying with him (1:39), the word staying in a man (5:38), the branch staying in the vine (15:4). On the prophets the Spirit came and went; on Saul it came and later left him (1 Samuel 16:14). This one it comes down on and stays.\f*.
\v 33 And I did not know him, but the one who sent me to immerse in water, that one said to me, 'The one on whom you see the Spirit coming down and remaining on him — this is the one who immerses in the Holy Spirit \add [i.e. the outpouring the prophets promised for the last days]\add*.'
\v 34 And I have seen and have testified that this one is the Son of God."
\s1 The First to Follow
\p
\v 35 The next day again John was standing there, and two of his disciples,
\v 36 and looking at Jesus as he walked, he says, "Look — the Lamb of God."
\v 37 And the two disciples heard him speaking, and they followed Jesus \add [i.e. the witness did exactly what a witness is for: he lost his own followers to the one he pointed at]\add*.
\v 38 And Jesus turned and saw them following and says to them, "What are you looking for?\f + \fr 1:38 \ft The first words out of Jesus's mouth in this book are a question, and it is the question the book keeps asking. He asks it again in the garden when the soldiers come for him (18:4, 7) and once more in the garden of the tomb (20:15). What are you after? Whom are you after?\f*" And they said to him, "Rabbi" (which is translated, Teacher), "where are you staying?"
\v 39 He says to them, "Come and you will see." So they came and saw where he was staying, and they stayed with him that day; it was about the tenth hour \add [i.e. mid-afternoon — the kind of detail a man remembers because he was there]\add*.
\v 40 Andrew, the brother of Simon Peter, was one of the two who heard from John and followed him.
\v 41 This one first finds his own brother Simon and says to him, "We have found the Messiah" (which is translated, Christ)\f + \fr 1:41 \ft \tl Messias\tl* is the Aramaic; \tl Christos\tl* is the Greek for it. Both mean "anointed one." Anointing with oil was how a man was installed in office — kings, priests. It is a job title, not a surname.\f*.
\v 42 He brought him to Jesus. Jesus looked straight at him and said, "You are Simon the son of John\f + \fr 1:42 \ft The critical text reads "son of John"; older English versions have "son of Jona."\f*; you will be called Cephas" (which is translated, Peter) \add [i.e. Rock, in his own language and then in theirs]\add*.
\s1 Philip and Nathanael
\p
\v 43 The next day he wanted to go out into Galilee, and he finds Philip. And Jesus says to him, "Follow me."
\v 44 Now Philip was from Bethsaida, out of the city of Andrew and Peter.
\v 45 Philip finds Nathanael and says to him, "The one Moses wrote of in the Torah, and the prophets too — we have found him: Jesus son of Joseph, the one from Nazareth."
\v 46 And Nathanael said to him, "Can anything good come out of Nazareth?\f + \fr 1:46 \ft Not snobbery about a backwater so much as an argument from Scripture: no text points to Nazareth. The village is unmentioned in the Old Testament, in Josephus, and in the rabbinic writings.\f*" Philip says to him, "Come and see."
\v 47 Jesus saw Nathanael coming toward him and says about him, "Look — truly an Israelite, and no deceit in him\f + \fr 1:47 \ft Greek \tl dolos\tl*, the word the Greek Old Testament uses when Isaac tells Esau, "Your brother came with deceit and took your blessing" (Genesis 27:35). Jacob was the man of \tl dolos\tl*, and Jacob was renamed Israel. Here is an Israelite with the name and without the flaw.\f*."
\v 48 Nathanael says to him, "How do you know me?" Jesus answered and said to him, "Before Philip called you, while you were under the fig tree, I saw you."
\v 49 Nathanael answered him, "Rabbi, you are the Son of God, you are the King of Israel\f + \fr 1:49 \ft Both titles belong to the king of David's line. "You are my Son; today I have begotten you" is said over him at his crowning (Psalm 2:7; compare 2 Samuel 7:14). Pilate will have the second one written over a cross (19:19).\f*."
\v 50 Jesus answered and said to him, "Because I told you that I saw you under the fig tree, do you trust? You will see greater things than these."
\v 51 And he says to him, "Amen, amen\f + \fr 1:51 \ft The doubled "amen" is this book's own; the other three accounts use it single. It marks what follows as spoken on the speaker's own authority.\f*, I say to you, you will see heaven opened and the angels of God going up and coming down on the son of man\f + \fr 1:51 \ft Jacob dreamed of a stairway set on the earth with its top in heaven, and the angels of God going up and coming down on it, and woke saying, "This is the gate of heaven" (Genesis 28:12-17). Jesus takes the ladder out of the dream and puts himself where it stood. The traffic between heaven and earth runs over him.\f*."

\c 2
\s1 The Wine at Cana
\p
\v 1 And on the third day there was a wedding in Cana of Galilee, and the mother of Jesus was there.
\v 2 And Jesus also was invited to the wedding, and his disciples.
\v 3 And when the wine ran short \add [i.e. a public disgrace for the family, and a week-long feast still to get through]\add*, the mother of Jesus says to him, "They have no wine."
\v 4 And Jesus says to her, "What is that to me and to you, woman?\f + \fr 2:4 \ft "Woman" is not rude — he uses it again to the Samaritan at the well, to Mary at the tomb, and to his mother from the cross. But "what is that to me and to you" is a set Hebrew phrase for declining to be drawn in (Judges 11:12; 2 Samuel 16:10; 1 Kings 17:18). He is saying that her request does not set his timetable.\f* My hour has not yet come\f + \fr 2:4 \ft "The hour" enters here and governs the rest of the book. It is not the hour of a miracle; it is the hour of his death and glory. Twice men fail to seize him "because his hour had not yet come" (7:30; 8:20). Then at 12:23 it comes.\f*."
\v 5 His mother says to the servants, "Whatever he says to you, do it \add [i.e. exactly what Pharaoh said of Joseph when the famine came]\add*."
\v 6 Now six\f + \fr 2:6 \ft Six, one short of seven. Stone was used because stone, unlike clay, was held not to take on uncleanness. These jars are the working equipment of the purification system, and there is enough water in them — well over a hundred gallons in total — to supply a wedding many times this size.\f* stone water jars were standing there for the purification of the Jews, each holding two or three measures.
\v 7 Jesus says to them, "Fill the jars with water." And they filled them to the brim.
\v 8 And he says to them, "Now draw some out and take it to the master of the feast." And they took it.
\v 9 And when the master of the feast tasted the water that had become wine — and he did not know where it came from, though the servants who had drawn the water knew \add [i.e. the ones who did what he said were the ones who knew what had happened]\add* — the master of the feast calls the bridegroom
\v 10 and says to him, "Every man sets out the good wine first, and when they have drunk freely, the lesser. You have kept the good wine until now\f + \fr 2:10 \ft The prophets marked the age to come by wine running down the hills: "the mountains shall drip sweet wine" (Amos 9:13; Joel 3:18), a feast of aged wine on the mountain (Isaiah 25:6). The steward is complaining about the order of service. He is describing the arrival of that age and does not know it.\f*."
\v 11 This, the beginning of the signs\f + \fr 2:11 \ft Greek \tl sēmeion\tl*, a sign — something that points past itself. The other three accounts prefer "acts of power"; this writer never once calls them that. The Greek Old Testament uses \tl sēmeia\tl* of what God did in Egypt (Exodus 7:3; Deuteronomy 4:34). Seven of them are told in this book, and the writer says at the end why he chose the ones he chose (20:30-31).\f*, Jesus did in Cana of Galilee, and he made his glory visible, and his disciples trusted in him.
\p
\v 12 After this he went down to Capernaum, he and his mother and his brothers and his disciples, and there they stayed a few days.
\s1 Clearing the Temple
\p
\v 13 And the Passover of the Jews was near, and Jesus went up to Jerusalem.
\v 14 And in the temple he found those selling cattle and sheep and doves, and the money-changers sitting there \add [i.e. the trade that let pilgrims from far off buy an animal on arrival and change foreign coin for the one currency the temple would take]\add*.
\v 15 And having made a whip out of cords he drove them all out of the temple, with the sheep and the cattle, and he poured out the coins of the money-changers and overturned their tables.
\v 16 And to those selling the doves he said, "Take these out of here! Do not make my Father's house a house of trade\f + \fr 2:16 \ft Zechariah's last chapter ends with the day when "there shall no longer be a trader in the house of the LORD of hosts" (Zechariah 14:21). Malachi says the Lord will come suddenly to his temple and purify it (Malachi 3:1-3). What he does here is what those texts said would be done, and he calls the house his Father's while he does it.\f*."
\v 17 His disciples remembered that it is written, "Zeal for your house will eat me up\f + \fr 2:17 \ft Psalm 69:9, but with the tense moved from past to future: in the psalm the zeal "has" consumed him; here it "will". Psalm 69 is the psalm of the man hated without cause, estranged from his brothers, given vinegar for his thirst. This book will quote it twice more, at 15:25 and at the cross (19:28-29).\f*."
\v 18 So the Jews answered and said to him, "What sign do you show us, that you do these things?"
\v 19 Jesus answered and said to them, "Tear down this sanctuary\f + \fr 2:19 \ft He switches words. Verse 14 has \tl hieron\tl*, the whole temple complex with its courts; here he says \tl naos\tl*, the inner house where the presence dwells. That is the word verse 21 explains.\f*, and in three days I will raise it up."
\v 20 So the Jews said, "This sanctuary was built in forty-six years \add [i.e. Herod's rebuilding, begun before any of us were grown and still not finished]\add*, and you will raise it up in three days?"
\v 21 But he was speaking about the sanctuary of his body.
\v 22 So when he was raised from among the dead, his disciples remembered that he had said this, and they trusted the Scripture and the word Jesus had spoken \add [i.e. they lived through it first and understood it afterward, which is how it goes all through this book]\add*.
\p
\v 23 And while he was in Jerusalem at the Passover, at the feast, many trusted in his name, watching the signs he was doing.
\v 24 But Jesus himself was not entrusting himself to them\f + \fr 2:24 \ft The same verb both ways: they trusted him, he did not trust them. Signs can produce a real response that will not hold weight. Chapter 6 will show a crowd like this one walking away.\f*, because he knew all men,
\v 25 and because he had no need for anyone to testify about man; for he himself knew what was in man.

\c 3
\s1 Nicodemus Comes at Night
\p
\v 1 Now there was a man\f + \fr 3:1 \ft The chapter break hides the join. The last line of chapter 2 says he knew what was in "man"; the first line here says there was a "man". Nicodemus is the specimen.\f* from among the Pharisees, Nicodemus his name, a ruler of the Jews \add [i.e. a member of the council that governs the nation]\add*.
\v 2 This one came to him by night\f + \fr 3:2 \ft A councillor visiting a controversial Galilean might sensibly come after dark. But in this book night is never only the time of day: the light shines in the darkness (1:5), men loved the darkness (3:19), and when Judas goes out to betray him the writer says simply, "and it was night" (13:30). Nicodemus comes out of the dark toward the light, and this chapter leaves him standing between the two.\f* and said to him, "Rabbi, we know that you have come from God as a teacher; for no one can do these signs that you do unless God is with him."
\v 3 Jesus answered and said to him, "Amen, amen, I say to you, unless someone is begotten from above\f + \fr 3:3 \ft Greek \tl anōthen\tl*, which means both "from above" and "again." It is taken here as "from above," which is how this writer uses the word everywhere else (3:31; 19:11, 23). But Nicodemus's reply in the next verse shows he heard "again," and English cannot say both at once. The same word, and the same problem, at verse 7.\f*, he cannot see the kingdom of God\f + \fr 3:3 \ft This book almost never says "the kingdom of God" — only here and in verse 5, where the other three accounts use it constantly. It is the language Nicodemus would use: God's reign taking effect in the open, the thing the prophets promised (Daniel 2:44; Obadiah 21). Elsewhere this writer says "life of the age" for the same thing.\f*."
\v 4 Nicodemus says to him, "How can a man be begotten when he is old? He cannot enter his mother's womb a second time and be born, can he?"
\v 5 Jesus answered, "Amen, amen, I say to you, unless someone is begotten out of water and Spirit\f + \fr 3:5 \ft Ezekiel put the two together in one promise: "I will sprinkle clean water on you, and you shall be clean... And I will put my Spirit within you" (Ezekiel 36:25-27; compare Isaiah 44:3). A teacher of Israel is expected to recognize what is being quoted at him.\f*, he cannot enter the kingdom of God.
\v 6 What has been begotten out of the flesh is flesh, and what has been begotten out of the Spirit is spirit \add [i.e. each kind produces its own kind, and no amount of the first will ever yield the second]\add*.
\v 7 Do not be amazed that I said to you, 'You must be begotten from above.'
\v 8 The wind blows where it wishes, and you hear the sound of it, but you do not know where it comes from or where it is going. So it is with everyone who has been begotten out of the Spirit\f + \fr 3:8 \ft One Greek word, \tl pneuma\tl*, means both wind and Spirit, and Hebrew \tl ruach\tl* does the same. The whole saying turns on that single word, and English has to break it in two. You can hear the wind and you cannot manage it — which is the point being made to a man whose profession is knowing how things work.\f*."
\p
\v 9 Nicodemus answered and said to him, "How can these things happen?"
\v 10 Jesus answered and said to him, "You are the teacher of Israel, and you do not know these things? \add [i.e. this is not new doctrine; the prophets you teach said it]\add*
\v 11 Amen, amen, I say to you, we speak what we know and we testify to what we have seen, and you do not receive our testimony.
\v 12 If I told you earthly things and you do not trust, how will you trust if I tell you heavenly things?
\v 13 And no one has gone up into heaven except the one who came down out of heaven, the son of man\f + \fr 3:13 \ft The words "who is in heaven," which older English versions add at the end of this verse, are not in the critical text.\f*.
\v 14 And just as Moses lifted up the serpent in the wilderness\f + \fr 3:14 \ft Numbers 21:4-9. Snakes were killing the people; God told Moses to put a bronze serpent on a pole, and "everyone who is bitten, when he looks at it, shall live." Looking was required. Those who looked lived; those who did not, died. A first-century Jewish comment on the episode is careful to say the bronze thing saved nobody — God did (Wisdom 16:6-7). But it had to be lifted up where all could see it.\f*, so must the son of man be lifted up\f + \fr 3:14 \ft Greek \tl hypsōthēnai\tl*, which means both to be hoisted up physically and to be raised in honor — the same double sense as in "my servant shall be exalted and lifted up" (Isaiah 52:13). This writer uses it three times (here, 8:28, 12:32) and then tells you outright what he means by it: "he was saying this to signify by what kind of death he was about to die" (12:33). The hoisting is a crucifixion, and the crucifixion is the exaltation.\f*,
\v 15 so that everyone who trusts may have in him life of the age\f + \fr 3:15 \ft The critical text attaches "in him" to the having, as rendered. Most English versions attach it to the trusting: "whoever believes in him may have eternal life." "Life of the age" is \tl zōē aiōnios\tl* — not simply life that goes on and on, but the life that belongs to the age to come, the life of God's own restored world (Daniel 12:2). Chapter 17 defines it: "that they know you, the only true God" (17:3).\f*.
\p
\v 16 "For this is how God loved the world\f + \fr 3:16 \ft Greek \tl houtōs\tl*, which states manner, not amount: this is how, this is the way. Most English versions have "God so loved the world," and readers hear a measure of how much. The verse is not measuring the love; it is pointing at what the love did.\f*: he gave the only Son\f + \fr 3:16 \ft Greek \tl monogenēs\tl*, "the only one there is, one of a kind" — not "only begotten." It is the Greek Old Testament's word for Isaac in Genesis 22:2: "Take your son, your only son, whom you love." Everyone who heard it heard Abraham on the mountain. The same word at 1:14, 1:18, and 3:18.\f*, so that everyone who trusts in him might not be destroyed but have life of the age.
\v 17 For God did not send the Son into the world to judge the world, but so that the world might be rescued through him.
\v 18 The one who trusts in him is not judged. The one who does not trust has been judged already, because he has not trusted in the name of the only Son of God \add [i.e. no further verdict is needed; refusing the light is the verdict]\add*.
\v 19 And this is the judgment: that the light has come into the world, and men loved the darkness rather than the light, for their works were evil.
\v 20 For everyone who does worthless things hates the light and does not come to the light, so that his works may not be shown up \add [i.e. what he wants is not darkness for its own sake but cover]\add*.
\v 21 But the one who does the truth\f + \fr 3:21 \ft "Doing the truth" is a Hebrew way of speaking — \tl 'asah emet\tl*, to act faithfully, to deal honestly (Genesis 47:29; Nehemiah 9:33). Truth here is not something believed but something done.\f* comes to the light, so that his works may be made visible as worked in God."\f + \fr 3:16-21 \ft Greek has no quotation marks, and nothing in the text says whether Jesus is still speaking from verse 16 or the writer has taken over. The quotation is run through verse 21 here. English versions divide; some close it at verse 15.\f*
\s1 John's Last Word: He Must Grow Greater
\p
\v 22 After these things Jesus and his disciples came into the Judean land, and there he spent time with them and was immersing.
\v 23 And John also was immersing in Aenon near Salim, because there was much water there, and they were coming and being immersed;
\v 24 for John had not yet been thrown into prison \add [i.e. the two ministries overlapped, which is why the comparison could be made at all]\add*.
\v 25 So a dispute arose from John's disciples with a Jew about purification.
\v 26 And they came to John and said to him, "Rabbi, the one who was with you beyond the Jordan, to whom you have testified — look, this one is immersing, and all are coming to him \add [i.e. we are losing our crowd to him]\add*."
\v 27 John answered and said, "A man cannot receive anything unless it has been given to him out of heaven \add [i.e. neither of us picked his own place; there is nothing here to compete over]\add*.
\v 28 You yourselves testify for me that I said, 'I am not the Christ,' but 'I have been sent ahead of that one.'
\v 29 The one who has the bride is the bridegroom\f + \fr 3:29 \ft In the prophets the bridegroom of Israel is God himself: "your Maker is your husband" (Isaiah 54:5); "as the bridegroom rejoices over the bride, so shall your God rejoice over you" (Isaiah 62:5). John hands that role to Jesus and keeps for himself the role of the friend who arranged the match — whose work is finished the moment the couple is joined, and who is not sorry about it.\f*. But the friend of the bridegroom, who stands and hears him, rejoices with joy at the bridegroom's voice. So this joy of mine has been made full.
\v 30 That one must grow greater, and I must grow less \add [i.e. not a defeat but the plan; a forerunner who stayed prominent would have failed]\add*.
\p
\v 31 "The one who comes from above is over all. The one who is out of the earth is out of the earth and speaks out of the earth. The one who comes out of heaven is over all.
\v 32 What he has seen and heard, this he testifies, and no one receives his testimony.
\v 33 The one who has received his testimony has set his seal that God is true \add [i.e. to take the Son's word is to certify that God does not lie]\add*.
\v 34 For the one whom God sent speaks the words of God, for he does not give the Spirit by measure\f + \fr 3:34 \ft The Greek does not say who does the giving; it is left unstated here, as the Greek leaves it. Many versions supply "God." The point either way is the absence of a limit: the prophets got the Spirit for a task and a season, this one without a ration.\f*.
\v 35 The Father loves the Son and has given all things into his hand.
\v 36 The one who trusts in the Son has life of the age; but the one who refuses to obey\f + \fr 3:36 \ft Greek \tl apeitheō\tl*, to refuse compliance, to disobey — not \tl apisteō\tl*, to disbelieve. Most English versions have "does not believe." The word chosen is the one the Greek Old Testament uses for Israel's refusals in the wilderness (Deuteronomy 1:26). The opposite of trusting the Son, in this sentence, is not doubting him but not doing what he says.\f* the Son will not see life — instead the wrath of God remains on him \add [i.e. it does not arrive as a new sentence; it is already there and does not lift]\add*."\f + \fr 3:31-36 \ft As with verses 16-21, nothing in the Greek marks where the speech ends. It is run here as John's words through verse 36; it may equally be the writer's own comment.\f*

\c 4
\s1 Through Samaria
\p
\v 1 So when Jesus knew that the Pharisees had heard that Jesus was making and immersing more disciples than John —
\v 2 although Jesus himself was not immersing, but his disciples —
\v 3 he left Judea and went away again into Galilee.
\v 4 And he had to pass through Samaria\f + \fr 4:4 \ft It was the direct road, but many Jews went the long way round through Perea to avoid Samaritan ground; Josephus records travellers being killed on this route. The verb is \tl edei\tl*, "it was necessary" — the word this book uses for what God's plan requires (3:14; 3:30; 9:4; 10:16).\f*.
\v 5 So he comes to a city of Samaria called Sychar, near the piece of land that Jacob gave to his son Joseph \add [i.e. the ground both peoples claim, where Joseph's bones were buried when they were brought up out of Egypt]\add*.
\v 6 And Jacob's spring was there. So Jesus, worn out from the journey, was sitting just as he was by the spring. It was about the sixth hour \add [i.e. midday, the hottest part, when nobody comes for water if she can help it]\add*.
\p
\v 7 A woman out of Samaria comes to draw water. Jesus says to her, "Give me a drink" —
\v 8 for his disciples had gone away into the city to buy food.
\v 9 So the Samaritan woman says to him, "How is it that you, being a Jew, ask a drink from me, a Samaritan woman?" — for Jews have no dealings with Samaritans\f + \fr 4:9 \ft Greek \tl synchraomai\tl*, which can mean generally "have dealings with" and more narrowly "use vessels in common with." The broader sense is taken. The quarrel was centuries old: after Assyria emptied the northern kingdom and settled foreigners in it (2 Kings 17:24-41), the community that resulted kept only the five books of Moses, worshiped on Mount Gerizim, and was regarded by Jews as no nation at all (Sirach 50:25-26).\f*.
\v 10 Jesus answered and said to her, "If you knew the gift of God, and who it is who says to you, 'Give me a drink,' you would have asked him, and he would have given you living water\f + \fr 4:10 \ft In everyday Greek "living water" just means running water, from a spring rather than a cistern — which is how she takes it. In the prophets it means God himself: "they have forsaken me, the fountain of living waters, and hewn out for themselves cisterns, broken cisterns that can hold no water" (Jeremiah 2:13). Zechariah promised living waters flowing out of Jerusalem on that day (Zechariah 14:8); Ezekiel saw a river running out from under the temple (Ezekiel 47:1-12).\f*."
\v 11 The woman says to him, "Sir, you have no bucket, and the well is deep. Where then do you get the living water?
\v 12 You are not greater than our father Jacob, are you, who gave us the well and drank from it himself, and his sons and his livestock?"
\v 13 Jesus answered and said to her, "Everyone who drinks of this water will thirst again;
\v 14 but whoever drinks of the water that I will give him will never thirst into the age. Instead the water that I will give him will become in him a spring of water leaping up into life of the age \add [i.e. he stops being someone who must keep coming back to a well, and becomes a well]\add*."
\v 15 The woman says to him, "Sir, give me this water, so that I may not thirst nor come here to draw."
\p
\v 16 He says to her, "Go, call your husband and come here."
\v 17 The woman answered and said to him, "I have no husband." Jesus says to her, "You said it well: 'I have no husband';
\v 18 for you have had five husbands, and the one you have now is not your husband. This you have said truly \add [i.e. he names it exactly, without insult and without letting it stand as an obstacle]\add*."
\v 19 The woman says to him, "Sir, I see that you are a prophet \add [i.e. only a prophet knows what he has no way of knowing]\add*.
\v 20 Our fathers worshiped on this mountain\f + \fr 4:20 \ft Mount Gerizim, in sight of the well. The Samaritan Pentateuch commands an altar there; the Samaritans built a temple on it, and John Hyrcanus destroyed that temple in 128 BC. This is the one question dividing the two peoples: not whether to worship the God of Moses, but where he chose to be worshiped (Deuteronomy 12:5).\f*, and you people say that in Jerusalem is the place where one must worship."
\v 21 Jesus says to her, "Trust me, woman: an hour is coming when neither on this mountain nor in Jerusalem will you worship the Father.
\v 22 You worship what you do not know; we worship what we know, because the rescue is out of the Jews \add [i.e. the promise runs through that line, whatever this chapter is about to do with it]\add*.
\v 23 But an hour is coming, and is now here, when the true worshipers will worship the Father in spirit and truth; for indeed the Father is looking for such people to worship him \add [i.e. he is not waiting to be found; he is out searching]\add*.
\v 24 God is spirit, and those who worship him must worship in spirit and truth\f + \fr 4:24 \ft "In spirit" does not mean inwardly rather than outwardly. \tl Pneuma\tl* in this book is the Holy Spirit (1:32-33; 7:39; 14:17), the one who is given after the glorification. Worship "in spirit and truth" is worship the Spirit makes possible and the revelation in Jesus shapes. Solomon had already said the building could not hold God (1 Kings 8:27). The question of "which mountain" dies here.\f*."
\v 25 The woman says to him, "I know that Messiah is coming, the one called Christ. When that one comes, he will announce everything to us \add [i.e. the Samaritans looked for the prophet like Moses of Deuteronomy 18, whose work is to tell them what God says]\add*."
\v 26 Jesus says to her, "I am — the one speaking to you\f + \fr 4:26 \ft The plainest self-disclosure in any of the four accounts, and it is made to a Samaritan woman at a well at midday rather than to the teacher of Israel in the previous chapter. The Greek is \tl egō eimi\tl*, "I am" — the words God speaks over himself in Exodus 3:14 and repeatedly in Isaiah ("that you may know and believe me and understand that I am he," Isaiah 43:10). Here the immediate sense is "I am the one you just described," but the phrase will come back through this book without any predicate at all (8:24, 28, 58; 18:5).\f*."
\p
\v 27 And at this his disciples came, and they were amazed that he was speaking with a woman; yet no one said, "What are you looking for?" or "Why are you speaking with her?"
\v 28 So the woman left her water jar \add [i.e. left behind the thing she had come for]\add* and went away into the city and says to the men,
\v 29 "Come, see a man who told me everything I ever did. This could not be the Christ, could it?" \add [i.e. she does not overstate it; she puts the question and lets them go and look]\add*
\v 30 They went out of the city and were coming to him.
\p
\v 31 In the meantime the disciples were urging him, saying, "Rabbi, eat."
\v 32 But he said to them, "I have food to eat that you do not know about."
\v 33 So the disciples said to one another, "No one brought him anything to eat, did they?"
\v 34 Jesus says to them, "My food is to do the will of the one who sent me and to finish his work\f + \fr 4:34 \ft "Finish" is \tl teleioō\tl*. The work has an end point, and he names it from the cross: "It has been finished" (19:30).\f*.
\v 35 Do you not say, 'Four months more, and the harvest comes'? Look, I say to you, lift up your eyes and see the fields — they are white for harvest \add [i.e. a whole town is at this moment walking out across those fields toward him]\add*. Already
\v 36 the reaper receives wages and gathers fruit for life of the age, so that the sower and the reaper may rejoice together.
\v 37 For in this the saying is true: 'One is the sower and another the reaper.'
\v 38 I sent you to reap what you have not labored for; others have labored, and you have entered into their labor."
\s1 The Samaritans Believe
\p
\v 39 And out of that city many of the Samaritans trusted in him because of the word of the woman testifying, "He told me everything I ever did."
\v 40 So when the Samaritans came to him, they were asking him to stay with them; and he stayed there two days \add [i.e. a Jewish teacher eating their food and sleeping under their roofs for two days, which was the whole quarrel, ignored]\add*.
\v 41 And many more trusted because of his own word,
\v 42 and they were saying to the woman, "No longer do we trust because of your talk, for we ourselves have heard, and we know that this one is truly the Rescuer of the world\f + \fr 4:42 \ft Greek \tl sōtēr tou kosmou\tl*. In Israel's scriptures "Savior" is God's own title (Isaiah 45:15, 21); in the empire it was given to Caesar, stamped on inscriptions all over the east. Samaritans, of all people, are the ones who say it, and they do not say "of Israel" — they say "of the world."\f*."
\p
\v 43 And after the two days he went out from there into Galilee.
\v 44 For Jesus himself testified that a prophet has no honor in his own homeland.
\v 45 So when he came into Galilee, the Galileans welcomed him, having seen everything he did in Jerusalem at the feast; for they too had gone to the feast \add [i.e. a welcome built on what they had watched him do, which is exactly the kind he does not entrust himself to]\add*.
\s1 The Second Sign: A Word Sent to Capernaum
\p
\v 46 So he came again to Cana of Galilee, where he had made the water wine. And there was a certain royal official \add [i.e. a man in the service of Herod's court]\add* whose son was sick in Capernaum.
\v 47 This man, hearing that Jesus had come out of Judea into Galilee, went off to him and was asking that he come down and heal his son, for he was about to die.
\v 48 So Jesus said to him, "Unless you people\f + \fr 4:48 \ft The "you" is plural. He is not answering this man only but the Galileans generally, who have just welcomed him on the strength of what they saw in Jerusalem. Set beside the Samaritans, who believed on a word with no sign at all, the contrast is the point.\f* see signs and wonders, you will never trust."
\v 49 The royal official says to him, "Sir, come down before my little child dies."
\v 50 Jesus says to him, "Go; your son lives." The man trusted the word Jesus said to him and went \add [i.e. he came for a visit and left with a sentence, and he went]\add*.
\v 51 And as he was already going down, his slaves met him, saying that his boy was alive.
\v 52 So he asked them the hour in which he had begun to get better. So they said to him, "Yesterday at the seventh hour the fever left him."
\v 53 So the father knew that it was at that hour in which Jesus said to him, "Your son lives"; and he trusted, he and his whole household.
\v 54 This again, a second sign, Jesus did when he came out of Judea into Galilee \add [i.e. the first sign changed what was in the jars; this one worked across twenty miles on nothing but a spoken word]\add*.

\c 5
\s1 Thirty-Eight Years at the Pool
\p
\v 1 After these things there was a feast of the Jews, and Jesus went up to Jerusalem.
\v 2 Now there is in Jerusalem, at the Sheep\f + \fr 5:2 \ft The Greek says only "at the sheep —" and stops; the noun is missing. "Gate" is supplied, as most do, from the sheep gate of Nehemiah 3:1 and 12:39, on the northeast side near the temple. It was the gate the sacrificial animals came through.\f* Gate, a pool called in Hebrew Bethzatha\f + \fr 5:2 \ft The critical text reads "Bethzatha"; most English versions have "Bethesda," probably from Aramaic \tl beth chesda\tl*, house of mercy. A twin pool with five colonnades — four round the sides and one across the middle — was excavated at this spot in the nineteenth century, exactly as described.\f*, with five colonnades.
\v 3 In these lay a crowd of the sick — blind, lame, withered\f + \fr 5:3 \ft The words "waiting for the moving of the water" at the end of this verse are not in the critical text, and neither is the whole of verse 4 — the angel who came down at a season and stirred the pool, and the healing of whoever got in first. That is why the numbering here goes from 3 to 5. The sick man's own explanation in verse 7 shows what the crowd believed; the note explaining it was added later by copyists.\f*.
\v 5 And a certain man was there who had been in his sickness thirty-eight years\f + \fr 5:5 \ft Thirty-eight years is the length of Israel's stalling in the wilderness — "the time from our leaving Kadesh-barnea until we crossed the brook Zered was thirty-eight years" (Deuteronomy 2:14), the years of a generation that could not go in. Whether the number is meant to be heard that way or is simply the number, it describes the same thing: a condition nobody expects to change.\f*.
\v 6 Jesus, seeing this one lying there and knowing that he had already been that way a long time, says to him, "Do you want to become well?\f + \fr 5:6 \ft Not a pointless question. After thirty-eight years a man's whole life is organized around the pool, the waiting, and the race he keeps losing. The question puts his own will in view before anything is done to him.\f*"
\v 7 The sick man answered him, "Sir, I have no man to throw me into the pool when the water is stirred up; and while I am coming, another goes down ahead of me \add [i.e. the system rewards the speed my condition takes away]\add*."
\v 8 Jesus says to him, "Get up, lift your mat and walk."
\v 9 And immediately the man became well, and he lifted his mat and was walking. Now it was a Sabbath on that day\f + \fr 5:9 \ft The writer withholds this until after the healing, so that the reader weighs the healing first and the violation second. He will do the same with the blind man in chapter 9.\f*.
\s1 My Father Is Working, and I Am Working
\p
\v 10 So the Jews were saying to the healed man, "It is a Sabbath, and it is not permitted for you to lift your mat\f + \fr 5:10 \ft They are not inventing a rule. Jeremiah 17:21-22 forbids carrying a load through the gates of Jerusalem on the Sabbath, and Nehemiah enforced it (Nehemiah 13:15-19). Note what they do not ask about: a man who could not walk for thirty-eight years is walking, and the question is about his bedding.\f*."
\v 11 But he answered them, "The one who made me well, that one said to me, 'Lift your mat and walk.'"
\v 12 They asked him, "Who is the man who said to you, 'Lift it and walk'?"
\v 13 But the healed man did not know who it was, for Jesus had slipped away, there being a crowd in the place.
\v 14 After these things Jesus finds him in the temple and said to him, "Look, you have become well. Sin no more, so that nothing worse happens to you \add [i.e. the body has been put right; do not suppose that is the whole of what is wrong]\add*."
\v 15 The man went off and told the Jews that it was Jesus who had made him well \add [i.e. and so the one who was healed became the means by which they found the healer]\add*.
\v 16 And for this reason the Jews were persecuting Jesus, because he was doing these things on a Sabbath.
\v 17 But he answered them, "My Father is working until now, and I am working\f + \fr 5:17 \ft This is not a denial of the charge but a reframing of it. Genesis says God rested on the seventh day, and Jewish teachers had long faced the obvious problem: children are born on the Sabbath and men die on the Sabbath, so God has plainly not downed tools. Philo of Alexandria argued that God never ceases at all, since everything would collapse if he did. The standing answer was that the two acts God never suspends are giving life and judging, because the whole world is his house and nothing in it lies outside his authority. Jesus says the Father has never stopped and neither has he — and then the discourse that follows takes up precisely those two prerogatives, life and judgment.\f*."
\v 18 So for this reason the Jews were seeking all the more to kill him, because he was not only loosing the Sabbath but was also calling God his own\f + \fr 5:18 \ft "His own" is the word that does the damage. Israel as a nation could call God Father (Deuteronomy 32:6; Isaiah 63:16). Saying "my" Father, in the exclusive sense, is heard as claiming his rank.\f* Father, making himself equal with God.
\p
\v 19 So Jesus answered and was saying to them, "Amen, amen, I say to you, the Son can do nothing from himself except what he sees the Father doing; for whatever that one does, these things the Son also does in the same way \add [i.e. this is not a lesser power but a complete dependence — and notice that nothing at all is excluded from it]\add*.
\v 20 For the Father loves the Son and shows him everything he himself does, and he will show him greater works than these, so that you may be amazed.
\v 21 For just as the Father raises the dead and makes them alive, so also the Son makes alive whom he wishes\f + \fr 5:21 \ft Raising the dead is God's alone. "It is I who put to death and give life" (Deuteronomy 32:39); "The LORD kills and brings to life" (1 Samuel 2:6); and when the king of Israel is asked to cure a leper he tears his clothes and says, "Am I God, to kill and to make alive?" (2 Kings 5:7). Elijah and Elisha raised the dead by asking God to do it. This says the Son does it, and does it "whom he wishes."\f*.
\v 22 For the Father judges no one, but has given all judgment to the Son,
\v 23 so that all may honor the Son just as they honor the Father\f + \fr 5:23 \ft This is the purpose of the handover, and it is the sharpest claim in the chapter. The honor owed to God is to be given to the Son — not a lesser honor of a similar kind, but the same. For anyone reciting "Hear, O Israel, the LORD our God, the LORD is one," the sentence is either blasphemy or the truth.\f*. The one who does not honor the Son does not honor the Father who sent him.
\v 24 Amen, amen, I say to you, the one who hears my word and trusts the one who sent me has life of the age and does not come into judgment, but has crossed over out of death into life\f + \fr 5:24 \ft "Has crossed over" is a perfect tense: a border already behind him. The same verb describes Jesus's own crossing "out of this world to the Father" (13:1).\f*.
\v 25 Amen, amen, I say to you, an hour is coming, and is now here, when the dead will hear the voice of the Son of God, and those who hear will live \add [i.e. the dead he means are the ones standing in front of him]\add*.
\v 26 For just as the Father has life in himself, so also he gave to the Son to have life in himself \add [i.e. not life drawn from a supply, but life as a source]\add*.
\v 27 And he gave him authority to do judgment, because he is a son of man\f + \fr 5:27 \ft The Greek has no article: "a son of man," that is, a human being. Most English versions capitalize it into the title, "the Son of Man." Both are in play — the figure of Daniel 7:13-14 who receives everlasting dominion, and the plain fact that the one who will judge human beings is one.\f*.
\v 28 Do not be amazed at this, because an hour is coming\f + \fr 5:28 \ft Notice the change. Verse 25 said "an hour is coming, and is now here" — the waking of the spiritually dead, already under way. Here the "and is now here" drops off. This second hour is future only: everyone in the graves, bodily, coming out.\f* in which all who are in the tombs will hear his voice
\v 29 and will come out — those who did good things, into a rising to life; and those who practiced worthless things, into a rising to judgment \add [i.e. as Daniel said, some to life of the age and some to shame (Daniel 12:2)]\add*.
\v 30 I can do nothing from myself. As I hear, I judge, and my judgment is right, because I do not seek my own will but the will of the one who sent me \add [i.e. the judgment is just precisely because the judge has no interest of his own in it]\add*.
\s1 The Witnesses Against You
\p
\v 31 "If I testify about myself, my testimony is not true\f + \fr 5:31 \ft He accepts the standard of their own law: a matter is established by two or three witnesses, not by one man's word about himself (Deuteronomy 19:15). What follows is the calling of witnesses.\f*.
\v 32 There is another who testifies about me, and I know that the testimony he testifies about me is true.
\v 33 You have sent to John, and he has testified to the truth \add [i.e. the delegation you sent to the Jordan came back with an answer you did not follow up]\add*.
\v 34 But I do not receive the testimony from a man; rather I say these things so that you may be rescued.
\v 35 That one was the lamp\f + \fr 5:35 \ft A lamp, not the light. The distinction was made in the prologue: "He was not the light" (1:8). A lamp burns for a while on borrowed fuel and shows you a room; it is not the day. "I have prepared a lamp for my anointed" (Psalm 132:17).\f* burning and shining, and you were willing to be glad for an hour in his light \add [i.e. you enjoyed the excitement while it lasted and did not follow where he pointed]\add*.
\v 36 But I have the testimony greater than John's; for the works the Father has given me to finish, these very works that I am doing, testify about me that the Father has sent me.
\v 37 And the Father who sent me, he himself has testified about me. You have never heard his voice nor seen his form\f + \fr 5:37 \ft At Sinai Israel "heard the sound of words, but saw no form" (Deuteronomy 4:12). He tells the custodians of that revelation that they have managed neither: not the voice, though they handle the text that records it, and not the form, though the works of God are being done in front of them.\f*,
\v 38 and you do not have his word remaining in you, because the one that one sent — him you do not trust.
\v 39 You search the Scriptures\f + \fr 5:39 \ft The Greek form is both indicative and imperative. It is taken here as a statement of fact — "you search," which they do, thoroughly and for a living. The other reading is a command, "Search the Scriptures."\f*, because you think that in them you have life of the age; and those are the very ones testifying about me \add [i.e. you have taken the signpost for the destination]\add*.
\v 40 And you are not willing to come to me so that you may have life \add [i.e. the problem is not that you cannot; it is that you will not]\add*.
\p
\v 41 "I do not receive glory from men.
\v 42 But I know you, that you do not have the love of God in yourselves.
\v 43 I have come in the name of my Father, and you do not receive me. If another comes in his own name, that one you will receive.
\v 44 How can you trust, when you receive glory from one another and do not seek the glory that is from the only God?\f + \fr 5:44 \ft This names the machinery. They live inside a closed exchange of honor — each man's standing conferred by the others' regard — and that system is the standard by which every claim gets weighed. To believe would mean stepping outside it and letting God's verdict be the only one that counts, at the cost of everything the system pays out. As long as it governs them, belief is not merely unlikely; it is structurally impossible.\f*
\v 45 Do not think that I will accuse you before the Father. There is one who accuses you: Moses, in whom you have set your hope.
\v 46 For if you trusted Moses, you would trust me, for that one wrote about me.
\v 47 But if you do not trust his writings, how will you trust my words?"

\c 6
\s1 Bread for Five Thousand
\p
\v 1 After these things Jesus went away across the Sea of Galilee — of Tiberias \add [i.e. the name the Roman world used for it, after the city Herod built on its shore]\add*.
\v 2 And a large crowd was following him, because they were watching the signs he was doing on the sick.
\v 3 And Jesus went up on the mountain and was sitting there with his disciples.
\v 4 Now the Passover, the feast of the Jews, was near\f + \fr 6:4 \ft The second of three Passovers in this book (2:13; 6:4; 11:55). It is not scene-setting. Everything that follows — bread in a wilderness, flesh to be eaten, blood, a crowd remembering Moses — is said against the sound of the Passover liturgy.\f*.
\v 5 So Jesus, lifting up his eyes and seeing that a large crowd was coming toward him, says to Philip, "Where shall we buy bread so that these may eat?"
\v 6 But he was saying this to test him\f + \fr 6:6 \ft Greek \tl peirazōn\tl*. It is the word used of what God did to Israel in the wilderness by means of bread: "I will rain bread from heaven for you... that I may test them, whether they will walk in my law or not" (Exodus 16:4; compare Deuteronomy 8:2, "to know what was in your heart"). The bread test is being run again.\f*, for he himself knew what he was about to do.
\v 7 Philip answered him, "Two hundred denarii worth of bread is not enough for them, for each to get a little \add [i.e. eight months' wages, and still only crumbs apiece]\add*."
\v 8 One of his disciples, Andrew the brother of Simon Peter, says to him,
\v 9 "There is a little boy here who has five barley loaves\f + \fr 6:9 \ft Barley is the poor man's grain, and the detail is only in this account. It puts the scene next to Elisha, who was brought twenty barley loaves, told his servant to feed a hundred men with them, heard the same objection — "What, shall I set this before a hundred men?" — and answered, "They shall eat and have some left over" (2 Kings 4:42-44). Elisha had twenty loaves for a hundred. Here there are five for five thousand.\f* and two dried fish; but what is that for so many?"
\v 10 Jesus said, "Have the men sit down." Now there was much grass in the place \add [i.e. spring grass, which fits the Passover season]\add*. So the men sat down, about five thousand in number.
\v 11 So Jesus took the loaves, and having given thanks he distributed them\f + \fr 6:11 \ft The words "to the disciples, and the disciples" are not in the critical text. Here Jesus hands out the bread himself.\f* to those reclining, and likewise of the dried fish, as much as they wanted.
\v 12 And when they were filled he says to his disciples, "Gather up the broken pieces left over, so that nothing is lost\f + \fr 6:12 \ft "Lost" is \tl apollymi\tl*, the same verb as "perish" in 3:16 and as "I should lose nothing" in verse 39 below. The instruction about bread scraps and the statement about the people the Father has given him use the same word.\f*."
\v 13 So they gathered them and filled twelve baskets with broken pieces from the five barley loaves, left over by those who had eaten \add [i.e. one basket for each tribe, and all of it surplus]\add*.
\v 14 So when the men saw the sign he did, they were saying, "This is truly the Prophet who is coming into the world \add [i.e. the prophet like Moses of Deuteronomy 18 — and Moses is the man who fed a nation in a wilderness]\add*."
\v 15 So Jesus, knowing that they were about to come and seize him to make him king, withdrew again to the mountain, himself alone \add [i.e. they had the right man and the wrong office, and the wrong idea of how he takes it]\add*.
\s1 On the Sea
\p
\v 16 And when evening came, his disciples went down to the sea,
\v 17 and getting into a boat they were going across the sea to Capernaum. And it had already become dark, and Jesus had not yet come to them,
\v 18 and the sea was rising because a great wind was blowing.
\v 19 So having rowed about twenty-five or thirty stadia \add [i.e. three or four miles out, in the middle of it]\add*, they see Jesus walking on the sea and coming near the boat, and they were afraid.
\v 20 But he says to them, "I am; do not be afraid\f + \fr 6:20 \ft Walking on the sea belongs to God: he "alone stretched out the heavens and walks on the sea as on firm ground" (Job 9:8); "your way was through the sea, your path through the great waters" (Psalm 77:19). And the pairing of "I am" with "do not be afraid" is Isaiah's formula for God speaking to his people in deep water: "Do not fear, for I am with you... when you pass through the waters, I will be with you" (Isaiah 43:1-5).\f*."
\v 21 So they were willing to take him into the boat, and immediately the boat was at the land they were going to.
\p
\v 22 The next day the crowd standing across the sea saw that there had been no other little boat there except one, and that Jesus had not gone into the boat with his disciples, but that his disciples had gone away alone.
\v 23 Other little boats came from Tiberias near the place where they ate the bread after the Lord had given thanks.
\v 24 So when the crowd saw that Jesus was not there, nor his disciples, they themselves got into the little boats and came to Capernaum looking for Jesus.
\v 25 And finding him across the sea they said to him, "Rabbi, when did you get here?"
\s1 Do Not Work for the Food That Perishes
\p
\v 26 Jesus answered them and said, "Amen, amen, I say to you, you are looking for me not because you saw signs, but because you ate of the loaves and were filled \add [i.e. you registered the meal and missed what the meal was pointing at]\add*.
\v 27 Do not work for the food that perishes, but for the food that remains into life of the age, which the son of man will give you; for on this one the Father, God, has set his seal\f + \fr 6:27 \ft A seal marked ownership and made a document genuine; the bearer of a seal acted with the authority of the one who sealed it. The manna, by design, perished — it bred worms if kept overnight (Exodus 16:19-20).\f*."
\v 28 So they said to him, "What shall we do, to work the works of God?"
\v 29 Jesus answered and said to them, "This is the work of God: that you trust in the one that one sent \add [i.e. the whole list collapses into one item, and it is not a performance]\add*."
\v 30 So they said to him, "What sign then do you do, so that we may see and trust you? What do you work?
\v 31 Our fathers ate the manna in the wilderness, as it is written, 'Bread out of heaven he gave them to eat.'"\f + \fr 6:31 \ft Psalm 78:24, with Exodus 16 behind it. The argument is a challenge: one meal for one crowd is not much beside forty years for a whole nation. Do better than Moses.\f*
\v 32 So Jesus said to them, "Amen, amen, I say to you, Moses did not give you the bread out of heaven; but my Father gives you the true bread out of heaven\f + \fr 6:32 \ft Three corrections in one sentence. Not Moses but the Father — Moses was the middleman, not the source. Not "gave" but "gives" — it is happening now, not back then. And not the manna but the "true" bread, the real thing the manna was a preview of.\f*.
\v 33 For the bread of God is the one coming down out of heaven and giving life to the world."
\v 34 So they said to him, "Lord, always give us this bread."
\s1 I Am the Bread of Life
\p
\v 35 Jesus said to them, "I am the bread of life\f + \fr 6:35 \ft The first of seven "I am" sayings with something added: bread of life, light of the world, door of the sheep, good shepherd, the rising and the life, the way and the truth and the life, the true vine. They asked for bread like the manna. He does not offer a supply; he says he is it.\f*. The one who comes to me will never hunger, and the one who trusts in me will never thirst, ever \add [i.e. as Isaiah promised: they shall not hunger or thirst (Isaiah 49:10)]\add*.
\v 36 But I said to you that you have even seen me, and you do not trust.
\v 37 Everything the Father gives me will come to me, and the one who comes to me I will never throw outside\f + \fr 6:37 \ft The Greek shifts number in mid-sentence. "Everything the Father gives" is neuter singular — the whole company, taken together. "The one who comes" is masculine singular — one person at a time. The two halves are the same reality from two sides, and neither cancels the other. The promise is about what "he" will not do: he will not be the one who throws you out.\f*,
\v 38 because I have come down from heaven not to do my own will but the will of the one who sent me.
\v 39 And this is the will of the one who sent me: that of everything he has given me I should lose nothing, but should raise it up on the last day.
\v 40 For this is the will of my Father: that everyone who watches the Son and trusts in him should have life of the age, and I will raise him up on the last day \add [i.e. the same will stated twice, once as the whole company given and once as the single person who looks and trusts]\add*."
\p
\v 41 So the Jews were grumbling\f + \fr 6:41 \ft Greek \tl gongyzō\tl*, which is the wilderness word. It is used again and again in the Greek Old Testament for Israel complaining against Moses and against God over exactly this — bread in a desert (Exodus 16:2, 7-9, 12; Numbers 14:27-29). They are re-enacting the scene they just quoted at him.\f* about him because he said, "I am the bread that came down out of heaven,"
\v 42 and they were saying, "Is this not Jesus the son of Joseph, whose father and mother we know? How does he now say, 'I have come down out of heaven'?"
\v 43 Jesus answered and said to them, "Do not grumble with one another.
\v 44 No one can come to me unless the Father who sent me draws\f + \fr 6:44 \ft Greek \tl helkyō\tl*, to draw or drag. Two things fix its sense here. The next verse says how the drawing is done: by teaching — "they will all be taught by God," and everyone who has heard and learned comes. And this writer uses the same verb again at 12:32, where the scope is stated: "I will draw all to myself." In the Greek Old Testament God says, "I have loved you with an everlasting love; therefore I have drawn you with lovingkindness" (Jeremiah 31:3).\f* him, and I will raise him up on the last day.
\v 45 It is written in the Prophets, 'And they will all be taught by God.'\f + \fr 6:45 \ft Isaiah 54:13, part of the promise of Jerusalem rebuilt and her children taught by God — the same stream as Jeremiah's new covenant, "they shall all know me, from the least of them to the greatest" (Jeremiah 31:34). The word "all" is in the text he quotes.\f* Everyone who has heard from the Father and learned comes to me.
\v 46 Not that anyone has seen the Father, except the one who is from God; this one has seen the Father.
\v 47 Amen, amen, I say to you, the one who trusts has life of the age\f + \fr 6:47 \ft The words "in me" after "trusts" are not in the critical text.\f*.
\v 48 I am the bread of life.
\v 49 Your fathers ate the manna in the wilderness, and they died \add [i.e. your own example proves my point: the bread you cite kept nobody alive]\add*.
\v 50 This is the bread coming down out of heaven, so that someone may eat of it and not die.
\v 51 I am the living bread that came down out of heaven. If anyone eats of this bread he will live into the age; and the bread that I will give is my flesh, for the life of the world \add [i.e. and it is given by being handed over to death]\add*."
\s1 Flesh to Eat and Blood to Drink
\p
\v 52 So the Jews were fighting with one another, saying, "How can this man give us his flesh to eat?"
\v 53 So Jesus said to them, "Amen, amen, I say to you, unless you eat the flesh of the son of man and drink his blood\f + \fr 6:53 \ft To a Jewish audience this is close to unspeakable. Drinking blood is forbidden outright and repeatedly: "You shall not eat the blood of any flesh, for the life of all flesh is its blood" (Leviticus 17:10-14; Genesis 9:4; Deuteronomy 12:23). But the reason the Torah gives for the ban is the key to the saying: the life is in the blood, and God gave the blood on the altar to make atonement. He is not licensing a violation of the law; he is claiming that his own life, poured out, is what the altar's blood was for. And the setting is Passover, where a lamb's blood shielded the house and its flesh was eaten that night.\f*, you do not have life in yourselves.
\v 54 The one who chews\f + \fr 6:54 \ft Greek \tl trōgō\tl*, which is not the ordinary word for eating. It means to gnaw or chew, and is used of animals feeding. Most English versions soften it to "eats." The writer has switched verbs deliberately at this point and keeps the harsher one for the rest of the discourse (6:56, 57, 58) and once more at 13:18. Whatever this means, it is not being made easier to swallow.\f* my flesh and drinks my blood has life of the age, and I will raise him up on the last day.
\v 55 For my flesh is true food, and my blood is true drink.
\v 56 The one who chews my flesh and drinks my blood remains in me, and I in him \add [i.e. the mutual staying that chapter 15 will spell out with a vine and its branches]\add*.
\v 57 Just as the living Father sent me and I live because of the Father, so also the one who chews me — that one will live because of me.
\v 58 This is the bread that came down out of heaven, not as the fathers ate and died. The one who chews this bread will live into the age."
\v 59 These things he said in a synagogue while teaching in Capernaum \add [i.e. in public, on the record, before the whole town]\add*.
\s1 Many of His Disciples Turn Back
\p
\v 60 So many of his disciples\f + \fr 6:60 \ft Not the Twelve and not hostile outsiders. \tl Mathētai\tl* here is the wider circle of people who had been following him and learning from him.\f*, when they heard, said, "This word is hard\f + \fr 6:60 \ft Greek \tl sklēros\tl*, harsh, rough, not "difficult to understand" but "intolerable." Their complaint is not that they cannot follow the argument.\f*. Who can listen to it?"
\v 61 But Jesus, knowing in himself that his disciples were grumbling about this, said to them, "Does this make you stumble?
\v 62 What then if you should watch the son of man going up where he was before?
\v 63 The Spirit is the one who makes alive; the flesh is no help at all. The words that I have spoken to you are spirit and are life\f + \fr 6:63 \ft This is the key to the whole discourse and it is not a retraction. Flesh on its own — taken apart from the Spirit — accomplishes nothing; that was already said of birth in 3:6. The Spirit is the life-giver, and the vehicle by which the Spirit gives it is these words. To eat this flesh and drink this blood is to receive, by the Spirit, the life that his death releases; and it is his words that carry it.\f*.
\v 64 But there are some of you who do not trust." For Jesus knew from the beginning who they were who did not trust and who it was who would hand him over.
\v 65 And he was saying, "For this reason I have told you that no one can come to me unless it has been given him from the Father."
\p
\v 66 From this many of his disciples went back to the things behind\f + \fr 6:66 \ft The phrase is the Greek Old Testament's language for apostasy: "they have turned away backward" (Isaiah 1:4); "they went backward and not forward" (Jeremiah 7:24). And "no longer walked with him" is an imperfect — they had been walking with him, and stopped. The writer does not describe them as men who were never really following. He says they were following and then were not.\f* and no longer walked with him.
\v 67 So Jesus said to the Twelve, "You do not want to go away too, do you?" \add [i.e. the door is open to them as well; he does not hold anyone]\add*
\v 68 Simon Peter answered him, "Lord, to whom shall we go away? You have the words of life of the age \add [i.e. not "we have understood you" but "there is nowhere else"]\add*,
\v 69 and we have trusted and have come to know that you are the Holy One of God\f + \fr 6:69 \ft The critical text reads "the Holy One of God"; the majority text and older English versions have "the Christ, the Son of the living God."\f*."
\v 70 Jesus answered them, "Did I not choose you, the Twelve? And one of you is a devil\f + \fr 6:70 \ft Greek \tl diabolos\tl*, slanderer, accuser — the word the Greek Old Testament uses for the \tl satan\tl*, the adversary (Job 1-2; Zechariah 3:1-2). He does not say Judas has one; he says Judas is one. And the same sentence says Jesus chose him. Israel too was chosen, and being chosen never made a man within Israel faithful.\f*."
\v 71 Now he was speaking of Judas son of Simon Iscariot; for this one, one of the Twelve, was going to hand him over.

\c 7
\s1 His Brothers Tell Him to Go Public
\p
\v 1 And after these things Jesus was walking in Galilee; for he did not want to walk in Judea, because the Jews were seeking to kill him \add [i.e. since the Sabbath healing at the pool]\add*.
\v 2 Now the feast of the Jews, the Tents, was near\f + \fr 7:2 \ft Sukkot, the autumn harvest feast, kept for seven days in shelters of branches in memory of the wilderness (Leviticus 23:33-43) — the most joyful of the year. Two of its ceremonies stand behind everything Jesus says in chapters 7 and 8. Each morning a priest carried water in a golden pitcher up from the pool of Siloam and poured it out at the altar while the crowd sang Isaiah's "With joy you will draw water from the wells of salvation" (Isaiah 12:3). And each night four huge lampstands were lit in the Court of the Women, so bright, the later record says, that every courtyard in Jerusalem was lit by them. Water and light. Zechariah 14 had promised both — living waters flowing out of Jerusalem, and all the nations coming up to keep this very feast.\f*.
\v 3 So his brothers said to him, "Move on from here and go into Judea, so that your disciples too may watch the works you do.
\v 4 For no one does anything in secret and seeks himself to be out in the open. If you do these things, make yourself visible to the world \add [i.e. if it is real, prove it where it counts]\add*."
\v 5 For not even his brothers were trusting in him.
\v 6 So Jesus says to them, "My time is not yet here; but your time is always ready \add [i.e. nothing you do is timed to anything, so you may come and go as you like]\add*.
\v 7 The world cannot hate you, but it hates me, because I testify about it that its works are evil \add [i.e. you are no threat to it; that is exactly the difference between us]\add*.
\v 8 You go up to the feast. I am not going up\f + \fr 7:8 \ft The critical text reads "I am not going up to this feast"; the majority text and most older English versions add a word: "I am not yet going up." Even as it stands the sense holds: he will not go up in the manner his brothers are urging — publicly, on their schedule, as a display.\f* to this feast, because my time has not yet been filled full."
\v 9 And having said these things, he stayed in Galilee.
\p
\v 10 But when his brothers had gone up to the feast, then he also went up — not in the open, but as it were in secret.
\v 11 So the Jews were looking for him at the feast and were saying, "Where is that one?"
\v 12 And there was much muttering about him in the crowds. Some were saying, "He is good"; but others were saying, "No, he leads the crowd astray\f + \fr 7:12 \ft Not a mild complaint. Leading Israel astray is a capital charge under Deuteronomy 13:1-5, which prescribes death for the prophet who draws the people after other gods.\f*."
\v 13 Yet no one spoke openly about him, for fear of the Jews.
\s1 Teaching in the Middle of the Feast
\p
\v 14 But when the feast was already half over, Jesus went up into the temple and was teaching.
\v 15 So the Jews were amazed, saying, "How does this man know letters, not having been taught?"\f + \fr 7:15 \ft Their astonishment is about credentials. He has studied under no teacher they recognize, so by what right does he expound in the temple?\f*
\v 16 So Jesus answered them and said, "My teaching is not mine, but of the one who sent me \add [i.e. you are asking which school; the answer is not a school]\add*.
\v 17 If anyone wants to do his will, he will know about the teaching, whether it is from God or I am speaking from myself\f + \fr 7:17 \ft The test offered is not intellectual but a matter of the will. Willingness to do what God wants is what makes it possible to recognize what God says. It follows that failure to recognize it is not ignorance but refusal — which is the diagnosis this book keeps making (3:19-20; 5:40).\f*.
\v 18 The one who speaks from himself seeks his own glory; but the one who seeks the glory of the one who sent him, this one is true, and there is no unrighteousness in him.
\v 19 Did not Moses give you the Torah? And not one of you does the Torah. Why are you seeking to kill me?"\f + \fr 7:19 \ft He is answering a charge about a Sabbath, brought by men who are planning a killing.\f*
\v 20 The crowd answered, "You have a demon. Who is seeking to kill you?"\f + \fr 7:20 \ft The pilgrim crowd is not in on it. The intention belongs to the authorities (5:18; 7:1), and the Jerusalemites in verse 25 know all about it.\f*
\v 21 Jesus answered and said to them, "One work I did, and you are all amazed \add [i.e. the man at the pool, months ago, and it is still the sore point]\add*.
\v 22 For this reason Moses has given you circumcision — not that it is from Moses, but from the fathers\f + \fr 7:22 \ft Circumcision goes back to Abraham (Genesis 17:10-14), before Sinai; Moses incorporated it. The eighth day is fixed, so when it falls on a Sabbath the circumcision is performed anyway. Nobody disputed this.\f* — and on a Sabbath you circumcise a man.
\v 23 If a man receives circumcision on a Sabbath so that the Torah of Moses may not be broken, are you angry with me because I made a whole man well on a Sabbath?\f + \fr 7:23 \ft The argument runs from the lesser case to the greater, the standard form of rabbinic reasoning. If one part of a man may be dealt with on the Sabbath so that the law is kept, how much more the whole of him. It is not an attack on the Sabbath but a reading of it from inside their own rules.\f*
\v 24 Do not judge by appearance, but judge the righteous judgment \add [i.e. Isaiah said the coming king would judge exactly so — not by what his eyes see (Isaiah 11:3-4)]\add*."
\s1 They Think They Know Where He Is From
\p
\v 25 So some of the Jerusalemites were saying, "Is this not the one they are seeking to kill?
\v 26 And look — he is speaking openly, and they say nothing to him. Can it be that the rulers have truly come to know that this is the Christ?
\v 27 But we know where this one is from; and when the Christ comes, no one knows where he is from\f + \fr 7:27 \ft One strand of expectation held that the Messiah would be hidden until he suddenly appeared, his origins unknown. The irony runs both ways: they are wrong to think knowing Nazareth settles the question, and right without meaning it that no one knows where he is from.\f*."
\v 28 So Jesus cried out in the temple, teaching and saying, "You both know me and know where I am from \add [i.e. and it has told you nothing]\add*. And I have not come from myself, but the one who sent me is true, whom you do not know.
\v 29 I know him, because I am from him, and that one sent me."
\v 30 So they were seeking to seize him, and no one laid a hand on him, because his hour had not yet come.
\v 31 But many of the crowd trusted in him, and they were saying, "When the Christ comes, he will not do more signs than this one has done, will he?"
\p
\v 32 The Pharisees heard the crowd muttering these things about him, and the chief priests and the Pharisees sent officers to seize him \add [i.e. the temple police, acting for a coalition of two parties that normally cannot agree]\add*.
\v 33 So Jesus said, "Yet a little time I am with you, and then I go away to the one who sent me.
\v 34 You will look for me and you will not find me, and where I am you cannot come\f + \fr 7:34 \ft Wisdom says the same to those who refused her: "Then they will call upon me, but I will not answer; they will seek me diligently but will not find me" (Proverbs 1:28).\f*."
\v 35 So the Jews said to one another, "Where is this one about to go, that we will not find him? He is not about to go to the Dispersion among the Greeks and teach the Greeks, is he?\f + \fr 7:35 \ft They mean it as an absurdity. It is what happened.\f*
\v 36 What is this word he said, 'You will look for me and you will not find me, and where I am you cannot come'?"
\s1 Rivers of Living Water
\p
\v 37 And on the last day, the great day of the feast\f + \fr 7:37 \ft The climax of the water ceremony, when the priests circled the altar seven times. He stands up in the middle of it and offers what the ritual has been asking for.\f*, Jesus stood and cried out, saying, "If anyone thirsts, let him come to me and drink.
\v 38 The one who trusts in me — as the Scripture said, 'Out of his belly\f + \fr 7:38 \ft The Greek does not say whose belly, and it is left open here; some versions resolve it to the believer, some to Christ. Nor does any one text read exactly as quoted. What is being drawn on is a whole seam: the river running out from under the temple, deepening as it goes and making everything it touches live (Ezekiel 47:1-12); the living waters flowing out of Jerusalem (Zechariah 14:8); the water struck from the rock in the wilderness (Psalm 78:15-16).\f* will flow rivers of living water.'"\f + \fr 7:37-38 \ft The sentence can be punctuated two ways, and the Greek does not decide. It is broken here after "drink," so that the promise attaches to "the one who trusts in me." The other way joins the drinking to the trusting: "let him come to me, and let the one who trusts in me drink."\f*
\v 39 Now he said this about the Spirit, whom those who trusted in him were about to receive; for as yet there was no Spirit\f + \fr 7:39 \ft The Greek says flatly "for as yet there was no Spirit." Most English versions supply a word — "the Spirit had not yet been given" — which is the sense: not that the Spirit did not exist, but that the Spirit had not yet been poured out in the way just promised. And the condition for it is named: the glorification, which in this book means the cross.\f*, because Jesus had not yet been glorified.
\p
\v 40 So some of the crowd, hearing these words, were saying, "This is truly the Prophet";
\v 41 others were saying, "This is the Christ." But others were saying, "Surely the Christ does not come out of Galilee, does he?
\v 42 Did not the Scripture say that the Christ comes out of the seed of David, and from Bethlehem, the village where David was?\f + \fr 7:42 \ft Micah 5:2. The objection has an answer, and this writer never gives it — he never mentions Bethlehem at all. He has been arguing since the first sentence that the question of where Jesus is from is not answered by a birthplace.\f*"
\v 43 So a split came about in the crowd because of him.
\v 44 And some of them wanted to seize him, but no one laid hands on him.
\p
\v 45 So the officers came to the chief priests and Pharisees, and those said to them, "Why did you not bring him?"
\v 46 The officers answered, "Never has a man spoken like this \add [i.e. from the arresting party, who had no theological stake and could not explain themselves either]\add*."
\v 47 So the Pharisees answered them, "Have you also been led astray?
\v 48 Has any one of the rulers trusted in him, or any of the Pharisees?\f + \fr 7:48 \ft The argument from headcount among the qualified. It is about to be answered from the floor by one of their own, and the reader already knows about Nicodemus.\f*
\v 49 But this crowd, which does not know the Torah — they are cursed \add [i.e. the common people, who do not keep the detail we keep]\add*."
\v 50 Nicodemus says to them — the one who had come to him before, being one of them —
\v 51 "Our Torah does not judge a man unless it first hears from him and knows what he is doing, does it?\f + \fr 7:51 \ft He does not defend Jesus; he defends procedure, which the Torah requires (Deuteronomy 1:16-17; 17:4). It is a small, careful step out of the dark, and it costs him at once.\f*"
\v 52 They answered and said to him, "You are not from Galilee too, are you? Search and see that no prophet is raised up out of Galilee\f + \fr 7:52 \ft It is not true. Jonah was from Gath-hepher in Galilee (2 Kings 14:25). They tell Nicodemus to search while showing that they have not.\f*."
\p
\v 53 And they went, each to his own house.\f + \fr 7:53 \ft From here through 8:11 — the woman caught in adultery — the passage is absent from the oldest copies of this book. Manuscripts that carry it often mark it as doubtful, and some put it elsewhere: after Luke 21:38, or at another point in this chapter, or at the end of the book. Its vocabulary is unlike this writer's. The critical text prints it inside double brackets. It is set out here as text, in its traditional place, on the same footing as the longer ending of Mark.\f*

\c 8
\s1 The Woman Caught in Adultery
\p
\v 1 But Jesus went to the Mount of Olives.
\v 2 And at dawn he came again into the temple, and all the people were coming to him; and sitting down he was teaching them.
\v 3 And the scribes and the Pharisees bring a woman caught in adultery, and standing her in the middle
\v 4 they say to him, "Teacher, this woman has been caught in the very act of committing adultery.
\v 5 Now in the Torah Moses commanded us to stone such women\f + \fr 8:5 \ft Leviticus 20:10 and Deuteronomy 22:22 require death for adultery — and for both parties. She was caught in the act, so the man was there. He is not.\f*. So what do you say?"
\v 6 They were saying this to test him, so that they might have something to accuse him of \add [i.e. say stone her and he defies Rome, which alone may execute; say release her and he defies Moses]\add*. But Jesus bent down and was writing with his finger on the ground.
\v 7 And when they kept on asking him, he straightened up and said to them, "Let the one among you without sin be the first to throw a stone at her\f + \fr 8:7 \ft He does not set the law aside; he insists on all of it. The witnesses had to cast the first stones (Deuteronomy 17:7), and their own conduct had to bear examination (Deuteronomy 19:15-21).\f*."
\v 8 And again he bent down and was writing on the ground.
\v 9 And those who heard went out one by one, beginning with the elders, and he was left alone — and the woman, in the middle.
\v 10 And Jesus straightened up and said to her, "Woman, where are they? Did no one condemn you?"
\v 11 And she said, "No one, Lord." And Jesus said, "Neither do I condemn you. Go, and from now on sin no more \add [i.e. no condemnation, and no pretence that nothing happened]\add*."
\s1 I Am the Light of the World
\p
\v 12 So Jesus spoke to them again, saying, "I am the light of the world\f + \fr 8:12 \ft Spoken in the temple during the feast whose nights were lit by the four great lampstands, and, as verse 20 notes, in the treasury — which stood in the Court of the Women, where those lampstands burned. The lamps commemorated the pillar of fire that led Israel by night through the wilderness (Exodus 13:21). And light was what God had promised for the last day: "Arise, shine, for your light has come... nations shall come to your light" (Isaiah 60:1-3).\f*. The one who follows me will never walk in the darkness, but will have the light of life."
\v 13 So the Pharisees said to him, "You are testifying about yourself; your testimony is not true \add [i.e. by your own admission earlier, one witness is not enough]\add*."
\v 14 Jesus answered and said to them, "Even if I testify about myself, my testimony is true, because I know where I came from and where I am going. But you do not know where I come from or where I am going.
\v 15 You judge according to the flesh; I judge no one \add [i.e. I do not judge as you do, by what is on the surface]\add*.
\v 16 And even if I do judge, my judgment is true, because I am not alone, but I and the Father who sent me.
\v 17 And in your own Torah it is written that the testimony of two men is true.
\v 18 I am the one testifying about myself, and the Father who sent me testifies about me \add [i.e. there are your two witnesses, and you cannot summon the second one into a courtroom]\add*."
\v 19 So they were saying to him, "Where is your father?" Jesus answered, "You know neither me nor my Father. If you knew me, you would know my Father also."
\v 20 These words he spoke in the treasury, teaching in the temple; and no one seized him, because his hour had not yet come.
\s1 You Will Die in Your Sin
\p
\v 21 So he said to them again, "I am going away, and you will look for me, and you will die in your sin. Where I am going, you cannot come."
\v 22 So the Jews were saying, "Will he kill himself, since he says, 'Where I am going, you cannot come'?"\f + \fr 8:22 \ft A sneer: suicide would take him where no one would follow. He will die, and not by his own hand, and where he is going is to the Father.\f*
\v 23 And he was saying to them, "You are from below; I am from above. You are of this world; I am not of this world.
\v 24 So I said to you that you will die in your sins; for unless you trust that I am, you will die in your sins\f + \fr 8:24 \ft "I am" with nothing added — \tl egō eimi\tl*. In the Greek Old Testament this is God's own way of naming himself: "I am the one who is" (Exodus 3:14); "so that you may know and believe me and understand that I am he" (Isaiah 43:10); "I, I am he who blots out your transgressions" (Isaiah 43:25); "see now that I am he, and there is no god beside me" (Deuteronomy 32:39). Most English versions add "he" to smooth it. It is left bare here, as the Greek leaves it, and it recurs bare at 8:28, 8:58, and in the garden at 18:5.\f*."
\v 25 So they were saying to him, "Who are you?" Jesus said to them, "What I have been telling you from the beginning\f + \fr 8:25 \ft One of the hardest clauses in the book. \tl Tēn archēn ho ti kai lalō hymin\tl* is rendered here as a statement: I have been saying it from the start. It can also be read as a retort — "Why do I speak to you at all?" Either way the force is the same: nothing has been withheld from them.\f*.
\v 26 I have many things to say and to judge about you; but the one who sent me is true, and what I heard from him, these things I speak to the world."
\v 27 They did not know that he was speaking to them of the Father.
\v 28 So Jesus said, "When you lift up the son of man, then you will know that I am, and that I do nothing from myself, but as the Father taught me, these things I speak \add [i.e. the thing you do to be rid of me is the thing that will show you who I am]\add*.
\v 29 And the one who sent me is with me; he has not left me alone, because I always do the things pleasing to him."
\v 30 As he was saying these things, many trusted in him.
\s1 The Truth Will Set You Free
\p
\v 31 So Jesus was saying to the Jews who had trusted him, "If you remain in my word\f + \fr 8:31 \ft "Remain" is \tl menō\tl*, the verb that will govern chapter 15. The condition of being a disciple in truth is not the moment of belief but the staying. Read what follows with an eye on these same men: within one conversation they will pick up stones.\f*, you are truly my disciples,
\v 32 and you will know the truth, and the truth will set you free."
\v 33 They answered him, "We are seed of Abraham and have never been slaves to anyone\f + \fr 8:33 \ft Four hundred years in Egypt, the exile in Babylon, Persia, Greece, the Seleucids, and a Roman garrison in the fortress overlooking the temple as they speak. Whether they mean that no power ever owned them inwardly or simply are not counting, the claim is that they have no need of what he is offering.\f*. How do you say, 'You will become free'?"
\v 34 Jesus answered them, "Amen, amen, I say to you, everyone who does sin is a slave of sin \add [i.e. the bondage I mean is not political, and you are in it]\add*.
\v 35 And the slave does not remain in the house into the age; the son remains into the age\f + \fr 8:35 \ft The household picture is Abraham's own: the slave woman's son was sent away, and Isaac remained (Genesis 21:10). Lineage from Abraham does not by itself secure a permanent place in the house.\f*.
\v 36 So if the Son sets you free, you will really be free.
\v 37 I know that you are seed of Abraham; but you are seeking to kill me, because my word makes no room in you \add [i.e. it arrives and finds nowhere to lodge]\add*.
\v 38 What I have seen with the Father I speak; so you also do what you heard from the father\f + \fr 8:38 \ft The critical text reads "from the father"; most English versions have "from your father." As it stands the identification is left hanging, and the reader is made to wait for verse 44.\f*."
\s1 Whose Children You Are
\p
\v 39 They answered and said to him, "Our father is Abraham." Jesus says to them, "If you are children of Abraham, do the works of Abraham \add [i.e. descent is a fact about blood; children is a claim about likeness]\add*.
\v 40 But now you are seeking to kill me, a man who has spoken to you the truth that I heard from God. This Abraham did not do \add [i.e. when God's messengers came to Abraham he fed them]\add*.
\v 41 You do the works of your father." They said to him, "We were not born of sexual immorality; we have one Father — God."
\v 42 Jesus said to them, "If God were your Father, you would love me, for I came out from God and am here. For I have not come from myself, but that one sent me.
\v 43 Why do you not know my way of speaking? Because you cannot hear my word \add [i.e. the failure is not in your ears; it is in what you are aligned with]\add*.
\v 44 You are from the father, the devil, and you want to do the cravings of your father. That one was a murderer from the beginning\f + \fr 8:44 \ft Greek \tl anthrōpoktonos\tl*, man-killer, a rare word. "From the beginning" reaches back to the garden, where a lie brought death in, and to Cain, the first murder — an act a later letter in this same tradition attributes to "the evil one" (1 John 3:12). "Through the devil's envy death entered the world" (Wisdom 2:24). Men plotting a murder are told whose work they are doing.\f*, and he has not stood in the truth, because there is no truth in him. When he speaks the lie, he speaks out of his own, because he is a liar and the father of it\f + \fr 8:44 \ft The Greek leaves open whether he is the father of the lie or the father of the liar, and it is left open here.\f*.
\v 45 But because I speak the truth, you do not trust me \add [i.e. not in spite of it — because of it]\add*.
\v 46 Which of you convicts me of sin? If I speak truth, why do you not trust me?
\v 47 The one who is of God hears the words of God. For this reason you do not hear: because you are not of God \add [i.e. a diagnosis read off what they are doing, not a sentence passed before they did it]\add*."
\s1 Before Abraham Came to Be, I Am
\p
\v 48 The Jews answered and said to him, "Do we not say well that you are a Samaritan and have a demon?"\f + \fr 8:48 \ft Two insults at once — an outsider to the covenant, and a man out of his mind. He answers only the second.\f*
\v 49 Jesus answered, "I do not have a demon, but I honor my Father, and you dishonor me.
\v 50 But I do not seek my own glory; there is one who seeks it, and he judges.
\v 51 Amen, amen, I say to you, if anyone keeps my word, he will never see death into the age\f + \fr 8:51 \ft Not a promise that the body will not die — this writer knows disciples die, and deals with the point at 21:23. It is the death of 5:24, the state out of which the one who hears has already crossed over.\f*."
\v 52 The Jews said to him, "Now we know that you have a demon. Abraham died, and the prophets, and you say, 'If anyone keeps my word, he will never taste death into the age.'
\v 53 You are not greater than our father Abraham, are you, who died? And the prophets died. Whom do you make yourself out to be?"
\v 54 Jesus answered, "If I glorify myself, my glory is nothing. It is my Father who glorifies me, of whom you say that he is your God.
\v 55 And you have not known him, but I know him. And if I said that I do not know him, I would be like you, a liar. But I know him, and I keep his word.
\v 56 Abraham your father was glad that he would see my day; and he saw it and rejoiced\f + \fr 8:56 \ft What Abraham saw is not specified. God had told him that in him all the nations of the earth would be blessed and did not hide his purposes from him (Genesis 18:17-18); he believed a promise about a future he could not live to see (Genesis 15:6); and he received his only son back from the altar. Jewish tradition of the period speaks of Abraham being shown what was coming.\f*."
\v 57 So the Jews said to him, "You are not yet fifty years old, and you have seen Abraham?"
\v 58 Jesus said to them, "Amen, amen, I say to you, before Abraham came to be, I am\f + \fr 8:58 \ft Two different verbs, and everything is in the difference. Abraham "came to be" — the verb used of everything that was made (1:3). Jesus does not say "I was before Abraham," which would only claim to be older. He says "I am," present and without limit, the words God speaks over himself at the bush and through Isaiah (Exodus 3:14; Isaiah 43:10). His hearers do not misunderstand him. They reach for stones, which is the penalty for blasphemy (Leviticus 24:16).\f*."
\v 59 So they picked up stones to throw at him; but Jesus was hidden and went out of the temple\f + \fr 8:59 \ft The words "going through the midst of them, and so passed by" at the end of this verse are not in the critical text.\f*.

\c 9
\s1 A Man Blind from Birth
\p
\v 1 And as he was passing by, he saw a man blind from birth \add [i.e. not a man who lost his sight, but a man who never had it]\add*.
\v 2 And his disciples asked him, saying, "Rabbi, who sinned, this man or his parents, that he was born blind?\f + \fr 9:2 \ft The assumption behind the question was standard and had scriptural warrant of a sort — God visiting the iniquity of the fathers on the children (Exodus 20:5), the catalogue of afflictions that follow disobedience (Deuteronomy 28). The odd half of the question is the first half: how could a man sin before he was born?\f*"
\v 3 Jesus answered, "Neither this man sinned nor his parents, but it was so that the works of God might be made visible in him \add [i.e. this is not a punishment being explained; it is an occasion about to be used]\add*.
\v 4 We must work\f + \fr 9:4 \ft The critical text reads "We must work the works of him who sent me"; the majority text and older English versions have "I must work." The harder reading, kept here, draws the disciples into the work: it is not his errand alone.\f* the works of the one who sent me while it is day; night is coming, when no one can work.
\v 5 While I am in the world, I am the light of the world \add [i.e. the claim made in the temple two days ago is about to be demonstrated on a man who has never seen anything]\add*."
\v 6 Having said these things, he spat on the ground and made mud out of the spittle\f + \fr 9:6 \ft Making mud is kneading, one of the categories of work forbidden on the Sabbath. He healed the man at the pool with a word alone; here he chooses a method that cannot be mistaken for anything but labor. The Sabbath collision is not accidental. And the material is the material of Genesis: God "formed the man of dust from the ground" (Genesis 2:7); "you fashioned me like clay" (Job 10:9); "we are the clay, and you are our potter" (Isaiah 64:8). Eyes that never existed are not being repaired. They are being made.\f*, and he smeared the mud on his eyes
\v 7 and said to him, "Go, wash in the pool of Siloam" (which is translated, Sent)\f + \fr 9:7 \ft The Hebrew name \tl shiloach\tl* comes from the verb "to send," and the writer stops to say so — because "the one sent" is his standing description of Jesus, used more often than any other. The blind man is sent to wash in Sent. This is also the pool the priests drew from each morning of the feast just ended, for the water poured out at the altar.\f*. So he went off and washed, and he came seeing.
\p
\v 8 So the neighbors and those who had watched him before, that he was a beggar, were saying, "Is this not the one who sat and begged?"
\v 9 Some were saying, "This is he"; others were saying, "No, but he is like him." That one was saying, "I am he."
\v 10 So they were saying to him, "How then were your eyes opened?"
\v 11 That one answered, "The man called Jesus\f + \fr 9:11 \ft Watch how far he gets. Here Jesus is "the man called Jesus." At verse 17 he is "a prophet." At verse 33, "from God." At verse 38, "Lord," and the man is on his face. Every step is forced out of him by another round of questioning.\f* made mud and smeared my eyes and said to me, 'Go to Siloam and wash.' So I went off and washed and received sight."
\v 12 And they said to him, "Where is that one?" He says, "I do not know."
\s1 The Pharisees Investigate
\p
\v 13 They bring him to the Pharisees — the man once blind.
\v 14 Now it was a Sabbath on the day Jesus made the mud and opened his eyes.
\v 15 So again the Pharisees also were asking him how he received sight. And he said to them, "He put mud on my eyes, and I washed, and I see \add [i.e. three facts and no theology]\add*."
\v 16 So some of the Pharisees were saying, "This man is not from God, because he does not keep the Sabbath." But others were saying, "How can a sinful man do such signs?" And there was a split among them \add [i.e. one group reasons from the rule down, the other from the evidence up]\add*.
\v 17 So they say to the blind man again, "What do you say about him, since he opened your eyes?" And he said, "He is a prophet \add [i.e. the beggar is asked for a ruling because the experts cannot agree]\add*."
\p
\v 18 So the Jews did not trust about him that he had been blind and had received sight, until they called the parents of the one who had received sight \add [i.e. their first move is not to explain the sign but to disprove it]\add*
\v 19 and asked them, saying, "Is this your son, whom you say was born blind? How then does he now see?"
\v 20 So his parents answered and said, "We know that this is our son and that he was born blind.
\v 21 But how he now sees we do not know, or who opened his eyes we do not know. Ask him; he is of age. He will speak for himself \add [i.e. they confirm the facts and refuse the conclusion]\add*."
\v 22 His parents said these things because they were afraid of the Jews; for the Jews had already agreed that if anyone confessed him as Christ, he would be put out of the synagogue\f + \fr 9:22 \ft Greek \tl aposynagōgos\tl*, a word found nowhere in the New Testament outside this book (here, 12:42, 16:2). It is not exclusion from a service. The synagogue was the community — its court, its trade, its marriages, its burials — and to be put out of it was to lose the whole of one's life among one's people. Notice what is punished: not believing but "confessing". They cannot police what a man thinks; they can police what he says.\f*.
\v 23 For this reason his parents said, "He is of age; question him."
\p
\v 24 So a second time they called the man who had been blind and said to him, "Give glory to God\f + \fr 9:24 \ft Not an invitation to praise. It is the formula for putting a man on oath, as Joshua does to Achan: "My son, give glory to the LORD the God of Israel, and make confession to him; tell me now what you have done" (Joshua 7:19). They are demanding a retraction under oath.\f*. We know that this man is a sinner."
\v 25 So that one answered, "Whether he is a sinner I do not know. One thing I do know: that being blind, now I see \add [i.e. you may keep your verdict; you cannot have my eyes]\add*."
\v 26 So they said to him, "What did he do to you? How did he open your eyes?"
\v 27 He answered them, "I told you already and you did not hear. Why do you want to hear it again? You do not want to become his disciples too, do you?"\f + \fr 9:27 \ft The word is "too." Whether he means it or it escapes him under pressure, he has just placed himself on the other side.\f*
\v 28 And they reviled him and said, "You are that one's disciple; we are Moses' disciples\f + \fr 9:28 \ft The claim has already been answered in advance: "If you trusted Moses, you would trust me, for that one wrote about me" (5:46).\f*.
\v 29 We know that God has spoken to Moses; but this one — we do not know where he is from."
\v 30 The man answered and said to them, "Well, this is the amazing thing: that you do not know where he is from, and he opened my eyes \add [i.e. you are the men whose whole office is telling where a thing is from]\add*.
\v 31 We know that God does not hear sinners; but if anyone is God-fearing and does his will, this one he hears\f + \fr 9:31 \ft He argues from a principle they hold and cannot deny: "The LORD is far from the wicked, but he hears the prayer of the righteous" (Proverbs 15:29; compare Psalm 66:18; Isaiah 1:15).\f*.
\v 32 From of old it has not been heard that anyone opened the eyes of a man born blind \add [i.e. no prophet in the whole record ever did this one]\add*.
\v 33 If this one were not from God, he could do nothing."
\v 34 They answered and said to him, "You were born entirely in sins, and you are teaching us?\f + \fr 9:34 \ft They fall back on exactly the explanation Jesus ruled out in verse 3. Having no answer to the argument, they attack the man's birth and throw him out.\f*" And they threw him outside.
\s1 Jesus Finds Him
\p
\v 35 Jesus heard that they had thrown him outside, and finding him he said, "Do you trust in the son of man?\f + \fr 9:35 \ft The critical text reads "the son of man"; the majority text and older English versions have "the Son of God."\f*"
\v 36 That one answered and said, "And who is he, sir, so that I may trust in him?"\f + \fr 9:36 \ft He does not ask why he should trust; only who it is. The willingness is already there.\f*
\v 37 Jesus said to him, "You have both seen him, and the one speaking with you is that one \add [i.e. and these are the first eyes that ever looked at him]\add*."
\v 38 And he said, "I trust, Lord," and he bowed down before him\f + \fr 9:38 \ft The verb is \tl proskyneō\tl*, the word for the worship owed to God alone. When a man falls at Peter's feet, Peter pulls him up (Acts 10:25-26); when the seer falls before an angel, the angel forbids it (Revelation 19:10). Here nothing is corrected.\f*.
\v 39 And Jesus said, "For judgment I came into this world, so that those who do not see may see, and those who see may become blind\f + \fr 9:39 \ft Set beside 3:17, "God did not send the Son into the world to judge the world." Both are true in the way this book uses the word. He does not come to condemn; but light arriving in a dark room is a judgment whether or not it intends one, because it sorts those who come to it from those who back away (3:19-21).\f*."
\v 40 Some of the Pharisees who were with him heard these things and said to him, "Are we blind too?"
\v 41 Jesus said to them, "If you were blind, you would not have sin; but now you say, 'We see' — your sin remains.

\c 10
\s1 The Shepherd, the Thief, and the Voice
\p
\v 1 "Amen, amen, I say to you, the one who does not enter through the door into the sheepfold, but climbs in another way, that one is a thief and a robber\f + \fr 10:1 \ft There is no scene break here. He is still speaking to the Pharisees who have just thrown the healed man out of the synagogue, and what follows tells them what that act revealed about them. Behind it stands Ezekiel 34, God's indictment of Israel's shepherds: "Woe to the shepherds of Israel who have been feeding themselves... the sick you have not strengthened, the injured you have not bound up, the strayed you have not brought back, and with force and harshness you have ruled them" (Ezekiel 34:2-4). God's answer there is twofold: "I myself will search for my sheep" (34:11), and "I will set up over them one shepherd, my servant David" (34:23).\f*.
\v 2 But the one who enters through the door is the shepherd of the sheep.
\v 3 To this one the doorkeeper opens, and the sheep hear his voice, and he calls his own sheep by name and leads them out\f + \fr 10:3 \ft Several flocks might share one walled fold overnight; in the morning each shepherd called and only his own came. Recognition runs by ear, not by sight. "Leads them out" uses the exodus verb — the word for God bringing Israel out of Egypt.\f*.
\v 4 When he has put out all his own, he goes ahead of them, and the sheep follow him, because they know his voice \add [i.e. eastern shepherding: he walks in front and they come after; nobody is driven]\add*.
\v 5 But a stranger they will never follow; instead they will run away from him, because they do not know the voice of strangers \add [i.e. which is exactly what the man born blind just did]\add*."
\v 6 This figure Jesus spoke to them, but those did not know what the things were that he was speaking to them \add [i.e. they cannot see themselves in it, which is itself the point]\add*.
\p
\v 7 So Jesus said again, "Amen, amen, I say to you, I am the door of the sheep\f + \fr 10:7 \ft A shepherd would often sleep across the entrance of the fold, his own body the barrier between the flock and whatever came in the night. The door is both the way in and the thing that keeps out.\f*.
\v 8 All who came before me are thieves and robbers, but the sheep did not hear them \add [i.e. not Moses and the prophets, but everyone who has set himself over this flock without being sent to it]\add*.
\v 9 I am the door. If anyone enters through me, he will be rescued, and will go in and go out and find pasture\f + \fr 10:9 \ft "Going in and going out" is the Hebrew way of saying the whole of daily life lived without hindrance (Deuteronomy 28:6; Psalm 121:8) — the opposite of being shut out of a synagogue. And "pasture" is Ezekiel's word: "I will feed them in good pasture" (Ezekiel 34:14).\f*.
\v 10 The thief does not come except to steal and slaughter and destroy. I came so that they may have life, and have it in abundance.
\s1 I Am the Good Shepherd
\p
\v 11 "I am the good shepherd\f + \fr 10:11 \ft "Good" here is \tl kalos\tl* — not merely competent but the real thing, the shepherd as a shepherd ought to be. Ezekiel promised both that God would shepherd the flock himself and that he would set one shepherd over them. Verse 30 will say how both can be true at once.\f*. The good shepherd lays down his life for the sheep.
\v 12 The hired man, who is not a shepherd, whose own the sheep are not, watches the wolf coming and leaves the sheep and runs away — and the wolf snatches them and scatters them\f + \fr 10:12 \ft In the prophets the wolves are the rulers: "her officials within her are like wolves tearing the prey" (Ezekiel 22:27; Zephaniah 3:3). Snatching and scattering are precisely what the men in front of him have just done to a man who could not be made to deny what he knew.\f* —
\v 13 because he is a hired man and the sheep are no concern to him.
\v 14 I am the good shepherd, and I know my own and my own know me,
\v 15 just as the Father knows me and I know the Father; and I lay down my life for the sheep \add [i.e. the closeness between shepherd and sheep is measured against the closeness between Father and Son]\add*.
\v 16 And I have other sheep that are not out of this fold; those also I must lead, and they will hear my voice, and they will become one flock\f + \fr 10:16 \ft Two different words. The \tl aulē\tl*, the walled fold, is the enclosure; the \tl poimnē\tl* is the flock itself. Older English versions have "one fold," which makes it a matter of pens. It is not: the sheep from outside are not moved into this enclosure, they are made one flock under one shepherd — which is Ezekiel's word for the reunited people, "one shepherd over them all" (Ezekiel 37:24).\f*, one shepherd.
\v 17 For this reason the Father loves me: because I lay down my life, so that I may take it up again.
\v 18 No one takes it away from me, but I lay it down of myself. I have authority to lay it down, and I have authority to take it up again. This commandment I received from my Father\f + \fr 10:18 \ft Both halves stand together: it is entirely voluntary and it is commanded. Read the arrest and the trial with this sentence in hand. Nothing that is about to be done to him is being done "to" him.\f*."
\p
\v 19 A split came about again among the Jews because of these words.
\v 20 And many of them were saying, "He has a demon and is out of his mind. Why do you listen to him?"
\v 21 Others were saying, "These are not the words of one who has a demon. A demon cannot open the eyes of the blind, can it?"
\s1 At the Feast of Dedication
\p
\v 22 At that time the Dedication took place in Jerusalem. It was winter\f + \fr 10:22 \ft Hanukkah, kept for eight days in midwinter, celebrating the rededication of the temple in 164 BC after Antiochus had defiled it — the altar cleansed, the worship restored, the illegitimate ruler thrown off (1 Maccabees 4:36-59). Josephus calls it the festival of lights. Keep the occasion in view: the whole exchange that follows is about consecration (verse 36 uses the word) and about who has the right to rule this flock.\f*,
\v 23 and Jesus was walking in the temple, in Solomon's colonnade \add [i.e. the covered walk along the east side, held to be a remnant of the first temple]\add*.
\v 24 So the Jews surrounded him and were saying to him, "How long will you hold our life in suspense? If you are the Christ, tell us plainly."
\v 25 Jesus answered them, "I told you and you do not trust. The works that I do in my Father's name — these testify about me \add [i.e. the declaration you are asking for has already been made, in what you watched me do]\add*.
\v 26 But you do not trust, because you are not of my sheep \add [i.e. read off their behavior, not decreed over them in advance — the sheep are the ones who hear and follow, and these do neither]\add*.
\v 27 My sheep hear my voice, and I know them, and they follow me,
\v 28 and I give them life of the age, and they will never be destroyed into the age, and no one will snatch them out of my hand\f + \fr 10:28 \ft "Snatch" is the wolf's word from verse 12, and that fixes what is being promised: no outside power can tear them away. The hand is God's own idiom for saving power — "we are the people of his pasture, and the sheep of his hand" (Psalm 95:7). What the sentence addresses is predators. It does not speak to the sheep that wanders off, and this book has already shown disciples doing exactly that (6:66).\f*.
\v 29 What my Father has given me is greater than all\f + \fr 10:29 \ft The critical text has a neuter: "what my Father has given me is greater than all." Most English versions read a masculine, "my Father, who has given them to me, is greater than all."\f*, and no one can snatch out of the Father's hand.
\v 30 I and the Father are one\f + \fr 10:30 \ft "One" is neuter — one thing, not one person. It does not collapse the two; the Father sends and the Son is sent throughout this book. It says that in the work under discussion — holding the flock — there are not two hands but one grip. And it says both halves of Ezekiel's promise at once: God shepherding his people himself, and doing it through the shepherd he raises up.\f*."
\p
\v 31 The Jews picked up stones again to stone him.
\v 32 Jesus answered them, "Many good works I showed you from the Father. For which of them are you stoning me?"
\v 33 The Jews answered him, "We are not stoning you for a good work, but for blasphemy, and because you, being a man, make yourself God."
\v 34 Jesus answered them, "Is it not written in your Torah, 'I said, you are gods'?\f + \fr 10:34 \ft Psalm 82:6, where God addresses the judges of Israel — men entrusted with a share of his own work of judging — as "gods" and "sons of the Most High," and then tells them they will die like men. "Your Torah" here means the Scriptures as a whole; the Psalms are included.\f*
\v 35 If he called those gods to whom the word of God came — and the Scripture cannot be loosed —
\v 36 do you say of the one whom the Father made holy\f + \fr 10:36 \ft The verb is \tl hagiazō\tl*, to consecrate, set apart — the word for what this very feast celebrates being done to a building. He says it has been done to him.\f* and sent into the world, 'You are blaspheming,' because I said, 'I am the Son of God'?\f + \fr 10:36 \ft The argument runs from the smaller case to the larger. If Scripture itself can call mere judges "gods" because God's word came to them, then the charge of blasphemy cannot be made to stick this quickly against the one God has consecrated and sent. It does not withdraw the claim of verse 30; it demands that the claim be weighed by the evidence rather than shouted down.\f*
\v 37 If I am not doing the works of my Father, do not trust me.
\v 38 But if I am doing them, even if you do not trust me, trust the works, so that you may know and go on knowing that the Father is in me and I am in the Father."
\v 39 So they were seeking again to seize him, and he went out of their hand \add [i.e. their hand cannot hold him; his hand cannot be prised open]\add*.
\p
\v 40 And he went away again beyond the Jordan, to the place where John was at first immersing, and he stayed there \add [i.e. back to where the book began, at Bethany across the river]\add*.
\v 41 And many came to him, and they were saying, "John did no sign, but everything John said about this one was true \add [i.e. the witness who worked no wonders has been proved right by the one who did]\add*."
\v 42 And many trusted in him there.

\c 11
\s1 Lazarus Is Sick
\p
\v 1 Now a certain man was sick, Lazarus\f + \fr 11:1 \ft The name is the Greek form of Eleazar, "God has helped."\f* from Bethany, out of the village of Mary and Martha her sister.
\v 2 It was the Mary who anointed the Lord with perfume and wiped his feet with her hair, whose brother Lazarus was sick \add [i.e. named here by something the reader has not yet been told; it is coming in the next chapter]\add*.
\v 3 So the sisters sent to him, saying, "Lord, look — the one you love is sick \add [i.e. no request; they state the fact and leave it with him]\add*."
\v 4 And hearing it Jesus said, "This sickness is not toward death, but for the glory of God, so that the Son of God may be glorified through it \add [i.e. death is not where it is going, though death is on the way]\add*."
\v 5 Now Jesus loved Martha and her sister and Lazarus.
\v 6 So when he heard that he was sick, then indeed he stayed two days in the place where he was\f + \fr 11:6 \ft The join is deliberately jarring: he loved them, "so" he stayed where he was. The delay is not neglect but arithmetic. By the time he arrives Lazarus will be four days dead, past the point where anyone could suppose he had merely been asleep.\f*.
\p
\v 7 Then after this he says to the disciples, "Let us go into Judea again."
\v 8 The disciples say to him, "Rabbi, just now the Jews were seeking to stone you, and you are going there again?"
\v 9 Jesus answered, "Are there not twelve hours in the day? If anyone walks in the day he does not stumble, because he sees the light of this world \add [i.e. the day allotted for this work is not over, and until it is over nothing can cut it short]\add*.
\v 10 But if anyone walks in the night he stumbles, because the light is not in him."
\v 11 He said these things, and after this he says to them, "Lazarus our friend has fallen asleep, but I am going to wake him up \add [i.e. sleep, in the ordinary way of speaking, meant death — and it is a word that leaves room for waking]\add*."
\v 12 So the disciples said to him, "Lord, if he has fallen asleep, he will be rescued\f + \fr 11:12 \ft The verb is \tl sōthēsetai\tl*, this book's word for being rescued, rendered so here for consistency. What the disciples mean is only "then he will get better."\f*."
\v 13 But Jesus had spoken about his death; and those thought he was speaking about the rest of sleep.
\v 14 So then Jesus said to them plainly, "Lazarus died.
\v 15 And I rejoice for your sake that I was not there, so that you may trust. But let us go to him \add [i.e. had he been there he would have healed a sick man; now something else can be shown]\add*."
\v 16 So Thomas, the one called the Twin, said to his fellow disciples, "Let us go too, so that we may die with him \add [i.e. loyal, and expecting the worst, and not wrong about the danger]\add*."
\s1 I Am the Rising and the Life
\p
\v 17 So when Jesus came he found him already four days in the tomb\f + \fr 11:17 \ft Four days matters. A widely held belief of the period was that the soul hovered near the body for three days and departed on the fourth, when decay set in. By the fourth day, death was reckoned final beyond argument — as Martha says outright in verse 39.\f*.
\v 18 Now Bethany was near Jerusalem, about fifteen stadia away \add [i.e. under two miles, close enough that what happens here will be in the city by evening]\add*,
\v 19 and many of the Jews had come to Martha and Mary to console them about their brother \add [i.e. neighbors keeping the days of mourning, and so a crowd of witnesses]\add*.
\v 20 So Martha, when she heard that Jesus was coming, went to meet him; but Mary was sitting in the house.
\v 21 So Martha said to Jesus, "Lord, if you had been here my brother would not have died \add [i.e. a reproach and a confession in one sentence]\add*.
\v 22 And even now I know that whatever you ask God, God will give you."
\v 23 Jesus says to her, "Your brother will rise up."
\v 24 Martha says to him, "I know that he will rise up in the rising on the last day \add [i.e. the standard hope, and correct as far as it goes (Daniel 12:2)]\add*."
\v 25 Jesus said to her, "I am the rising and the life\f + \fr 11:25 \ft She has just placed the resurrection on a date. He moves it off the calendar and onto a person: it is not first of all an event that will happen but someone who is standing in front of her.\f*. The one who trusts in me, even if he dies, will live;
\v 26 and everyone who lives and trusts in me will never die into the age. Do you trust this?"\f + \fr 11:26 \ft The two halves are not in tension. The first grants that the body dies; the second denies that this is dying.\f*
\v 27 She says to him, "Yes, Lord. I have trusted that you are the Christ, the Son of God, the one coming into the world \add [i.e. said before any sign is worked, and it is the fullest confession anyone has made in this book so far]\add*."
\p
\v 28 And having said this she went off and called Mary her sister, saying quietly, "The Teacher is here and is calling you."
\v 29 And when that one heard, she got up quickly and was coming to him.
\v 30 Now Jesus had not yet come into the village, but was still in the place where Martha met him.
\v 31 So the Jews who were with her in the house and consoling her, seeing Mary get up quickly and go out, followed her, thinking that she was going to the tomb to weep there \add [i.e. and so the whole company of mourners comes along]\add*.
\v 32 So Mary, when she came where Jesus was and saw him, fell at his feet, saying to him, "Lord, if you had been here my brother would not have died."
\v 33 So Jesus, when he saw her weeping, and the Jews who came with her weeping, snorted with anger in the spirit\f + \fr 11:33 \ft Greek \tl enebrimēsato\tl*, a word used of a horse snorting. It is anger or violent agitation, not sorrow; most English versions soften it to "deeply moved" or "groaned in spirit." What the anger is directed at is not said — the wreckage death makes, the grief in front of him, the unbelief in it. The same word again at verse 38.\f* and troubled himself,
\v 34 and said, "Where have you laid him?" They say to him, "Lord, come and see."
\v 35 Jesus wept\f + \fr 11:35 \ft A different verb from the wailing of verse 33 — this one is simply the shedding of tears. The one who is about to call a dead man out of a tomb stands in front of it and cries. "In all their affliction he was afflicted" (Isaiah 63:9).\f*.
\v 36 So the Jews were saying, "Look how he loved him."
\v 37 But some of them said, "Could not this one, who opened the eyes of the blind man, have kept this man also from dying?"
\s1 Lazarus, Out Here
\p
\v 38 So Jesus, snorting with anger again within himself, comes to the tomb. Now it was a cave, and a stone was lying against it.
\v 39 Jesus says, "Take away the stone." Martha, the sister of the one who had died, says to him, "Lord, by now he stinks; it is the fourth day \add [i.e. the woman who confessed him in verse 27 objects when the stone has to move]\add*."
\v 40 Jesus says to her, "Did I not tell you that if you trust you will see the glory of God?"
\v 41 So they took away the stone\f + \fr 11:41 \ft The words "where the dead man was lying" at the end of this clause are not in the critical text.\f*. And Jesus lifted his eyes upward and said, "Father, I thank you that you heard me.
\v 42 And I knew that you always hear me; but for the sake of the crowd standing around I said it, so that they may trust that you sent me \add [i.e. the prayer is spoken aloud for them, not for the Father, who has already heard]\add*."
\v 43 And having said these things, he shouted with a great voice, "Lazarus, out here!"\f + \fr 11:43 \ft "An hour is coming, and is now here, when the dead will hear the voice of the Son of God, and those who hear will live" (5:25). One dead man, one command, one tomb — and at the last day, all of them (5:28-29). And the shepherd "calls his own sheep by name" (10:3).\f*
\v 44 The dead man came out, bound feet and hands with strips of cloth, and his face wrapped around with a cloth. Jesus says to them, "Loose him and let him go\f + \fr 11:44 \ft Lazarus comes out still wearing the grave. He is raised back into a life he will have to leave again, and other hands must unwrap him. Compare the tomb in chapter 20, where the cloths are left lying and the face-cloth folded by itself, and nobody unbinds anything.\f*."
\s1 The Council Decides
\p
\v 45 So many of the Jews who had come to Mary and had watched what he did trusted in him.
\v 46 But some of them went off to the Pharisees and told them what Jesus had done \add [i.e. the same event, and two opposite responses to it]\add*.
\v 47 So the chief priests and the Pharisees gathered a council and were saying, "What are we doing? For this man is doing many signs \add [i.e. they do not dispute the signs; they are working out what to do about them]\add*.
\v 48 If we let him go on like this, all will trust in him, and the Romans will come and take away both our place and our nation\f + \fr 11:48 \ft "Our place" means the temple. The fear is not unreasonable: Rome had no patience for messianic excitement in the provinces. Within forty years both the place and the nation were gone anyway.\f*."
\v 49 But a certain one of them, Caiaphas, being high priest that year\f + \fr 11:49 \ft Not that the office changed yearly — Caiaphas held it for some eighteen years. "That year" means the year these things happened, the year of the death.\f*, said to them, "You know nothing at all,
\v 50 nor do you reckon that it is better for you that one man die for the people and the whole nation not be destroyed \add [i.e. cold arithmetic: spend one to save the rest]\add*."
\v 51 Now this he did not say from himself; but being high priest that year he prophesied that Jesus was about to die for the nation,
\v 52 and not for the nation only, but so that he might also gather into one the scattered children of God\f + \fr 11:52 \ft The writer takes the high priest's political calculation and hears in it the thing itself. The word "for" that Caiaphas uses of expedience is the word Jesus uses of the shepherd laying down his life "for" the sheep (10:11) and of the bread given "for" the life of the world (6:51). And the gathering of the scattered is the prophets' promise of God collecting his people from every country (Ezekiel 34:13; Isaiah 43:5-6) — here reaching past the nation to "the other sheep that are not out of this fold" (10:16).\f*.
\v 53 So from that day they took counsel to kill him \add [i.e. the raising of a dead man is what settles them on a killing]\add*.
\v 54 So Jesus no longer walked openly among the Jews, but went away from there into the region near the wilderness, to a city called Ephraim, and there he stayed with the disciples.
\p
\v 55 Now the Passover of the Jews was near, and many went up to Jerusalem out of the country before the Passover, to purify themselves \add [i.e. the cleansing required of anyone who would eat the meal]\add*.
\v 56 So they were looking for Jesus and saying to one another as they stood in the temple, "What do you think? That he will not come to the feast at all?"
\v 57 Now the chief priests and the Pharisees had given orders that if anyone knew where he was, he should disclose it, so that they might seize him.

\c 12
\s1 The Anointing at Bethany
\p
\v 1 So six days before the Passover Jesus came to Bethany, where Lazarus was, whom Jesus had raised from among the dead \add [i.e. the countdown to the lamb's day has started]\add*.
\v 2 So they made him a supper there, and Martha was serving, and Lazarus was one of those reclining with him \add [i.e. a man four days dead, at the table, eating]\add*.
\v 3 So Mary took a pound of perfume of pure nard, very costly\f + \fr 12:3 \ft Nard was an aromatic oil pressed from a plant grown in the Himalayan highlands and shipped the whole way west; Judas prices this jar at a labourer's wages for a year. Anointing the head was the ordinary honor. Anointing the "feet" — and drying them with her own unbound hair — is the work of the lowest servant, done with the most expensive thing she owns.\f*, and anointed the feet of Jesus and wiped his feet with her hair; and the house was filled with the fragrance of the perfume.
\v 4 But Judas Iscariot, one of his disciples, the one about to hand him over, says,
\v 5 "Why was this perfume not sold for three hundred denarii and given to the poor?"
\v 6 Now he said this not because he cared about the poor, but because he was a thief\f + \fr 12:6 \ft The word is \tl kleptēs\tl*, the same word Jesus used two chapters ago for the man who does not come in by the door and who "comes only to steal and slaughter and destroy" (10:1, 10). The thief is inside the fold, and he is carrying the common purse.\f* and, having the money box, used to carry off what was put in it.
\v 7 So Jesus said, "Leave her alone, so that she may keep it for the day of my burial\f + \fr 12:7 \ft The critical text has a purpose clause, as rendered: that she may keep it for that day. Older English versions read it the other way — "against the day of my burying hath she kept this." Either way the act is named for what it is: a body being prepared, six days early.\f*.
\v 8 For the poor you always have with you\f + \fr 12:8 \ft Deuteronomy 15:11: "the poor will never cease out of the land; therefore I command you, open wide your hand to your brother." The text he echoes makes care for the poor a standing obligation precisely because poverty is permanent. What is not permanent is the body in the room.\f*, but me you do not always have."
\p
\v 9 So the large crowd of the Jews knew that he was there, and they came not only because of Jesus but also so that they might see Lazarus, whom he raised from among the dead.
\v 10 And the chief priests took counsel to kill Lazarus too \add [i.e. the evidence has to be destroyed along with the man who produced it]\add*,
\v 11 because on account of him many of the Jews were going away and trusting in Jesus.
\s1 The King on a Donkey's Colt
\p
\v 12 The next day the large crowd that had come to the feast, hearing that Jesus was coming into Jerusalem,
\v 13 took the branches of the palm trees\f + \fr 12:13 \ft Palm branches were the emblem of national victory. They were carried when Judas Maccabeus rededicated the temple (2 Maccabees 10:7) and when Simon took the citadel of Jerusalem "with praise and palm branches... because a great enemy had been crushed out of Israel" (1 Maccabees 13:51), and they were struck on the coins of the years of independence. The crowd is welcoming a liberator.\f* and went out to meet him, and they were shouting, "Hosanna! Blessed is the one coming in the name of the Lord — even the King of Israel!"\f + \fr 12:13 \ft Psalm 118:25-26, sung at the festivals; "Hosanna" is the Hebrew "save now." The last phrase, "even the King of Israel," is not in the psalm. The crowd adds it.\f*
\v 14 And Jesus, finding a young donkey, sat on it, as it is written,
\v 15 "Do not fear, daughter of Zion. Look, your king is coming, sitting on a donkey's colt."\f + \fr 12:15 \ft Zechariah 9:9, with "Do not fear" from Zephaniah 3:14-16 — where the king in the midst of Zion is the LORD himself. Zechariah's next line says what kind of king rides a donkey: "I will cut off the chariot from Ephraim and the war horse from Jerusalem, and the battle bow shall be cut off, and he shall speak peace to the nations" (Zechariah 9:10). They wave the branches of a war of independence; he answers by sitting on the animal of a peacetime road.\f*
\v 16 His disciples did not know these things at first; but when Jesus was glorified, then they remembered that these things had been written about him and that they had done these things to him \add [i.e. they took part in it without seeing what it was, and understood afterward]\add*.
\v 17 So the crowd that was with him when he called Lazarus out of the tomb and raised him from among the dead was testifying.
\v 18 For this reason also the crowd met him: because they heard that he had done this sign.
\v 19 So the Pharisees said to one another, "You see that you are getting nowhere. Look, the world has gone off after him\f + \fr 12:19 \ft They mean "everybody." But "the world" in this book is a loaded word — the world God loved and gave his Son for, the world that did not know him. Read as the writer intends it, their exasperated remark is a true statement, and the next verse proves it: Greeks arrive.\f*."
\s1 Greeks Come, and the Hour Comes
\p
\v 20 Now there were some Greeks among those going up to worship at the feast \add [i.e. Gentiles drawn to Israel's God, who kept the feasts without being circumcised into the nation]\add*.
\v 21 So these came to Philip, the one from Bethsaida of Galilee, and were asking him, saying, "Sir, we want to see Jesus."
\v 22 Philip comes and tells Andrew; Andrew and Philip come and tell Jesus \add [i.e. the two disciples with Greek names, from the mixed country up north]\add*.
\v 23 And Jesus answers them, saying, "The hour has come for the son of man to be glorified\f + \fr 12:23 \ft Since Cana it has been "my hour has not yet come" (2:4; 7:30; 8:20). The trigger for saying it has arrived is the appearance of Gentiles at the door — the first of the other sheep, the first of the scattered children. The grain can fall now, because the harvest it is for has shown up.\f*.
\v 24 Amen, amen, I say to you, unless the grain of wheat falls into the earth and dies, it remains alone; but if it dies, it bears much fruit \add [i.e. a seed kept safe in a jar stays a seed; the only way it becomes many is to be buried]\add*.
\v 25 The one who loves his life loses it, and the one who hates his life in this world will guard it into life of the age\f + \fr 12:25 \ft "Hates" is a Hebrew way of saying "puts second" — as in "Jacob I loved, but Esau I hated" (Malachi 1:2-3). The seed principle is not for him only; it is the shape of following him.\f*.
\v 26 If anyone serves me, let him follow me; and where I am, there my servant will also be. If anyone serves me, the Father will honor him.
\p
\v 27 "Now my life within me has been troubled. And what shall I say? 'Father, rescue me out of this hour'? But for this I came to this hour \add [i.e. he names the prayer he could pray and declines to pray it]\add*.
\v 28 Father, glorify your name." So a voice came out of heaven: "I have both glorified it and will glorify it again."
\v 29 So the crowd standing and hearing it were saying that thunder had happened; others were saying, "An angel has spoken to him \add [i.e. they hear the sound and not the speech — the whole problem of this book in one sentence]\add*."
\v 30 Jesus answered and said, "This voice has not come for my sake but for yours.
\v 31 Now is the judgment of this world; now the ruler of this world will be thrown outside\f + \fr 12:31 \ft "The ruler of this world" appears only in this book (here, 14:30, 16:11) — the power behind the world's opposition, the one identified at 8:44 as the devil, a murderer from the beginning. The cross looks like his victory. This says it is his eviction.\f*.
\v 32 And I, if I am lifted up out of the earth, will draw all to myself\f + \fr 12:32 \ft The Greek \tl pantas\tl* is masculine — all people, not all things; English "all" can still be heard the other way, but the Greek here cannot. Note also who draws. At 6:44 it was the Father who draws; here it is the Son, and the scope is stated: all.\f*."
\v 33 Now he was saying this to signify by what kind of death he was about to die \add [i.e. lifted up is a crucifixion, and the writer will not let it be read as anything vaguer]\add*.
\v 34 So the crowd answered him, "We have heard out of the Torah that the Christ remains into the age. And how do you say that the son of man must be lifted up? Who is this son of man?\f + \fr 12:34 \ft Their objection is scriptural and reasonable: the Messiah's throne is to be established forever (Psalm 89:36-37; Daniel 7:14). A Messiah who is lifted up and dies does not fit the category they have.\f*"
\v 35 So Jesus said to them, "Yet a little time the light is among you. Walk while you have the light, so that darkness may not overtake you. And the one who walks in the darkness does not know where he is going.
\v 36 While you have the light, trust in the light, so that you may become sons of light\f + \fr 12:36 \ft "Sons of" is the Hebrew way of naming what a person essentially belongs to. Not admirers of the light — people made of it.\f*." Jesus spoke these things, and going away he was hidden from them \add [i.e. and with that the public ministry ends]\add*.
\s1 Why They Could Not Trust
\p
\v 37 But though he had done so many signs before them, they were not trusting in him,
\v 38 so that the word of Isaiah the prophet might be filled full, which he said: "Lord, who has trusted our report? And to whom has the arm of the Lord been uncovered?"\f + \fr 12:38 \ft Isaiah 53:1, the opening of the song of the servant who is despised, rejected, and led like a lamb to the slaughter. The two questions expect the answer "almost nobody." The "arm of the LORD" is his saving power in action — which in that song is at work precisely in the suffering that everyone misreads.\f*
\v 39 For this reason they could not trust, because Isaiah said again,
\v 40 "He has blinded their eyes and hardened their heart, so that they may not see with the eyes and understand with the heart and turn, and I heal them."\f + \fr 12:40 \ft Isaiah 6:10, from the prophet's commissioning — the hardest sentence in the prophets. In Isaiah it is spoken over a people who have already spent generations refusing, and it announces that the refusing will now be confirmed and set. This book has shown the same order at work: men loved the darkness first (3:19), and the blindness that follows belongs to those who insist "we see" (9:41). The hardening is a verdict on a choice, not a bar placed in front of one.\f*
\v 41 Isaiah said these things because\f + \fr 12:41 \ft The critical text reads "because he saw his glory"; the majority text and older English versions have "when he saw his glory."\f* he saw his glory, and he spoke about him\f + \fr 12:41 \ft The vision meant is Isaiah 6 — the Lord seated on a throne, high and lifted up, the whole earth full of his glory. The writer says that the glory Isaiah saw was "his": the glory of the one now walking to a cross.\f*.
\v 42 Nevertheless, even many of the rulers trusted in him; but because of the Pharisees they were not confessing it, so that they would not be put out of the synagogue;
\v 43 for they loved the glory of men more than the glory of God\f + \fr 12:43 \ft The mechanism named at 5:44, now with names attached. Belief that will not be said out loud, because saying it costs standing, is the thing this book keeps setting beside a blind beggar who said it and was thrown out for it (9:34-38).\f*.
\s1 The Last Public Words
\p
\v 44 And Jesus cried out and said, "The one who trusts in me does not trust in me but in the one who sent me \add [i.e. trust aimed at me does not stop at me]\add*,
\v 45 and the one who watches me watches the one who sent me.
\v 46 I have come as a light into the world, so that everyone who trusts in me may not remain in the darkness.
\v 47 And if anyone hears my words and does not keep them, I do not judge him; for I did not come to judge the world, but to rescue the world.
\v 48 The one who rejects me and does not receive my words has one who judges him: the word that I spoke — that will judge him on the last day \add [i.e. he needs no accuser; what he refused will do it]\add*.
\v 49 For I did not speak from myself; but the Father who sent me, he himself has given me a commandment — what I should say and what I should speak.
\v 50 And I know that his commandment is life of the age. So what I speak, just as the Father has told me, so I speak."

\c 13
\s1 He Washed Their Feet
\p
\v 1 Now before the feast of the Passover\f + \fr 13:1 \ft In this book the supper is not the Passover meal; the Passover has not yet been eaten (see 18:28 and 19:14). Jesus dies on the day of preparation, at the hour the lambs are killed in the temple. The whole narrative is arranged so that the Lamb of God (1:29) dies when the lambs die.\f*, Jesus, knowing that his hour had come for him to cross over out of this world to the Father\f + \fr 13:1 \ft "Cross over" is \tl metabainō\tl* — the same verb used at 5:24 of the believer who "has crossed over out of death into life." His death is described as a passing over, on the eve of Passover.\f*, having loved his own who were in the world, loved them to the end\f + \fr 13:1 \ft Greek \tl eis telos\tl*, which means both "to the last moment" and "to the uttermost." Both hold. He does not stop loving them as the betrayal, the denial, and the scattering come on; and the loving goes the whole distance. This sentence is the heading over everything from here to chapter 17.\f*.
\v 2 And during supper, the devil having already thrown it into the heart\f + \fr 13:2 \ft The Greek does not say whose heart — his own or Judas's. It is left unstated here, as the Greek leaves it.\f* that Judas son of Simon Iscariot should hand him over,
\v 3 Jesus, knowing that the Father had given all things into his hands and that he had come out from God and was going to God \add [i.e. hold that sentence in mind while you read the next one]\add*,
\v 4 gets up from the supper and lays aside his outer garments\f + \fr 13:4 \ft "Lays aside" is \tl tithēsin\tl*, the very verb used of the shepherd who "lays down" his life (10:11, 15, 17-18), and in verse 12 he "takes" them back up again, the verb of 10:18. The whole movement — rising, setting aside, taking the form of a slave, finishing, resuming — is the gospel acted out at a table before it is enacted on a cross.\f*, and taking a towel he tied it around himself.
\v 5 Then he pours water into the basin and began to wash the disciples' feet\f + \fr 13:5 \ft Washing feet was the lowest work in a household. Jewish practice reckoned it too degrading to require of a Hebrew slave; a disciple might carry his teacher's sandals, but this was beneath even that. The teacher is doing it to the students, which does not merely reverse the courtesy — it dismantles the ranking that the whole relationship was built on.\f* and to wipe them with the towel he was tied with.
\p
\v 6 So he comes to Simon Peter. He says to him, "Lord, are you washing my feet?"\f + \fr 13:6 \ft The Greek puts the weight on the two pronouns: you — my feet? He is not objecting to the washing but to who is doing it for whom.\f*
\v 7 Jesus answered and said to him, "What I am doing you do not know now, but you will know after these things."
\v 8 Peter says to him, "You will never wash my feet into the age." Jesus answered him, "If I do not wash you, you have no part with me\f + \fr 13:8 \ft "Part" is \tl meros\tl*, the word for a share in an inheritance — the portion each tribe was allotted, the portion Levi was told he would not have because "the LORD is his inheritance" (Deuteronomy 10:9). Refusing to be served by him is refusing a place in the household. The disciple who will not receive cannot belong.\f*."
\v 9 Simon Peter says to him, "Lord, not my feet only, but also my hands and my head \add [i.e. from refusing altogether to demanding more, and still not understanding]\add*."
\v 10 Jesus says to him, "The one who has bathed has no need to wash, except the feet, but is entirely clean\f + \fr 13:10 \ft Two different verbs: to bathe the whole body, and to wash a part of it. A guest bathed at home and needed only the road dust taken off his feet on arrival. The bath has already happened — "you are already clean because of the word I have spoken to you" (15:3). What the basin deals with is what walking in the world puts on you.\f*. And you are clean — but not all of you."
\v 11 For he knew the one handing him over; for this reason he said, "You are not all clean \add [i.e. the water reaches Judas's feet too, and changes nothing]\add*."
\p
\v 12 So when he had washed their feet and taken his outer garments and reclined again, he said to them, "Do you know what I have done to you?
\v 13 You call me the Teacher and the Lord, and you say well, for I am.
\v 14 So if I, the Lord and the Teacher, washed your feet, you also owe it to wash one another's feet\f + \fr 13:14 \ft "Owe it" is a debt word, not a suggestion. And it is "one another's" — the obligation runs inside the community, in every direction. If the one at the top has done it, nobody below him can claim to be above it.\f*.
\v 15 For I gave you an example, so that just as I did to you, you also should do.
\v 16 Amen, amen, I say to you, a slave is not greater than his master, nor is one sent greater than the one who sent him.
\v 17 If you know these things, blessed are you if you do them \add [i.e. the blessing is on the doing, not the knowing]\add*.
\v 18 I am not speaking about all of you; I know whom I chose. But it is so that the Scripture may be filled full: 'The one chewing my bread has lifted his heel against me.'\f + \fr 13:18 \ft Psalm 41:9, where the betrayer is not an enemy but the friend who shared the table. Jewish tradition read it of Ahithophel, David's trusted counsellor, who went over to Absalom and hanged himself when his advice was refused (2 Samuel 15-17). "Chewing" is \tl trōgō\tl* again, the blunt word from the bread discourse (6:54).\f*
\v 19 From now on I tell you before it happens, so that when it happens you may trust that I am \add [i.e. so the betrayal, when it lands, proves the word rather than breaking it]\add*.
\v 20 Amen, amen, I say to you, the one who receives anyone I send receives me, and the one who receives me receives the one who sent me."
\s1 One of You Will Hand Me Over
\p
\v 21 Having said these things, Jesus was troubled in the spirit and testified and said, "Amen, amen, I say to you, one of you will hand me over."
\v 22 The disciples were looking at one another, at a loss about whom he was speaking \add [i.e. nobody suspected Judas, which is the measure of how well it had been hidden]\add*.
\v 23 One of his disciples, whom Jesus loved, was reclining at Jesus' bosom\f + \fr 13:23 \ft The first appearance of this figure, never named, who will be at the cross (19:26), at the tomb (20:2-8), on the shore (21:7), and named at the end as the witness behind the book (21:24). The phrase "at his bosom" is the phrase used of the Son in the prologue, "in the bosom of the Father" (1:18). At a formal meal the guests reclined on the left elbow; the man to the host's right lay back against his chest. It is the place of honor and of confidences.\f*.
\v 24 So Simon Peter nods to this one and says to him, "Say who it is he is speaking about."
\v 25 So that one, leaning back just so on Jesus' chest, says to him, "Lord, who is it?"
\v 26 So Jesus answers, "It is the one for whom I will dip the morsel and give it to him." So dipping the morsel he takes it and gives it to Judas son of Simon Iscariot\f + \fr 13:26 \ft Handing a guest a dipped piece of bread was a mark of favor from the host. The betrayer is singled out by an act of hospitality.\f*.
\v 27 And after the morsel, then Satan entered into that one. So Jesus says to him, "What you are doing, do quickly \add [i.e. he does not stop it; he sets its pace]\add*."
\v 28 But no one of those reclining knew for what purpose he said this to him.
\v 29 For some were thinking, since Judas had the money box, that Jesus was telling him, "Buy what we need for the feast," or that he should give something to the poor \add [i.e. and the feast is still ahead, which is why they could think it]\add*.
\v 30 So that one, having taken the morsel, went out immediately. And it was night\f + \fr 13:30 \ft Three words, and every earlier use of "darkness" in this book presses on them. The light shines in the darkness (1:5); men loved the darkness (3:19); the one who walks at night stumbles, because the light is not in him (11:10). He goes out of the light and into the dark, and the door shuts behind him. From here on, everything Jesus says is said to those who are left.\f*.
\s1 A New Commandment
\p
\v 31 So when he had gone out, Jesus says, "Now the son of man has been glorified, and God has been glorified in him\f + \fr 13:31 \ft "Now" — with the betrayer out the door and the machinery running. The glorifying is not what happens after the cross despite it; it is the cross. And the tense is past: so certain that it can be spoken of as done.\f*.
\v 32 If God has been glorified in him\f + \fr 13:32 \ft The critical text brackets these words as uncertain; they are printed here.\f*, God will also glorify him in himself, and will glorify him immediately.
\v 33 Little children\f + \fr 13:33 \ft The only place Jesus uses this word in the four accounts; it is the habitual address of the letters that come from this same circle (1 John 2:1, 12, 28). At the moment the betrayal starts moving he speaks to them like a parent.\f*, yet a little while I am with you. You will look for me, and just as I said to the Jews, 'Where I am going, you cannot come,' so I say to you now\f + \fr 13:33 \ft The same words, and not the same meaning. To the authorities it meant permanent exclusion (8:21). To these men it means "not yet" — as verse 36 immediately says: "you will follow later."\f*.
\v 34 A new commandment I give you: that you love one another; just as I loved you, that you also love one another\f + \fr 13:34 \ft What is new is not the command — "you shall love your neighbor as yourself" is Leviticus 19:18 — but the measure of it. Not "as yourself" but "as I loved you," which has just been demonstrated with a basin and will be demonstrated again tomorrow.\f*.
\v 35 By this all will know that you are my disciples: if you have love among one another\f + \fr 13:35 \ft The identifying mark is not a doctrine, a rite, an ancestry, or a display of power. It is how these people treat each other, and it is meant to be visible from outside.\f*."
\p
\v 36 Simon Peter says to him, "Lord, where are you going?" Jesus answered, "Where I am going you cannot follow me now, but you will follow later \add [i.e. and chapter 21 will say what following later costs him]\add*."
\v 37 Peter says to him, "Lord, why can I not follow you now? I will lay down my life for you\f + \fr 13:37 \ft He uses the shepherd's own words — lay down, on behalf of (10:11, 15). The sheep is offering to do for the shepherd what only the shepherd can do.\f*."
\v 38 Jesus answers, "You will lay down your life for me? Amen, amen, I say to you, a cock will not crow until you have denied me three times.

\c 14
\s1 In My Father's House
\p
\v 1 "Do not let your heart be troubled\f + \fr 14:1 \ft The same verb used of Jesus himself at the tomb (11:33), at the coming of the hour (12:27), and a moment ago at the table (13:21). He knows the disturbance from the inside. And they have grounds for it: a betrayer among them, their leader leaving, and the strongest of them told he will fail before dawn.\f*. Trust in God, and trust in me\f + \fr 14:1 \ft The Greek verbs can each be read as statement or command; taken here as "you do trust God — now trust me too." The trust already given to God is to be extended to the one God sent, and extended precisely when he is going away.\f*.
\v 2 In my Father's house are many dwelling places\f + \fr 14:2 \ft Greek \tl monai\tl*, from \tl menō\tl*, "remain" — the verb that will run right through chapter 15. These are the places where one "stays"; English cannot show it. The word occurs only twice in the New Testament, here and at verse 23, where the movement runs the other way: the Father and the Son come and make their dwelling place with the one who loves him. There is room for them in his house, and room for him in theirs.\f*; and if not, would I have told you that I am going to prepare a place for you?\f + \fr 14:2 \ft The critical text includes \tl hoti\tl*, which turns the line into the question rendered here. Older English versions read it as two sentences: "if it were not so, I would have told you. I go to prepare a place for you."\f*
\v 3 And if I go and prepare a place for you, I am coming again and will take you along to myself, so that where I am you also may be \add [i.e. the answer to "where I am going, you cannot come" — not now, but you will]\add*.
\v 4 And where I am going, you know the way."
\s1 I Am the Way
\p
\v 5 Thomas says to him, "Lord, we do not know where you are going. How can we know the way?"\f + \fr 14:5 \ft He is thinking of a road, and says honestly that he does not know it. The answer redefines the word.\f*
\v 6 Jesus says to him, "I am the way and the truth and the life\f + \fr 14:6 \ft He asked for directions and was given a person. In the Greek Old Testament "the way of the LORD" is both God's own manner of acting and the road he opens for his people: "Teach me your way, O LORD, that I may walk in your truth" (Psalm 86:11). The three words are not three items but one thing said three ways — the road, because he is the disclosure of the Father; the disclosure, because he is the life God gives.\f*. No one comes to the Father except through me.
\v 7 If you have known me, you will know my Father also\f + \fr 14:7 \ft The critical text has a real condition, as rendered — if you have known me, you will know. Many English versions have the contrary-to-fact form: "if you had known me, you would have known my Father."\f*. And from now on you know him and have seen him."
\v 8 Philip says to him, "Lord, show us the Father, and it is enough for us\f + \fr 14:8 \ft He is asking for what Moses asked for: "Show me your glory" (Exodus 33:18). It is a serious request with a long pedigree, and it is being made to the answer.\f*."
\v 9 Jesus says to him, "So long a time I am with you, and you have not known me, Philip? The one who has seen me has seen the Father \add [i.e. the showing you are asking for has already happened; you have been standing in it]\add*. How do you say, 'Show us the Father'?
\v 10 Do you not trust that I am in the Father and the Father is in me? The words that I say to you I do not speak from myself; but the Father remaining in me does his works.
\v 11 Trust me, that I am in the Father and the Father is in me; but if not, trust because of the works themselves \add [i.e. if my word is not enough for you, start with what you have watched me do]\add*.
\v 12 Amen, amen, I say to you, the one who trusts in me will also do the works that I do, and greater than these he will do, because I am going to the Father\f + \fr 14:12 \ft "Greater" is a matter of reach, not of spectacle. The reason is given in the clause that follows: because he is going to the Father — which is what releases the Spirit and opens the mission to the world. His own work ran three years in one small province.\f*.
\v 13 And whatever you ask in my name\f + \fr 14:13 \ft To act "in the name of" someone, in the usage of the time, was to act as his authorized agent — with his authority, on his business, within his commission. It is not a formula tacked to the end of a prayer; it describes what kind of asking is meant.\f*, this I will do, so that the Father may be glorified in the Son.
\v 14 If you ask me\f + \fr 14:14 \ft The critical text includes "me": "If you ask me anything in my name." Many English versions omit it.\f* anything in my name, I will do it.
\s1 Another Advocate
\p
\v 15 "If you love me, you will keep my commandments\f + \fr 14:15 \ft Love and obedience are not two things in this discourse. Love produces the keeping (here); the keeping identifies the love (verse 21); the absence of the keeping exposes the absence of the love (verse 24).\f*.
\v 16 And I will ask the Father, and he will give you another Advocate\f + \fr 14:16 \ft Greek \tl paraklētos\tl*, one called to your side: advocate, helper, comforter, counsel for the defence. "Advocate" is used here because in this book the work of this one is to testify (15:26) and to convict (16:8); the other senses are lost in English. And note "another" — another of the same kind. Jesus has been the first one; this is a second of his sort, which is why the departure is not abandonment. The same word describes Jesus himself in 1 John 2:1. Again at 14:26, 15:26, 16:7.\f*, so that he may be with you into the age —
\v 17 the Spirit of truth, whom the world cannot receive, because it does not watch him or know him. You know him, because he remains with you and will be in you\f + \fr 14:17 \ft The preposition changes: "with" you, and then "in" you. That is the whole exchange being offered — presence alongside them in a body that can only be in one place, traded for presence inside them that cannot be shut out by a door or ended by a death.\f*.
\v 18 I will not leave you orphaned\f + \fr 14:18 \ft The word for children whose protector is gone — the class God specially defends in the Law: "a father of the fatherless... is God in his holy habitation" (Psalm 68:5). He has just called them little children (13:33); now he says he will not leave them that way.\f*; I am coming to you.
\v 19 Yet a little while and the world no longer watches me; but you watch me. Because I live, you also will live.
\v 20 In that day you will know that I am in my Father, and you in me, and I in you\f + \fr 14:20 \ft The chain has three links now. Until this point the mutual indwelling has been between Father and Son (10:38; 14:10-11). Here the disciples are drawn inside it. Chapter 15 will give it a picture: a vine and its branches.\f*.
\v 21 The one who has my commandments and keeps them, that one is the one who loves me. And the one who loves me will be loved by my Father, and I will love him and will make myself visible to him."
\p
\v 22 Judas — not the Iscariot\f + \fr 14:22 \ft The writer marks the distinction; the other Judas went out into the night nine verses ago.\f* — says to him, "Lord, and what has happened that you are about to make yourself visible to us and not to the world?"\f + \fr 14:22 \ft The expectation was of a showing nobody could miss: "the glory of the LORD shall be revealed, and all flesh shall see it together" (Isaiah 40:5). His answer is that it will not be a display but a residence.\f*
\v 23 Jesus answered and said to him, "If anyone loves me he will keep my word, and my Father will love him, and we will come to him and make our dwelling place with him\f + \fr 14:23 \ft This is the answer to the question, and it re-reads verse 2. The showing will not be a spectacle over a city; it will be a residence in a person. "Dwelling place" is \tl monē\tl* again — the same word as the many dwellings in the Father's house. Israel's covenant ran toward exactly this: "I will make my dwelling among you... and I will be your God, and you shall be my people" (Leviticus 26:11-12; Ezekiel 37:26-27). Not a building. A man who loves him.\f*.
\v 24 The one who does not love me does not keep my words. And the word that you hear is not mine but the Father's who sent me.
\v 25 These things I have spoken to you while remaining with you.
\v 26 But the Advocate, the Holy Spirit, whom the Father will send in my name, that one will teach you everything and remind you of everything I said to you\f + \fr 14:26 \ft Reminding, not replacing. The Spirit brings back what has already been said and makes it mean what it always meant. This is the explanation of something the writer has already reported twice: after the resurrection "his disciples remembered that he had said this" (2:22), and "then they remembered that these things had been written about him" (12:16).\f*.
\s1 My Peace I Give You
\p
\v 27 "Peace I leave with you; my peace I give to you\f + \fr 14:27 \ft \tl Shalom\tl* is the ordinary word of farewell, and it means far more than the absence of trouble — wholeness, soundness, everything as it should be. It is the priests' blessing over Israel: "the LORD lift up his countenance upon you and give you peace" (Numbers 6:26), and the covenant of peace the prophets promised (Isaiah 54:10; Ezekiel 37:26). He hands it over on the night of his arrest.\f*. Not as the world gives do I give to you. Do not let your heart be troubled, nor let it be cowardly\f + \fr 14:27 \ft The pairing "do not fear, do not be afraid" is the formula spoken over Israel at the edge of the land and before battle (Deuteronomy 1:21; 31:6; Joshua 1:9).\f*.
\v 28 You heard that I said to you, 'I am going away and I am coming to you.' If you loved me, you would rejoice that I am going to the Father, because the Father is greater than I\f + \fr 14:28 \ft The distinction the book has kept throughout, alongside the unity it has also kept: the Father sends and the Son is sent; the Father commands and the Son obeys; the Father is the destination and the Son is going to him. In context the greatness is the reason for joy — he is going to the greater, not away into nothing.\f*.
\v 29 And now I have told you before it happens, so that when it happens you may trust.
\v 30 No longer will I speak much with you, for the ruler of the world is coming; and he has nothing in me\f + \fr 14:30 \ft Literally "in me he has nothing" — no hold, no foothold, no part. The devil found room in Judas's heart (13:2, 27). In him he finds nothing at all.\f*.
\v 31 But so that the world may know that I love the Father, and just as the Father commanded me, so I do. Get up, let us go from here\f + \fr 14:31 \ft They stand and leave, and the discourse continues through chapters 15-17 — most simply, spoken on the walk from the room to the garden. The word "let us go" is the same one used twice on the road to Lazarus's tomb (11:7, 15). Both times it means walking toward a death.\f*.

\c 15
\s1 I Am the True Vine
\p
\v 1 "I am the true vine\f + \fr 15:1 \ft The vine is Israel, and in the prophets it is always Israel that failed. God planted a choice vine and looked for good grapes, and it gave wild ones (Isaiah 5:1-7). "I planted you a choice vine... how then have you turned into a degenerate wild vine?" (Jeremiah 2:21). Psalm 80 begs God to look again at the vine he brought out of Egypt, now burned and trampled. And Ezekiel asks what vine wood is good for if it bears nothing, and answers: fuel (Ezekiel 15:1-8). "True" here, as everywhere in this book, means the real thing the earlier version pointed at. He is the vine Israel was planted to be.\f*, and my Father is the farmer.
\v 2 Every branch in me not bearing fruit he lifts away\f + \fr 15:2 \ft The Greek \tl airei\tl* means both "lifts away" and "lifts up"; "lifts away" is taken here. Note that these fruitless branches are described as being "in me." The book has already shown one: chosen, at the table, his feet washed, and not clean (6:70; 13:10-11).\f*, and every one bearing fruit he cleans, so that it may bear more fruit\f + \fr 15:2 \ft "Cleans" is \tl kathairei\tl*, the pruning word, and it is the root of "clean" in the next verse, which English keeps. Pruning is not punishment; it is what is done to a branch that is succeeding, to make it succeed more.\f*.
\v 3 You are already clean because of the word I have spoken to you \add [i.e. the bath of 13:10, and now without the exception, because Judas has gone]\add*.
\v 4 Remain in me, and I in you\f + \fr 15:4 \ft "Remain" is \tl menō\tl*, which occurs eleven times in seven verses here — the heaviest concentration in the book. And it is an imperative. He does not tell them that they will remain; he tells them to. A command assumes the other possibility.\f*. Just as the branch cannot bear fruit from itself unless it remains in the vine, so neither can you unless you remain in me.
\v 5 I am the vine, you are the branches. The one who remains in me and I in him, this one bears much fruit, because apart from me you can do nothing \add [i.e. what he has said of himself in relation to the Father, he now says of them in relation to himself]\add*.
\v 6 If anyone does not remain in me, he is thrown outside like the branch and dries up; and they gather them and throw them into the fire, and they are burned\f + \fr 15:6 \ft Ezekiel asked what use vine wood is when it bears nothing: it cannot even be made into a peg, and it goes on the fire (Ezekiel 15:2-6). The sentence is a warning inside a farewell, and the writer does not soften it. Set it beside 6:37 — "the one who comes to me I will never throw outside." The one who comes is not thrown out; the one who does not remain is.\f*.
\v 7 If you remain in me and my words remain in you, ask whatever you want, and it will happen for you \add [i.e. a man shaped by these words will not be asking for other things]\add*.
\v 8 In this my Father is glorified: that you bear much fruit and become my disciples.
\s1 Remain in My Love
\p
\v 9 "Just as the Father loved me, I also loved you. Remain in my love.
\v 10 If you keep my commandments, you will remain in my love, just as I have kept my Father's commandments and remain in his love \add [i.e. he is not asking of them anything other than what he has done himself]\add*.
\v 11 These things I have spoken to you so that my joy may be in you and your joy may be made full.
\p
\v 12 "This is my commandment: that you love one another just as I loved you \add [i.e. the whole list from verse 10, gathered into one item]\add*.
\v 13 No one has greater love than this: that someone lay down his life for his friends\f + \fr 15:13 \ft "Lay down" is the shepherd's verb again (10:11, 15, 17-18), and he will do it within a day. "Friends" is a weighty word: in Israel's scriptures Abraham is called the friend of God (2 Chronicles 20:7; Isaiah 41:8) and Moses spoke with God "as a man speaks to his friend" (Exodus 33:11).\f*.
\v 14 You are my friends if you do what I command you.
\v 15 No longer do I call you slaves, because the slave does not know what his master is doing. But I have called you friends, because everything I heard from my Father I made known to you \add [i.e. what raises them from slaves to friends is that they have been told]\add*.
\v 16 You did not choose me, but I chose you, and I appointed you to go and bear fruit, and that your fruit should remain — so that whatever you ask the Father in my name, he may give you.
\v 17 These things I command you: that you love one another.
\s1 The World Will Hate You
\p
\v 18 "If the world hates you, know that it has hated me before you \add [i.e. it is not aimed at you; it is aimed past you]\add*.
\v 19 If you were of the world, the world would love its own. But because you are not of the world, and I chose you out of the world, for this reason the world hates you.
\v 20 Remember the word that I said to you: 'A slave is not greater than his master.'\f + \fr 15:20 \ft The same proverb as 13:16, turned the other way. There it meant that no disciple may consider himself above washing feet. Here it means no disciple may expect exemption from what was done to the master.\f* If they persecuted me, they will persecute you also; if they kept my word, they will keep yours also.
\v 21 But all these things they will do to you because of my name, because they do not know the one who sent me \add [i.e. the whole of it comes down to that: they do not know the Father]\add*.
\v 22 If I had not come and spoken to them, they would not have sin; but now they have no excuse for their sin\f + \fr 15:22 \ft Not that there was no sin before he came, but that this particular sin did not exist and could not: the refusal of a revelation that had not yet been given. Light arriving is what makes turning from it culpable (3:19-20; 9:41).\f*.
\v 23 The one who hates me hates my Father also.
\v 24 If I had not done among them the works that no other did, they would not have sin; but now they have both seen and hated both me and my Father \add [i.e. the seeing is not in doubt; that is what makes the hating what it is]\add*.
\v 25 But this is so that the word written in their Torah may be filled full: 'They hated me without cause.'\f + \fr 15:25 \ft Psalm 69:4 (or Psalm 35:19). Psalm 69 is the psalm of the righteous sufferer, and this book quotes it three times across its length: the zeal that eats him up at the temple (2:17), the hatred without cause here, and the vinegar at the cross (19:28-29). "Their Torah" is not distancing; it is arguing on the ground they claim, as at 8:17 and 10:34. "Without cause" means exactly that — there is nothing to point to.\f*
\s1 The Advocate Will Testify
\p
\v 26 "When the Advocate comes, whom I will send you from the Father — the Spirit of truth who goes out from the Father — that one will testify about me\f + \fr 15:26 \ft The Advocate's work here is a witness's work. Set against a world that hates and persecutes, the community's answer is testimony, and there are two witnesses to it — which is what the Torah requires for a matter to stand (Deuteronomy 19:15). The Spirit is one of them.\f*.
\v 27 And you also testify, because you are with me from the beginning\f + \fr 15:27 \ft And they are the other. Their qualification is that they were there — the same claim the book makes for itself at 19:35 and 21:24.\f*.

\c 16
\s1 They Will Put You Out
\p
\v 1 "These things I have spoken to you so that you may not stumble\f + \fr 16:1 \ft The word is \tl skandalizō\tl*, to be tripped up, caught in a snare. The same verb was used of the disciples who found the bread teaching intolerable and walked away (6:61, 66). Foreknowledge is the point: told in advance, persecution becomes confirmation instead of refutation.\f*.
\v 2 They will put you out of the synagogues; but an hour is coming when everyone who kills you will think he is bringing an offering to God\f + \fr 16:2 \ft Not hypocrisy but conviction. The words used are the words of temple service: they will regard the killing as an act of worship. There was a paradigm for it — Phinehas, who killed for God's honor and was given a covenant of peace for it (Numbers 25:6-13) — and "zeal" of that kind was a recognized category in the first century. A man who would later write letters describes his own share in it in exactly these terms.\f*.
\v 3 And they will do these things because they did not know the Father or me.
\v 4 But these things I have spoken to you so that when their hour comes you may remember them, that I told you. And these things I did not tell you from the beginning, because I was with you \add [i.e. while I was here, my being here was the shelter]\add*.
\s1 It Is Better for You That I Go
\p
\v 5 "But now I am going to the one who sent me, and none of you asks me, 'Where are you going?'\f + \fr 16:5 \ft Peter asked exactly that at 13:36 and Thomas something like it at 14:5. The complaint is not that the question was never put but that nobody is putting it now: grief has closed the conversation down. They are absorbed in what they are losing and have stopped asking where he is going or what it is for.\f*
\v 6 But because I have spoken these things to you, grief has filled your heart.
\v 7 But I tell you the truth: it is better for you that I go away. For if I do not go away, the Advocate will not come to you; but if I go, I will send him to you\f + \fr 16:7 \ft The two presences do not overlap; one gives way to the other. That is why the departure counts as gain rather than loss. He is trading a body that can be in one place for a presence that will be inside every one of them, and cannot be arrested.\f*.
\v 8 And when that one comes, he will convict\f + \fr 16:8 \ft Greek \tl elenchō\tl*, a courtroom word: to expose, to prove wrong, to bring the evidence out. The Advocate's work toward the world is not persuasion but exposure. The same verb at 3:20 for deeds shown up by the light, and at 8:46, "which of you convicts me of sin?"\f* the world about sin and about righteousness and about judgment:
\v 9 about sin, because they do not trust in me;
\v 10 about righteousness, because I am going to the Father and you no longer watch me \add [i.e. the world condemned me as a criminal; the Father receiving me overturns that verdict]\add*;
\v 11 about judgment, because the ruler of this world has been judged \add [i.e. the world thinks it is passing sentence, and sentence is being passed on the power behind it]\add*.
\s1 The Spirit of Truth Will Guide You
\p
\v 12 "I still have many things to say to you, but you cannot bear them now \add [i.e. not withheld out of secrecy but out of mercy; the weight would break them tonight]\add*.
\v 13 But when that one comes, the Spirit of truth, he will guide you in all the truth; for he will not speak from himself, but whatever he hears he will speak, and he will announce to you the things coming\f + \fr 16:13 \ft The Spirit stands in the same relation to the Son that the Son stands in to the Father: he does not originate his message, he passes on what he receives (compare 14:10, 24; 12:49). Nothing new is coming in. What is coming is the whole of what has been given, made usable.\f*.
\v 14 That one will glorify me, because he will take from what is mine and announce it to you.
\v 15 Everything the Father has is mine. For this reason I said that he takes from what is mine and will announce it to you.
\v 16 A little while and you no longer watch me, and again a little while and you will see me\f + \fr 16:16 \ft The words "because I go to the Father" at the end of this verse are not in the critical text; they appear only in verse 17, where the disciples quote him.\f*."
\s1 Your Grief Will Turn into Joy
\p
\v 17 So some of his disciples said to one another, "What is this that he says to us: 'A little while and you do not watch me, and again a little while and you will see me,' and, 'because I am going to the Father'?"
\v 18 So they were saying, "What is this that he says, 'a little while'? We do not know what he is talking about."
\v 19 Jesus knew that they wanted to ask him, and he said to them, "Are you searching among one another about this, that I said, 'A little while and you do not watch me, and again a little while and you will see me'?
\v 20 Amen, amen, I say to you, you will weep and wail, but the world will rejoice. You will be grieved, but your grief will become joy \add [i.e. not replaced by joy — turned into it]\add*.
\v 21 A woman when she gives birth has grief, because her hour has come; but when the child is born, she no longer remembers the pressure, because of the joy that a man has been born into the world\f + \fr 16:21 \ft The image is out of the prophets, and it always means suffering that produces something. Israel confesses that her labor brought forth only wind and she could not save herself — and then the answer comes: "your dead shall live... hide yourselves for a little while until the fury has passed" (Isaiah 26:17-20), with the same phrase "a little while." Zion goes into labor and her children are born, and "you shall see, and your heart shall rejoice" (Isaiah 66:7-14). The daughter of Zion writhes like a woman in labor when her king is gone, and on the far side of it she is redeemed (Micah 4:9-10). Her hour is his hour.\f*.
\v 22 So you also now have grief; but I will see you again, and your heart will rejoice, and no one takes your joy away from you \add [i.e. note who does the seeing: he finds them, not the other way about]\add*.
\v 23 And in that day you will ask me nothing\f + \fr 16:23 \ft Two different verbs are both "ask" in English. This first one is asking a question; the second, "whatever you ask the Father," is asking for something. Greek keeps them apart and English cannot.\f*. Amen, amen, I say to you, whatever you ask the Father in my name\f + \fr 16:23 \ft "In my name" can attach to the asking or to the giving. It is attached to the asking here; the other reading is "he will give it to you in my name."\f*, he will give you.
\v 24 Until now you have asked nothing in my name. Ask and you will receive, so that your joy may be made full \add [i.e. up to tonight you have asked me directly, because I was standing here]\add*.
\s1 Plainly, About the Father
\p
\v 25 "These things I have spoken to you in figures\f + \fr 16:25 \ft The word is used earlier of the shepherd saying that his hearers could not decode (10:6). A figure is not a deception; it is speech that has to be unpacked. The hour when it will be unpacked is the hour of the Spirit, who guides into all the truth.\f*. An hour is coming when I will no longer speak to you in figures, but will announce to you plainly about the Father.
\v 26 In that day you will ask in my name; and I do not say to you that I will ask the Father about you,
\v 27 for the Father himself loves you, because you have loved me and have trusted that I came out from God\f + \fr 16:27 \ft Not that he will stop interceding — the next chapter is an intercession. It corrects a picture: the Father is not the reluctant party who has to be talked round. He loves them himself, and directly.\f*.
\v 28 I came out from the Father and have come into the world; again I leave the world and go to the Father\f + \fr 16:28 \ft The whole book in one sentence: from the Father, into the world, out of the world, to the Father.\f*."
\p
\v 29 His disciples say, "Look, now you speak plainly and say no figure.
\v 30 Now we know that you know all things and have no need for anyone to question you. By this we trust that you came out from God\f + \fr 16:30 \ft Their grounds are slender: he knew what they were about to ask before they asked it (verse 19). It is real belief resting on a sign, and it is about to be tested by something signs cannot carry them through.\f*."
\v 31 Jesus answered them, "Do you now trust?
\v 32 Look, an hour is coming, and has come, when you will be scattered each to his own, and you will leave me alone\f + \fr 16:32 \ft "Strike the shepherd, and the sheep will be scattered" (Zechariah 13:7). The scattering is not merely predicted; it "has come" — it is already under way, since the betrayer left the room.\f*. And I am not alone, because the Father is with me \add [i.e. their faithfulness will fail and his standing does not depend on it]\add*.
\v 33 These things I have spoken to you so that in me you may have peace. In the world you have pressure; but take courage — I have conquered the world\f + \fr 16:33 \ft The tense is past and settled: it has been done. Said on the night of the arrest, hours before the trial, by a man who is about to be flogged and killed. The pressure is real and present ("you have"), and so is the victory, and the second is the older of the two.\f*."

\c 17
\s1 Father, the Hour Has Come
\p
\v 1 These things Jesus spoke, and lifting up his eyes to heaven\f + \fr 17:1 \ft The posture of prayer (Psalm 123:1). He did the same at Lazarus's tomb, and there too the prayer was spoken aloud for the sake of the people standing around (11:41-42). Everything he has just said to the disciples he now says over their heads to the Father, in their hearing.\f* he said, "Father, the hour has come. Glorify your Son, so that the Son may glorify you,
\v 2 just as you gave him authority over all flesh\f + \fr 17:2 \ft "All flesh" is the Hebrew way of saying every human being (Isaiah 40:5; Joel 2:28). The authority is Daniel's: "to him was given dominion and glory and a kingdom, that all peoples, nations, and languages should serve him" (Daniel 7:14).\f*, so that to everything you have given him he may give life of the age.
\v 3 And this is life of the age: that they know you, the only true God, and the one you sent, Jesus Christ\f + \fr 17:3 \ft The only definition of "life of the age" in the book, and it is not a quantity of time but an activity: knowing. The verb is present and continuous. In the Greek Old Testament "knowing" is not information but acquaintance — the new covenant promise was "they shall all know me, from the least of them to the greatest" (Jeremiah 31:34).\f*.
\v 4 I glorified you on the earth, having finished the work that you have given me to do \add [i.e. spoken before the cross, and spoken as already done]\add*.
\v 5 And now glorify me, Father, alongside yourself, with the glory I had alongside you before the world was \add [i.e. not a promotion but a restoration — asking for back what the prologue said he had]\add*.
\s1 The Men You Gave Me
\p
\v 6 "I made your name visible\f + \fr 17:6 \ft The "name" in Israel's scriptures is not a label but the whole disclosure of who God is — what he told Moses at the bush and what he proclaimed as he passed by on the mountain: "the LORD, a God merciful and gracious, slow to anger, abounding in steadfast love and faithfulness" (Exodus 34:6). Making the name visible is the work summarized in 1:18.\f* to the men you gave me out of the world. They were yours, and you gave them to me, and they have kept your word.
\v 7 Now they have known that everything you have given me is from you,
\v 8 because the words you gave me I have given them, and they received them and truly knew that I came out from you, and they trusted that you sent me \add [i.e. said of the same men who will scatter before morning; the trust is real and it is not yet strong]\add*.
\p
\v 9 "I ask about them. I do not ask about the world, but about those you have given me, because they are yours\f + \fr 17:9 \ft Not a withdrawal of concern for the world — God loved it and sent his Son into it (3:16-17). This particular prayer has a particular object. The world is reached later in the prayer, and it is reached through them: "so that the world may trust" (verse 21), "so that the world may know" (verse 23).\f*;
\v 10 and all mine are yours, and yours are mine, and I have been glorified in them.
\v 11 And I am no longer in the world, and they are in the world, and I am coming to you \add [i.e. that is the whole problem this prayer is about]\add*. Holy Father, keep them in your name, which you have given me\f + \fr 17:11 \ft The critical text reads as rendered: the "name" is what was given him. Most English versions read the relative pronoun as referring to the men — "those whom you have given me." The same wording again in verse 12. And note the reversal: all evening they have been told to "keep" his commandments; now he asks the Father to "keep" them.\f*, so that they may be one just as we are.
\v 12 While I was with them, I was keeping them in your name, which you have given me, and I guarded them, and not one of them was destroyed except the son of destruction\f + \fr 17:12 \ft "Son of destruction" is a Hebrew idiom naming a man by what characterizes him — the one who belongs to ruin. It is Judas. The Greek construction can be read two ways, either as a true exception to the keeping or as a contrast with it, and this book has consistently placed Judas inside the group and not of it: chosen, yet "one of you is a devil" (6:70); at the table, yet "not all of you are clean" (13:10-11).\f*, so that the Scripture might be filled full.
\v 13 But now I am coming to you, and I speak these things in the world so that they may have my joy made full in themselves \add [i.e. the prayer is prayed out loud so that they hear themselves commended to the Father]\add*.
\v 14 I have given them your word, and the world hated them, because they are not of the world, just as I am not of the world.
\v 15 I do not ask that you take them out of the world, but that you keep them from the evil one\f + \fr 17:15 \ft The Greek can be read "from evil" or "from the evil one"; the personal sense is taken, since this book has a person in view — the ruler of this world (12:31; 14:30; 16:11), who has already got inside one man at this table.\f*.
\v 16 They are not of the world, just as I am not of the world.
\v 17 Make them holy in the truth; your word is truth\f + \fr 17:17 \ft "Make holy" is \tl hagiazō\tl*, to consecrate — the word used of setting apart priests and vessels for service (Exodus 28:41; 29:44), and of Jeremiah set apart before he was born (Jeremiah 1:5). It is not withdrawal from the world; the very next verse sends them into it. It is being set apart "for" something.\f*.
\v 18 Just as you sent me into the world, I also sent them into the world.
\v 19 And for their sake I make myself holy, so that they also may be made holy in truth\f + \fr 17:19 \ft The same verb, now used of himself. What a man does when he consecrates himself is set himself apart for an offering. He is doing it the night before.\f*.
\s1 For Those Who Will Trust Through Their Word
\p
\v 20 "And I do not ask about these only, but also about those trusting in me through their word \add [i.e. everyone who will ever come to trust him by hearing about him — which includes whoever is reading this]\add*,
\v 21 so that they may all be one, just as you, Father, are in me and I in you, that they also may be in us, so that the world may trust that you sent me\f + \fr 17:21 \ft The oneness asked for is not an arrangement but a likeness: the Father and the Son are the measure of it. And it has a purpose outside itself. The world cannot see the Father, and cannot now see the Son; what it can see is a community whose life together looks like that relationship. That is what is meant to persuade it.\f*.
\v 22 And the glory you have given me I have given them, so that they may be one just as we are one —
\v 23 I in them and you in me, so that they may be brought to completion into one, so that the world may know that you sent me and loved them just as you loved me\f + \fr 17:23 \ft The last clause is the largest claim in the prayer. Not that the Father loves them in some lesser way, but with the love he has for the Son — the same love, on the same terms.\f*.
\p
\v 24 "Father, what you have given me\f + \fr 17:24 \ft The Greek is a neuter singular, "what you have given me," not "those whom." It is rendered as the Greek has it. The same collective way of speaking as at 6:37 and 6:39.\f* — I want that where I am, they also may be with me, so that they may watch my glory which you have given me, because you loved me before the founding of the world \add [i.e. the answer to 14:3, asked for out loud]\add*.
\v 25 Righteous Father, the world did not know you, but I knew you, and these knew that you sent me \add [i.e. three parties, and what each of them knows: the world nothing, the Son the Father himself, and these men the fact of the sending]\add*.
\v 26 And I made your name known to them and will make it known, so that the love with which you loved me may be in them, and I in them\f + \fr 17:26 \ft The last words of the whole farewell, and they answer the crisis it opened with. "Yet a little while I am with you" (13:33) troubled them. The reply is not that he will come back to being "with" them but that he will be "in" them, and the Father's love for him with him.\f*."

\c 18
\s1 The Arrest in the Garden
\p
\v 1 Having said these things, Jesus went out with his disciples across the Kidron ravine\f + \fr 18:1 \ft The valley between the city and the Mount of Olives. David crossed it barefoot and weeping, driven out of Jerusalem by his own son and betrayed by his trusted counsellor Ahithophel (2 Samuel 15:23, 31) — the counsellor who went on to hang himself. The anointed king leaves the city by the same ditch.\f*, where there was a garden, into which he and his disciples entered\f + \fr 18:1 \ft The word for garden is used twice in this book: here, and of the place where he is buried (19:41). The passion is framed by two gardens, and in the second one a woman will mistake him for the gardener.\f*.
\v 2 And Judas, the one handing him over, also knew the place, because Jesus had often gathered there with his disciples \add [i.e. the betrayal runs on knowledge that only years of company could give]\add*.
\v 3 So Judas, taking the cohort\f + \fr 18:3 \ft A technical Roman term — a cohort at full strength was some six hundred men; a detachment is meant. Roman soldiers and temple police together: the whole apparatus of the occupying power and the religious establishment, in one party.\f* and officers from the chief priests and from the Pharisees, comes there with torches and lamps and weapons\f + \fr 18:3 \ft They bring manufactured light into the dark to arrest the light of the world, and weapons to take a man who will not resist.\f*.
\v 4 So Jesus, knowing everything coming upon him, went out and says to them, "Whom are you looking for?"\f + \fr 18:4 \ft He is not discovered. He comes out to meet them, and it is he who puts the questions.\f*
\v 5 They answered him, "Jesus the Nazorean." He says to them, "I am\f + \fr 18:5 \ft Bare, with no predicate — \tl egō eimi\tl*, as at 8:24, 8:28, and 8:58. Most English versions add "he." It is left bare here, and again in verses 6 and 8. On the surface it identifies him. The next verse says what it did.\f*." And Judas, the one handing him over, was standing with them.
\v 6 So when he said to them, "I am," they went backward and fell to the ground\f + \fr 18:6 \ft An armed cohort put on the ground by two words. Nothing is explained; the effect is simply recorded. It settles the question the rest of the chapter might otherwise raise: whatever is done to him from here is done because he permits it. "No one takes it away from me" (10:18).\f*.
\v 7 So again he asked them, "Whom are you looking for?" And they said, "Jesus the Nazorean."
\v 8 Jesus answered, "I told you that I am. So if you are looking for me, let these go" —
\v 9 so that the word he said might be filled full: "Of those you have given me I lost not one\f + \fr 18:9 \ft The writer treats Jesus's own words as needing fulfilment exactly as he treats Scripture. The word being fulfilled is the prayer of 17:12, spoken minutes ago. And the fulfilment is physical: the shepherd standing between the flock and the wolves, negotiating their release.\f*."
\v 10 So Simon Peter, having a sword, drew it and struck the high priest's slave and cut off his right ear. And the slave's name was Malchus \add [i.e. named, which is the kind of thing a man who was there remembers]\add*.
\v 11 So Jesus said to Peter, "Put the sword into the sheath. The cup the Father has given me — shall I not drink it?\f + \fr 18:11 \ft In the prophets the cup is what God hands to those who must drink his judgment: "in the hand of the LORD there is a cup... all the wicked of the earth shall drain it" (Psalm 75:8; Isaiah 51:17; Jeremiah 25:15). Peter is fighting against something the Father has poured out. That is why the sword has to go away.\f*"
\s1 Taken to Annas
\p
\v 12 So the cohort and the commander and the officers of the Jews seized Jesus and bound him\f + \fr 18:12 \ft "Bound" is the word used when Abraham "bound Isaac his son and laid him on the altar" (Genesis 22:9) — the passage every hearer of "the only son, whom you love" already had in mind (see 3:16).\f*,
\v 13 and they led him to Annas first; for he was father-in-law of Caiaphas, who was high priest that year\f + \fr 18:13 \ft Annas held the office from about AD 6 to 15, and after him five of his sons and his son-in-law held it. He had been deposed by Rome and had lost nothing that mattered; the writer goes on calling him "the high priest." The prisoner is taken to the man who really decides.\f*.
\v 14 Now Caiaphas was the one who had counseled the Jews that it was better for one man to die for the people \add [i.e. the verdict was reached before the hearing; this is not an inquiry]\add*.
\s1 Peter at the Door
\p
\v 15 And Simon Peter was following Jesus, and another disciple. Now that disciple was known to the high priest, and he went in with Jesus into the courtyard of the high priest;
\v 16 but Peter was standing at the door outside. So the other disciple, the one known to the high priest, went out and spoke to the doorkeeper and brought Peter in.
\v 17 So the servant girl, the doorkeeper, says to Peter, "You are not also of this man's disciples, are you?\f + \fr 18:17 \ft The question is framed to invite the answer no, and it comes from a slave girl at a gate. Jesus said "I am" to an armed cohort and they fell down. Peter says "I am not" to a doorkeeper.\f*" That one says, "I am not."
\v 18 And the slaves and the officers were standing there, having made a charcoal fire\f + \fr 18:18 \ft An uncommon word, used twice in the whole New Testament — here, and on a beach in Galilee where Peter is put back together (21:9). The writer chose it deliberately. Peter denies at one charcoal fire and is restored at another.\f* because it was cold, and they were warming themselves. And Peter was also standing with them and warming himself \add [i.e. standing with the men who arrested him, keeping warm]\add*.
\s1 Questioned by the High Priest
\p
\v 19 So the high priest questioned Jesus about his disciples and about his teaching \add [i.e. how many follow him, and what has he been telling them — the questions of a man looking for a charge]\add*.
\v 20 Jesus answered him, "I have spoken openly to the world. I always taught in a synagogue and in the temple, where all the Jews come together, and I said nothing in secret\f + \fr 18:20 \ft "I did not speak in secret, in a land of darkness" is God's own claim through Isaiah, set against the muttered oracles of the idols (Isaiah 45:19). There is no hidden teaching to extract, because everything was said in the open.\f*.
\v 21 Why do you question me? Question those who have heard what I spoke to them. Look, these know what I said\f + \fr 18:21 \ft He is insisting on procedure. A charge is established by witnesses, not by interrogating the accused into supplying it (Deuteronomy 19:15). The demand exposes what kind of hearing this is.\f*."
\v 22 And when he said these things, one of the officers standing by gave Jesus a slap, saying, "Is that how you answer the high priest?"
\v 23 Jesus answered him, "If I spoke wrongly, testify about the wrong; but if well, why do you beat me?"\f + \fr 18:23 \ft He asks for evidence, which is the one thing this hearing will not produce.\f*
\v 24 So Annas sent him bound to Caiaphas the high priest.
\p
\v 25 And Simon Peter was standing and warming himself. So they said to him, "You are not also of his disciples, are you?" That one denied it and said, "I am not."
\v 26 One of the slaves of the high priest, a relative of the man whose ear Peter cut off, says, "Did I not see you in the garden with him?"\f + \fr 18:26 \ft No longer a bystander's guess. This man was there, and it was his relative that Peter wounded.\f*
\v 27 So again Peter denied it, and immediately a cock crowed\f + \fr 18:27 \ft Exactly as said at 13:38. The writer records no weeping and offers no comment; he lets the crowing land. Peter's story is left open here and is not taken up again until chapter 21.\f*.
\s1 Before Pilate
\p
\v 28 So they lead Jesus from Caiaphas to the praetorium\f + \fr 18:28 \ft The Roman governor's headquarters in the city. The transfer is necessary because the case now needs a power the council does not have.\f*. And it was early. And they themselves did not enter the praetorium, so that they would not be defiled but could eat the Passover\f + \fr 18:28 \ft Entering a Gentile's house would make a man unclean for the meal that evening. So they stand outside in the street, keeping the rules, while they hand over an innocent man to be killed. The note also fixes the chronology: the Passover has not been eaten yet.\f*.
\v 29 So Pilate came outside to them and says, "What accusation do you bring against this man?"
\v 30 They answered and said to him, "If this one were not doing evil, we would not have handed him over to you \add [i.e. no charge, only their own authority: take our word for it]\add*."
\v 31 So Pilate said to them, "You take him and judge him according to your own Torah." The Jews said to him, "It is not permitted for us to kill anyone\f + \fr 18:31 \ft The right to execute belonged to the governor alone. What they want is not a verdict but a death, and for that they need Rome.\f*" —
\v 32 so that the word of Jesus might be filled full which he said, signifying by what kind of death he was about to die\f + \fr 18:32 \ft Jewish execution was by stoning, which is what the crowd twice reached for (8:59; 10:31). Being "lifted up" (3:14; 8:28; 12:32) requires a Roman cross. The political fact that they must come to Pilate is what makes the manner of death the manner he foretold.\f*.
\s1 My Kingdom Is Not of This World
\p
\v 33 So Pilate went into the praetorium again and called Jesus and said to him, "Are you the king of the Jews?"\f + \fr 18:33 \ft The weight is on the pronoun: you — a bound and beaten prisoner — are the king of the Jews?\f*
\v 34 Jesus answered, "Do you say this from yourself, or did others tell you about me?"\f + \fr 18:34 \ft "King of the Jews" means one thing in a Roman court, where it is treason, and another in a Jewish dispute, where it is a claim about God's promise. He asks which conversation this is.\f*
\v 35 Pilate answered, "I am not a Jew, am I? Your own nation and the chief priests handed you over to me. What did you do?"
\v 36 Jesus answered, "My kingdom is not of this world. If my kingdom were of this world, my officers would be fighting so that I would not be handed over to the Jews\f + \fr 18:36 \ft The proof offered is negative and visible: no one is fighting. He is not denying that he has a kingdom or that it operates in the world. He is denying that it comes from the world or runs on the world's power. Peter reached for a sword an hour ago and was told to put it away.\f*. But as it is, my kingdom is not from here."
\v 37 So Pilate said to him, "So you are a king?" Jesus answered, "You say that I am a king\f + \fr 18:37 \ft The Greek can be agreement — "you are right, I am" — or deflection: "so you say." It is taken here as agreement, since what follows goes on to define the kingship rather than disown it.\f*. For this I have been born and for this I have come into the world: to testify to the truth. Everyone who is of the truth hears my voice\f + \fr 18:37 \ft The shepherd discourse walks into the governor's chamber. "The sheep hear his voice... my sheep hear my voice and follow me" (10:3, 27); "he who is of God hears the words of God" (8:47). The kingdom is wherever that voice is heard and obeyed, and the offer is made to a Roman: "everyone" who is of the truth.\f*."
\v 38 Pilate says to him, "What is truth?\f + \fr 18:38 \ft He has just been told how to recognize it — by hearing the voice — and he asks what it is, and does not stay for an answer. The reader knows what Pilate does not: that it is standing in front of him in chains (14:6).\f*" And having said this he went out again to the Jews and says to them, "I find no cause in him.
\v 39 But there is a custom for you that I release one man to you at the Passover. So do you want me to release to you the king of the Jews?"
\v 40 So they shouted again, saying, "Not this one, but Barabbas!" Now Barabbas was a bandit\f + \fr 18:40 \ft The word is \tl lēstēs\tl* — the same word Jesus used for the men who do not come in by the door: "a thief and a robber" (10:1, 8). In the first century it commonly meant an armed insurgent. The name \tl Bar-Abbas\tl* means "son of the father." Given the choice between the shepherd and the robber, between the Son of the Father and a man whose name merely says it, they take the robber.\f*.

\c 19
\s1 The Crown of Thorns
\p
\v 1 So then Pilate took Jesus and flogged him\f + \fr 19:1 \ft The Roman \tl flagellum\tl* was a whip of leather thongs weighted with bone or metal. Pilate's calculation appears in verse 5: show the crowd a man broken far enough that they will not need him dead. "By his wounds we are healed" (Isaiah 53:5).\f*.
\v 2 And the soldiers, twisting together a crown out of thorns, put it on his head, and they threw a purple garment around him\f + \fr 19:2 \ft A crown and a purple robe are the regalia of a king; purple dye was among the costliest goods in the empire and was the emperor's color. The soldiers mean it as a joke. What they have actually staged is an investiture.\f*,
\v 3 and they were coming to him and saying, "Hail, king of the Jews!"\f + \fr 19:3 \ft The greeting is the one shouted at Caesar. They put the emperor's acclamation on a flogged Galilean, and every word of it is true.\f* — and they were giving him slaps.
\v 4 And Pilate went outside again and says to them, "Look, I am bringing him out to you, so that you may know that I find no cause in him \add [i.e. the second of three times he says it]\add*."
\v 5 So Jesus came outside, wearing the thorny crown and the purple garment. And he says to them, "Look — the man!\f + \fr 19:5 \ft Pilate means it dismissively: here is your dangerous rebel, look at the state of him. But the reader has been told since the first page who this man is — "the Word became flesh" — and the governor of Judea is presenting him to the world he made.\f*"
\v 6 So when the chief priests and the officers saw him, they shouted, saying, "Crucify! Crucify!" Pilate says to them, "You take him and crucify him; for I find no cause in him\f + \fr 19:6 \ft Three times Pilate declares him innocent (18:38; 19:4, 6) — a Roman judge with the power of life and death, on the record. Whatever else the execution is, it is not a judge deceived. And the demand comes specifically from the chief priests and their police, not from the pilgrims.\f*."
\v 7 The Jews answered him, "We have a Torah, and according to the Torah he owes it to die, because he made himself Son of God\f + \fr 19:7 \ft The real charge, finally spoken. Before Pilate the accusation has been political; here it is what it always was — blasphemy, punishable by death (Leviticus 24:16). The phrase "made himself" has run through the whole book on their lips: making himself equal with God (5:18), making himself God (10:33). They have understood the claim correctly. They have judged it false.\f*."
\s1 Where Are You From?
\p
\v 8 So when Pilate heard this word, he was more afraid,
\v 9 and he went into the praetorium again and says to Jesus, "Where are you from?\f + \fr 19:9 \ft The question of the whole book, asked at last by a Roman. "We know where this man is from" (7:27); "we do not know where he is from" (9:29). Pilate is not asking about Galilee.\f*" But Jesus gave him no answer\f + \fr 19:9 \ft "He was oppressed, and he was afflicted, yet he opened not his mouth; like a lamb that is led to the slaughter" (Isaiah 53:7). He has already told Pilate the truth about himself, and Pilate said "What is truth?" and walked out. There is nothing to add.\f*.
\v 10 So Pilate says to him, "You do not speak to me? Do you not know that I have authority to release you and I have authority to crucify you?"
\v 11 Jesus answered him, "You would have no authority against me at all unless it had been given you from above\f + \fr 19:11 \ft "From above" is \tl anōthen\tl*, the word from the conversation with Nicodemus (3:3, 7, 31). Pilate's power is real and it is on loan. The judge is being told, in his own courtroom, whose court it is.\f*. For this reason the one who handed me over to you has the greater sin\f + \fr 19:11 \ft Guilt is measured by what was known and refused. Pilate is a pagan administrator out of his depth; the men who handed him over hold the Scriptures, have watched the signs, and know exactly what they are doing (compare 9:41; 15:22).\f*."
\v 12 From this Pilate was seeking to release him; but the Jews shouted, saying, "If you release this one you are no friend of Caesar\f + \fr 19:12 \ft "Friend of Caesar" was a formal standing in the imperial system, and losing it could cost a governor his post and more. Having begun with their own law, they now reach for Rome's politics. It is the one argument Pilate cannot survive.\f*. Everyone who makes himself a king speaks against Caesar."
\v 13 So Pilate, hearing these words, brought Jesus outside and sat down\f + \fr 19:13 \ft The Greek can be read either as Pilate sitting down on the judgment seat or as Pilate seating Jesus on it. The first is taken here.\f* on a judgment seat at a place called the Stone Pavement, and in Hebrew Gabbatha.
\v 14 Now it was the preparation of the Passover; it was about the sixth hour\f + \fr 19:14 \ft The day the Passover lambs were killed, and the hour at which the killing began in the temple. The book that opened with "Look — the Lamb of God" (1:29) has been driving at this line since its first chapter.\f*. And he says to the Jews, "Look — your king!"
\v 15 So those shouted, "Away, away! Crucify him!"\f + \fr 19:15 \ft "Away with him" is the verb John used of "the Lamb of God who lifts away the sin of the world" (1:29). The crowd is calling for the lamb to be taken away, and it is.\f* Pilate says to them, "Shall I crucify your king?" The chief priests answered, "We have no king but Caesar\f + \fr 19:15 \ft The most complete self-condemnation in the book. Israel's confession was that God alone is king — "the LORD is our king; he will save us" (Isaiah 33:22) — and when the nation first asked for a human king God told Samuel, "they have rejected me from being king over them" (1 Samuel 8:7). To be rid of this one, the chief priests say the words out loud.\f*."
\v 16 So then he handed him over to them to be crucified. So they took Jesus,
\s1 The Place of a Skull
\p
\v 17 and carrying the cross himself\f + \fr 19:17 \ft This account has no Simon of Cyrene. He carries it. "No one takes it away from me, but I lay it down of myself" (10:18).\f* he went out to the place called Place of a Skull, which in Hebrew is called Golgotha,
\v 18 where they crucified him\f + \fr 19:18 \ft Two words, and no description at all. Everyone reading knew what crucifixion was; the writer does not linger over the mechanics of it.\f*, and with him two others, one on each side, and Jesus in the middle \add [i.e. "and he was numbered with the transgressors" (Isaiah 53:12)]\add*.
\v 19 And Pilate also wrote a notice and put it on the cross. And it was written: "Jesus the Nazorean, the king of the Jews."
\v 20 So many of the Jews read this notice, because the place where Jesus was crucified was near the city; and it was written in Hebrew, in Latin, in Greek\f + \fr 19:20 \ft The three languages of that world: the language of the covenant people, the language of the empire, and the language everyone did business in. The Roman placard naming the crime is a proclamation of the kingship, published to everybody. "I, if I am lifted up out of the earth, will draw all to myself" (12:32).\f*.
\v 21 So the chief priests of the Jews were saying to Pilate, "Do not write 'The king of the Jews,' but that that one said, 'I am king of the Jews.'"\f + \fr 19:21 \ft They want it written as his claim rather than as a fact.\f*
\v 22 Pilate answered, "What I have written, I have written \add [i.e. the man who would not hold his ground on the verdict holds it on the wording]\add*."
\p
\v 23 So the soldiers, when they crucified Jesus, took his garments and made four parts, a part for each soldier, and the tunic. Now the tunic was seamless, woven from the top throughout\f + \fr 19:23 \ft The word for "seamless" occurs nowhere else in the New Testament. Josephus describes the high priest's tunic in the same way: not two pieces stitched at the shoulders but one woven length with an opening for the head (\tl Antiquities\tl* 3.161). And "from the top" is again \tl anōthen\tl*, from above.\f*.
\v 24 So they said to one another, "Let us not tear it, but let us cast lots for it, whose it will be" — so that the Scripture might be filled full which says, "They divided my garments among themselves, and for my clothing they cast a lot."\f + \fr 19:24 \ft Psalm 22:18. The psalm of the righteous sufferer runs under this whole scene: mocked by everyone who sees him, surrounded, his hands and feet pierced, his tongue sticking to his jaws, his clothes gambled for. It also ends where this book ends — "I will tell of your name to my brothers," and "all the ends of the earth shall remember and turn to the LORD."\f* So the soldiers did these things.
\s1 Woman, Look — Your Son
\p
\v 25 And standing by the cross of Jesus were his mother, and his mother's sister, Mary the wife of Clopas, and Mary Magdalene \add [i.e. the women stayed; of the men, only one]\add*.
\v 26 So Jesus, seeing his mother and the disciple whom he loved standing by, says to his mother, "Woman, look — your son."
\v 27 Then he says to the disciple, "Look — your mother." And from that hour the disciple took her into his own home\f + \fr 19:26-27 \ft It is provision for a widow, and it is more. He calls her "woman," as he did at Cana when the hour had not yet come (2:4); now it has. From the cross he makes two people who were not family into mother and son. The prologue said that those who received him were given the right to become children of God, born "not out of bloods, nor out of the will of flesh" (1:12-13). Here that new household is constituted, by his word, while he is dying.\f*.
\s1 It Has Been Finished
\p
\v 28 After this, Jesus, knowing that everything had already been finished, says — so that the Scripture might be brought to completion — "I thirst\f + \fr 19:28 \ft "For my thirst they gave me vinegar to drink" (Psalm 69:21) — the third time this book draws on that psalm (2:17; 15:25). The thirst is real; crucifixion produced it. The writer notes that it also completes what was written.\f*."
\v 29 A jar full of sour wine was standing there. So putting a sponge full of the sour wine on hyssop\f + \fr 19:29 \ft Hyssop is the plant of the Passover: "take a bunch of hyssop and dip it in the blood in the basin, and touch the lintel and the two doorposts" (Exodus 12:22). It is also the plant of cleansing — from contact with the dead (Numbers 19:18) and in David's prayer, "purge me with hyssop and I shall be clean" (Psalm 51:7). The stalk that carried the lamb's blood to the doorposts is held up to the mouth of the Lamb.\f*, they brought it to his mouth.
\v 30 So when Jesus took the sour wine he said, "It has been finished\f + \fr 19:30 \ft One word in Greek, \tl tetelestai\tl*, in a tense that means done and staying done. It is the same root as "to finish his work" at 4:34 and "having finished the work" at 17:4. Not "it is over" but "it has been completed."\f*." And bowing his head he handed over the spirit\f + \fr 19:30 \ft Greek \tl paredōken to pneuma\tl*. The verb is the one used all through the arrest and trial for handing him over — Judas, the priests, Pilate. At the end he is the one who hands something over, and it is his own life. And \tl pneuma\tl* is both his breath and the Spirit; this book has said the Spirit could not be given until he was glorified (7:39). The double sense cannot be kept in English; it is rendered lower case.\f*.
\s1 Not a Bone Broken
\p
\v 31 So the Jews, since it was preparation day, so that the bodies would not remain on the cross on the Sabbath — for that Sabbath day was a great one\f + \fr 19:31 \ft The law required a hanged body to be taken down before nightfall so as not to defile the land (Deuteronomy 21:22-23), and this Sabbath was the Passover Sabbath. Their scruple runs the whole of this chapter: they will not enter a Gentile's house, and they will not leave a body hanging, and they are doing this.\f* — asked Pilate that their legs be broken and they be taken away\f + \fr 19:31 \ft Breaking the legs killed a crucified man quickly: he could no longer push up to breathe.\f*.
\v 32 So the soldiers came and broke the legs of the first and of the other who was crucified with him;
\v 33 but coming to Jesus, when they saw that he was already dead, they did not break his legs.
\v 34 But one of the soldiers pierced his side with a spear, and immediately blood and water came out\f + \fr 19:34 \ft Blood and water, together, out of the body of the one who called his blood true drink (6:55) and promised rivers of living water — which the writer said meant the Spirit, not yet given because he was not yet glorified (7:38-39). He is glorified now.\f*.
\v 35 And the one who has seen has testified, and his testimony is true, and that one knows that he speaks true things, so that you also may trust\f + \fr 19:35 \ft The writer breaks off the narrative to swear to this. It is the most emphatic claim of eyewitness in the book, and it is attached to the pierced side. The same voice speaks again at 21:24, where the witness is identified.\f*.
\v 36 For these things happened so that the Scripture might be filled full: "Not a bone of his will be broken."\f + \fr 19:36 \ft Two texts stand behind it and both are meant. The Passover lamb: "you shall not break any of its bones" (Exodus 12:46; Numbers 9:12). And the righteous sufferer: "he keeps all his bones; not one of them is broken" (Psalm 34:20). He is the lamb, and he is the righteous man God does not abandon.\f*
\v 37 And again another Scripture says, "They will look at the one they pierced."\f + \fr 19:37 \ft Zechariah 12:10, and every part of it fits. God says, "I will pour out a spirit of grace and supplication, and they will look on me whom they have pierced, and they shall mourn for him as one mourns for an only son." A pouring out of the Spirit; a piercing; mourning for an "only son"; and a strange slide from "me" to "him" that leaves the pierced one and God somehow the same.\f*
\s1 The Burial
\p
\v 38 And after these things Joseph from Arimathea, being a disciple of Jesus but hidden for fear of the Jews\f + \fr 19:38 \ft One of the secret believers this book has described — men who believed and would not say so, "for they loved the glory of men more than the glory of God" (12:42-43). The death is what brings him out. Asking Pilate for the body of a crucified man is not something that can be done quietly.\f*, asked Pilate that he might take away the body of Jesus; and Pilate gave permission. So he came and took away his body.
\v 39 And Nicodemus also came, the one who came to him by night at first\f + \fr 19:39 \ft His third appearance, and the writer identifies him each time by the night visit. He came in the dark (3:2); he made a careful procedural objection and was jeered at (7:50-51); now he comes in daylight with a hundred pounds of spices for a dead man's body.\f*, bringing a mixture of myrrh and aloes, about a hundred pounds\f + \fr 19:39 \ft An enormous quantity — the scale of a royal funeral; Josephus says five hundred servants carried spices at Herod's. The soldiers crowned him in mockery, Pilate proclaimed him in writing, and Nicodemus buries him as a king.\f*.
\v 40 So they took the body of Jesus and bound it in linen cloths with the spices, as is the custom of the Jews to bury \add [i.e. described in detail here because of what will be found lying in that tomb]\add*.
\v 41 Now in the place where he was crucified there was a garden, and in the garden a new tomb in which no one had yet been laid.
\v 42 So there, because of the preparation day of the Jews, since the tomb was near, they laid Jesus \add [i.e. the Sabbath was minutes away, and the tomb was the one within reach]\add*.

\c 20
\s1 The Stone Taken Away
\p
\v 1 And on the first day of the week Mary Magdalene comes early, while it was still dark\f + \fr 20:1 \ft The clock reading, and more. Every use of darkness in this book has been loaded, and the light has just been put out and buried. She comes in the dark because as far as anyone knows that is where things now stand.\f*, to the tomb, and she sees the stone taken away from the tomb.
\v 2 So she runs and comes to Simon Peter and to the other disciple whom Jesus loved and says to them, "They have taken the Lord out of the tomb, and we do not know where they have laid him \add [i.e. she reads an empty tomb as a robbery, because that is the only thing it can mean]\add*."
\v 3 So Peter went out, and the other disciple, and they were coming to the tomb.
\v 4 And the two were running together; and the other disciple ran ahead faster than Peter and came to the tomb first.
\v 5 And bending down he sees the linen cloths lying there; however he did not go in.
\v 6 So Simon Peter also comes, following him, and he went into the tomb. And he watches the linen cloths lying there,
\v 7 and the cloth that had been on his head — not lying with the linen cloths, but rolled up in a place by itself\f + \fr 20:7 \ft The detail is the argument. Grave robbers take the wrappings with the body, spices and all, or leave a mess. These are lying where the body was, and the face-cloth is folded and set apart. Compare Lazarus, who came out of his tomb still tied up and had to be unwrapped by other people (11:44). Here the cloths stay and the body is gone.\f*.
\v 8 So then the other disciple who had come to the tomb first went in also, and he saw and trusted\f + \fr 20:8 \ft The first resurrection faith in the book, and it happens without seeing Jesus at all — on the evidence of an empty tomb and a folded cloth. Hold this beside verse 29: "blessed are those who have not seen and have trusted."\f*;
\v 9 for they did not yet know the Scripture, that he must rise up from among the dead \add [i.e. he believed before he understood, not because of understanding]\add*.
\v 10 So the disciples went away again to themselves.
\s1 Mary in the Garden
\p
\v 11 But Mary stood outside at the tomb weeping. So as she wept she bent down into the tomb,
\v 12 and she watches two angels in white sitting, one at the head and one at the feet, where the body of Jesus had been lying\f + \fr 20:12 \ft One at each end of the place where the body lay, facing in. That is the arrangement of the two cherubim on the cover of the ark, at either end of the place where God said, "There I will meet with you" (Exodus 25:18-22). The empty slab is framed as the place of meeting.\f*.
\v 13 And those say to her, "Woman, why are you weeping?" She says to them, "They have taken away my Lord, and I do not know where they have laid him."
\v 14 Having said these things she turned around and watches Jesus standing there, and she did not know that it was Jesus \add [i.e. she is looking for a corpse, and cannot see a living man when he is in front of her]\add*.
\v 15 Jesus says to her, "Woman, why are you weeping? Whom are you looking for?\f + \fr 20:15 \ft The question he asked his first followers on the first page — "What are you looking for?" (1:38) — and the question he asked the soldiers in the garden (18:4, 7). It is the book's question, and it is asked for the last time here.\f*" That one, thinking he was the gardener\f + \fr 20:15 \ft The tomb is in a garden, so a gardener is a reasonable guess. It is also the third garden in three chapters: arrested in one, buried in one, and here mistaken for the man who works the ground — which is what the first man was made to do in the first garden (Genesis 2:15).\f*, says to him, "Sir, if you carried him off, tell me where you laid him, and I will take him away."
\v 16 Jesus says to her, "Mary." Turning, that one says to him in Hebrew, "Rabbouni" (which is to say, Teacher)\f + \fr 20:16 \ft Her eyes failed. One word — her own name — and she knows. "He calls his own sheep by name... the sheep follow him because they know his voice" (10:3-4).\f*.
\v 17 Jesus says to her, "Do not hold on to me\f + \fr 20:17 \ft Greek \tl mē mou haptou\tl*, "stop holding on to me" — she is already gripping him. It is not a ban on touching; a week later Thomas is invited to put his hand into the wound. It is a redirection: the old way of having him near is ending, and something else is beginning.\f*, for I have not yet gone up to the Father. But go to my brothers\f + \fr 20:17 \ft The first time in this book he calls them that. They have been disciples, then his own, then friends (15:15); now, after the resurrection, brothers. "I will tell of your name to my brothers" (Psalm 22:22) — the far end of the psalm that has been running under the crucifixion.\f* and tell them, 'I am going up to my Father and your Father, and my God and your God\f + \fr 20:17 \ft Not "our Father." The distinction is kept: his sonship and theirs are not the same thing. But what is his is now also theirs, and that is the point of the sentence.\f*.'"
\v 18 Mary Magdalene comes announcing to the disciples, "I have seen the Lord," and that he said these things to her \add [i.e. the first person to carry the news is the one who came to embalm a body]\add*.
\s1 Peace to You — Receive the Holy Spirit
\p
\v 19 So when it was evening on that day, the first of the week, and the doors had been shut where the disciples were for fear of the Jews, Jesus came and stood in the middle and says to them, "Peace to you\f + \fr 20:19 \ft The ordinary greeting, \tl shalom\tl*, and the specific thing he promised them on the last night: "Peace I leave with you; my peace I give to you" (14:27); "so that in me you may have peace" (16:33). He says it into a locked room full of frightened men.\f*."
\v 20 And having said this he showed them his hands and his side. So the disciples rejoiced when they saw the Lord\f + \fr 20:20 \ft "Your grief will become joy... I will see you again, and your heart will rejoice, and no one takes your joy away from you" (16:20-22). The risen body is the crucified body; the wounds are the identification. Glory does not erase them.\f*.
\v 21 So Jesus said to them again, "Peace to you. Just as the Father has sent me, I also send you \add [i.e. the prayer of 17:18 now spoken to their faces as a commission]\add*."
\v 22 And having said this he breathed on them and says to them, "Receive the Holy Spirit\f + \fr 20:22 \ft The verb is rare, and the Greek Old Testament uses it in exactly two places that matter. God "breathed into his face the breath of life, and the man became a living being" (Genesis 2:7). And in the valley of dry bones: "prophesy to the breath... and say, Come from the four winds, O breath, and breathe upon these slain, that they may live" (Ezekiel 37:9), where the bones are Israel saying "our hope is lost." He breathes on a company of men shut in a room in fear, and it is both of those at once: a new making, and a dead people stood up on its feet.\f*.
\v 23 If you send away the sins of any, they have been sent away from them; if you hold any fast, they have been held fast\f + \fr 20:23 \ft The second verb in each pair is a perfect: they have been sent away, they have been held fast — done, and standing done. Most English versions have a simple present, "they are forgiven." The "you" throughout is plural: this is given to the gathered company, not to an individual, and it is given in the same breath as the Spirit and the sending. What the Lamb accomplished (1:29), they are commissioned to announce.\f*."
\s1 Thomas
\p
\v 24 But Thomas, one of the Twelve, the one called the Twin, was not with them when Jesus came.
\v 25 So the other disciples were saying to him, "We have seen the Lord." But he said to them, "Unless I see in his hands the mark of the nails, and put my finger into the place of the nails, and put my hand into his side, I will never trust \add [i.e. he wants the wounds, not just an appearance — proof that the risen one is the crucified one]\add*."
\v 26 And after eight days his disciples were again inside, and Thomas with them. Jesus comes, the doors having been shut, and he stood in the middle and said, "Peace to you."
\v 27 Then he says to Thomas, "Bring your finger here and see my hands, and bring your hand and put it into my side; and do not become untrusting, but trusting\f + \fr 20:27 \ft He offers exactly what Thomas demanded, item by item — and Thomas said it when he was not in the room.\f*."
\v 28 Thomas answered and said to him, "My Lord and my God\f + \fr 20:28 \ft The book comes full circle. It opened with the writer telling the reader, "the Word was God" (1:1); it closes with a man saying it to his face. \tl Kyrios\tl* is the word that stands for the divine name all through the Greek Old Testament, and \tl theos\tl* is God. And both carry "my": this is not a doctrine being stated but a man handing himself over.\f*."
\v 29 Jesus says to him, "Because you have seen me, you have trusted?\f + \fr 20:29 \ft The Greek can be a question or a statement; it is taken here as a question.\f* Blessed are those who have not seen and have trusted\f + \fr 20:29 \ft Which is everyone who will read this book. The beloved disciple believed at the empty tomb without seeing him (verse 8); those who come later will believe through the word of men who did (17:20). The blessing is pronounced over them.\f*."
\s1 Why This Book Was Written
\p
\v 30 So Jesus did many other signs before the disciples, which are not written in this book \add [i.e. what you have read was selected, and here is the standard by which it was selected]\add*.
\v 31 But these have been written so that you may trust\f + \fr 20:31 \ft The critical text prints the verb with its tense-marker in brackets, so the Greek itself does not settle whether the purpose is that readers come to trust or that they go on trusting. "May trust" is used here because it stays open, as the Greek does. The book works either way: an invitation to those outside, and a strengthening for those already in.\f* that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God, and so that trusting you may have life in his name.

\c 21
\s1 Nothing All Night
\p
\v 1 After these things Jesus made himself visible again to the disciples at the Sea of Tiberias. And he made himself visible this way.
\v 2 Simon Peter and Thomas called the Twin and Nathanael from Cana of Galilee and the sons of Zebedee and two others of his disciples were together \add [i.e. seven of them, back in the country where the first of them were called]\add*.
\v 3 Simon Peter says to them, "I am going fishing." They say to him, "We are coming with you too." They went out and got into the boat, and in that night they caught nothing\f + \fr 21:3 \ft No commission has yet been given to Peter personally, and nothing has been said about his denial. He goes back to what he knows how to do. A night's work, and the nets come up empty. "Apart from me you can do nothing" (15:5).\f*.
\v 4 And when it was already becoming morning, Jesus stood on the shore; the disciples did not know, however, that it was Jesus.
\v 5 So Jesus says to them, "Children, you do not have any fish, do you?" They answered him, "No."
\v 6 And he said to them, "Throw the net on the right side of the boat, and you will find some." So they threw it, and they were no longer strong enough to drag it in from the multitude of the fish \add [i.e. the same men, the same boat, the same water, and one word]\add*.
\v 7 So that disciple whom Jesus loved says to Peter, "It is the Lord\f + \fr 21:7 \ft The pattern of these two, kept up to the end. The beloved disciple sees first — at the tomb, and here. Peter moves first — into the tomb, and here into the water.\f*." So Simon Peter, hearing that it is the Lord, tied his outer garment around him — for he was naked — and threw himself into the sea \add [i.e. he dresses to go and then jumps, which is Peter entire]\add*.
\v 8 But the other disciples came in the little boat, for they were not far from the land, only about two hundred cubits away, dragging the net of fish.
\s1 Breakfast on the Shore
\p
\v 9 So when they got out onto the land they see a charcoal fire lying there\f + \fr 21:9 \ft The uncommon word from the high priest's courtyard, where Peter stood warming himself and said three times that he was not one of them (18:18). It is used in only these two places. The fire is already lit when they arrive; the scene of restoration was laid out before they got there.\f*, and fish lying on it, and bread.
\v 10 Jesus says to them, "Bring some of the fish you just caught \add [i.e. he has food already, and he asks for theirs as well]\add*."
\v 11 So Simon Peter went up and dragged the net onto the land, full of large fish, a hundred and fifty-three of them; and though there were so many, the net was not torn\f + \fr 21:11 \ft Somebody counted them, which is the sort of thing that gets remembered. Interpretations of the number are ancient and none is certain. What the sentence itself insists on is the two facts: an enormous catch, and a net that held. "Let us not tear it" was said of the tunic at the cross (19:24), and "that they may all be one" was prayed the night before (17:21).\f*.
\v 12 Jesus says to them, "Come, eat breakfast." But none of the disciples dared to ask him, "Who are you?" — knowing that it was the Lord \add [i.e. they wanted to ask, and they knew, and both are true]\add*.
\v 13 Jesus comes and takes the bread and gives it to them, and the fish likewise\f + \fr 21:13 \ft The same two verbs as at the feeding of the five thousand on this same lake: he took the loaves and gave them out (6:11). He is still the one who provides the meal.\f*.
\v 14 This was already the third time Jesus was made visible to the disciples after being raised from among the dead.
\s1 Simon, Do You Love Me?
\p
\v 15 So when they had eaten breakfast, Jesus says to Simon Peter, "Simon son of John\f + \fr 21:15 \ft His birth name, the one Jesus used the first time they met before giving him another (1:42). The conversation starts from the ground up.\f*, do you love me more than these?\f + \fr 21:15 \ft "More than these" can mean more than these men love me, or more than you love these things — the boats, the nets, the trade he has just gone back to. The Greek does not decide, and it is left undecided here. Peter's own boast was that he would stand when everyone else fell (13:37).\f*" He says to him, "Yes, Lord, you know that I love you\f + \fr 21:15-17 \ft Three pairs of words alternate across this exchange and all three flatten in English. Two verbs for "love"; two verbs for feeding and shepherding, kept here; and two nouns, lambs and sheep, also kept. The two love-verbs are rendered alike because this writer uses them interchangeably elsewhere — both are used of the Father loving the Son (3:35; 5:20), and both of Jesus loving Lazarus (11:3, 5). What carries the weight is not the shade of the word but the number: three questions for three denials, at a charcoal fire.\f*." He says to him, "Feed my lambs\f + \fr 21:15 \ft "My" lambs. The flock is not handed over to him; the care of it is. Behind this stands Ezekiel 34, where God condemns the shepherds who fed themselves, promises to shepherd the flock himself, and promises to set one shepherd over them. The man being given the crook is the one who three months ago could not admit to a servant girl that he knew the shepherd.\f*."
\v 16 He says to him again, a second time, "Simon son of John, do you love me?" He says to him, "Yes, Lord, you know that I love you." He says to him, "Shepherd my sheep."
\v 17 He says to him the third time, "Simon son of John, do you love me?" Peter was grieved that he said to him the third time, "Do you love me?" And he said to him, "Lord, you know all things; you know that I love you\f + \fr 21:17 \ft He does not offer evidence, and he does not repeat his old promise. He appeals to what Jesus knows. That is the whole distance travelled from "I will lay down my life for you" (13:37).\f*." Jesus says to him, "Feed my sheep.
\v 18 Amen, amen, I say to you, when you were younger you tied your own belt and walked where you wanted; but when you grow old, you will stretch out your hands, and another will tie your belt and carry you where you do not want\f + \fr 21:18 \ft Every clause is a reversal: girded by another instead of himself, taken instead of going, and hands stretched out — the posture of a man on a crossbeam. The boast that failed at the fire will be made good in the end, and not by his own strength.\f*."
\v 19 Now he said this signifying by what kind of death he would glorify God\f + \fr 21:19 \ft Word for word the phrase used twice of Jesus's own death (12:33; 18:32). Peter's death is described in the same terms as his master's, and it is called glorifying God.\f*. And having said this he says to him, "Follow me\f + \fr 21:19 \ft The first command, given again at the end. "Where I am going you cannot follow me now, but you will follow later" (13:36). Later has a shape now, and Peter has just been told what it is.\f*."
\s1 What About This One?
\p
\v 20 Peter, turning, watches the disciple whom Jesus loved following — the one who also leaned back on his chest at the supper and said, "Lord, who is the one handing you over?"
\v 21 So Peter, seeing this one, says to Jesus, "Lord, and what about this one?"\f + \fr 21:21 \ft If that is to be my end, what is to be his?\f*
\v 22 Jesus says to him, "If I want him to remain until I come, what is that to you? You follow me \add [i.e. mind your own calling; his is not yours to audit]\add*."
\v 23 So this word went out among the brothers, that that disciple does not die. But Jesus did not tell him that he does not die, but, "If I want him to remain until I come, what is that to you?"\f + \fr 21:23 \ft The correction is careful, and it is about accuracy: a conditional had been passed round as a promise. It suggests that by the time this was written the disciple had died, or was expected to, and that the rumour needed answering. The same insistence on getting the testimony right runs through 19:35 and the next verse.\f*
\p
\v 24 This is the disciple who testifies about these things and who wrote these things, and we know that his testimony is true\f + \fr 21:24 \ft The witness is finally identified, though still not named. Two things are said of him: he testifies, in the present — the testimony is still standing — and he wrote. And then a different voice, a "we," vouches for him. Through this whole book truth has been established by more than one witness (5:31-37; 8:17-18). The book ends by supplying its own second one.\f*.
\v 25 And there are also many other things that Jesus did, which, if they were written one by one, I suppose the world itself would not have room for the books being written\f + \fr 21:25 \ft The closing "Amen" of the majority text is not in the critical text.\f*.
